{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 3, "clears_when": "ONE decision completes the FULL round-trip and is observed doing it, end to end: an answer submitted from the deployed SPA appears on `/api/bot/work/decisions` as `in_transit`, and then a LATER read of the same route grades that same request `committed` with the `answer` block visible in `docs/claude/work/objects/<id>.yaml` on `main`. Record WHICH request and WHO answered it. ⚠️ MERGING THE TWO PRs DOES NOT CLEAR THIS — a merge is not a deploy, and a deploy is not an answer. That distinction is why `BL-20260901-DB-EXPLORER-IS-UNGATED-AND-REACHES-DEVICE-TOKENS-RAW-TOKEN-COLUMN` is still open one day after #10682 merged. ⚠️ NEITHER DOES A GREEN TEST: `tests/test_work_decisions.py` already exercises submit→commit→`committed` in a harness (28 passing, and the two load-bearing invariants are mutation-tested). A harness is not the fleet, and the four stages this row lists are precisely the ones a harness cannot reach. ⚠️ NOR DOES A `workflow_dispatch` RUN OF `work-decision-commit` BY ITSELF: it is worth doing FIRST and it clears stage (d) only — a green empty run proves the job can reach the VM and read the log, which is genuinely useful and is NOT the round-trip. Say which stages you cleared rather than clearing the row on the easiest one. ⚠️ AND IF THE OPERATOR DECIDES NOT TO SET `DASHBOARD_API_TOKEN` on the web-api, this row does NOT clear — it CHANGES: answering from the UI is then declared closed, which is a legitimate decision and must be RECORDED as one rather than left look", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-12"}, "id": "OI-20260901-DECISION-ROUNDTRIP-SHIPPED-AND-NO-DECISION-HAS-EVER-MADE-THE-ROUND-TRIP", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "audit", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/OPEN-ITEMS.json#OI-20260901-DECISION-ROUNDTRIP-SHIPPED-AND-NO-DECISION-HAS-EVER-MADE-THE-ROUND-TRIP", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/OPEN-ITEMS.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='OI-20260901-DECISION-ROUNDTRIP-SHIPPED-AND-NO-DECISION-HAS-EVER-MADE-THE-ROUND-TRIP'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[LOUD] Operating-layer PHASE H (the control half) shipped the DECISION ROUND-TRIP as a draft PR (#10705 bot, ict-trader-dashboard#211 SPA): a work object may declare answerable `decision_requests[]`, `GET /api/bot/work/decisions` is the inbox, `POST /api/bot/work/decision` submits an answer (token-gated, FAIL-CLOSED), and `scripts/ops/commit_work_decisions.py` + `.github/workflows/work-decision-commit.yml` commit it into the repo. ⚠️ NOT ONE DECISION HAS MADE THE ROUND-TRIP, and every stage of it is independently unproven on the fleet: (a) the routes are NOT DEPLOYED (draft PR); (b) `DASHBOARD_API_TOKEN` is UNSET on the live web-api as far as this session could establish, so the write route would serve 503 and `writeGate.state` reads `closed_no_token` — whether to set it is an OPERATOR decision, not a bug; (c) the SPA panel has never been RENDERED (this session cannot render the deployed page, the same limit `WO-20260901-PHASE-B` held an edge open for); (d) the committer workflow's SSH+curl step has NEVER RUN — it is proven by shape, copied from `vm-diag-snapshot.yml`, not by a run, and this repo has scheduled workflows that are merged, enabled, correct and fire LATE and ERRATICALLY rather than on their declared cadence -- CORRECTED 2026-09-02: probes.yml HAS now fired on cron (actions_list on probes.yml, event=schedule, total_count 1, run #34, conclusion success, 2026-09-01T10:12:17Z) and that row is CLEARED. The caution it carried still stands and is now better evidenced, not weaker: its cron is `20 5 * * *` and it fired at 10:12Z, roughly 4h50m LATE, and once rather than daily. So correct cron syntax still is not evidence of a run -- read the run history). ⚠️ THE RISK THIS ROW WATCHES IS NOT THAT THE CODE IS WRONG. It is that a decision channel with a question in it and no round-trip behind it is WORSE than no channel: the operator answers, nothing lands, and the question reads `in_transit` forever while looking like it was dealt with. The design's own words are that visibility is the enforcement mechanism this system has never had — a channel that swallows answers is anti-enforcement. ⚠️ THERE IS EXACTLY ONE REAL QUESTION IN IT TODAY: `DEC-20260901-READ-GATE-SEQUENCING` on `WO-20260901-PHASE-H`, and it BLOCKS the other half of Phase H (the read gate), which was deliberately not built because its own precondition — Streamlit + Android off the live feed — is unmet. Measured before it existed: of 584 objects, ZERO declared a `decision_requests` block and ZERO carried an `operator_decision` edge. `verified_at` is deliberately null: no observation has been made, and dating it today would be a claim of one."}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 7, "clears_when": "A retirement is DONE — the thing is GONE and check_sunset_dispositions.py verified its absence on a `retired` row it did not write itself. ⚠️ A MERGE DOES NOT CLEAR THIS: shipping the pass is a deploy, not an observation, the same distinction that keeps OI-20260826-SESSION-BRIEF-NEVER-READ-BY-A-FRESH-SESSION open. ⚠️ NOR DOES A PASSING SELF-TEST, and this is the specific trap here: both unreachable branches (E2's `enforcing`, E3's carry escalation) are asserted green by their own self-tests today, so a green suite proves the CODE works and says nothing about whether the MECHANISM ever fired. ⚠️ NOR DOES ANOTHER SUNSET PASS RUNNING: more candidates is more detection, and detection was never the missing half — check_unwired_artifacts has reported 115 findings and exited 0 for weeks. The observation wanted is a REMOVAL. ⚠️ AND A DISPOSITION OF `keep` ON EVERYTHING DOES NOT CLEAR IT EITHER: that is a legitimate answer per row and an illegitimate answer for the population, and it would mean the forcing function was answered into inertness — which is a more useful finding than a pass, and belongs in `observation` rather than being treated as a clear.", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-06"}, "id": "OI-20260901-SUNSET-PASS-SHIPPED-AND-NOTHING-HAS-BEEN-RETIRED", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "audit", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/OPEN-ITEMS.json#OI-20260901-SUNSET-PASS-SHIPPED-AND-NOTHING-HAS-BEEN-RETIRED", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/OPEN-ITEMS.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='OI-20260901-SUNSET-PASS-SHIPPED-AND-NOTHING-HAS-BEEN-RETIRED'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[LOUD] E3 shipped (Phase G): scripts/ops/sunset_pass.py grades retirement candidates weekly, docs/claude/SUNSET-DISPOSITIONS.json records answers, and check_sunset_dispositions.py escalates an unanswered one. Its first run named 10 candidates over 52 legs. ⚠️ NOTHING HAS BEEN RETIRED. The phase's own done-condition — 'something has actually been REMOVED' — is NOT met and was deliberately not claimed: every candidate is either Tier-3 (the operator's call) or a Tier-1 tool where the correct disposition is `wire`/`keep` rather than delete. Shipped and working are different states, and this is the row that keeps them apart."}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 1, "clears_when": "A ping is CONFIRMED to have arrived in @ict_cluade_bot AND NOT in the trader chat. !! DELIVERY IS NOT THE CRITERION AND MUST NOT BE READ AS ONE. Pings have been delivering throughout -- one enqueued 20:58:49Z on 2026-09-01 was confirmed delivered, to the TRADER chat, while the operator watched the new bot and correctly reported nothing arrived. So 'the ping went out' and 'the drain reported success' clear NOTHING here; the claim is SEPARATION. !! NOR IS THE STARTUP LOG SUFFICIENT ALONE. isolated=True proves which TOKEN the bridge resolved, which is necessary and genuinely new -- but a token is not a conversation, and in a DM the chat_id is the OPERATOR's own id (365546917) for every bot by construction, so the VM side cannot distinguish the two chats even in principle. The last hop is observable only on the operator's phone. Clearing needs BOTH: (a) the startup line reads token=TELEGRAM_CLAUDE_BOT_SECRET[dedicated] ... isolated=True, AND (b) a human confirms which conversation it appeared in. Either half alone leaves this open.", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-12"}, "id": "OI-20260901-CLAUDE-CHANNEL-SEPARATION-SHIPPED-BUT-UNPROVEN", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "audit", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/OPEN-ITEMS.json#OI-20260901-CLAUDE-CHANNEL-SEPARATION-SHIPPED-BUT-UNPROVEN", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/OPEN-ITEMS.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='OI-20260901-CLAUDE-CHANNEL-SEPARATION-SHIPPED-BUT-UNPROVEN'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[LOUD] !! CORRECTED 2026-09-12 BY session_013iSqp4LsU1eq8K326eUtgj (MI-279 U5) -- THE SET-ENV HAS HAPPENED AND THIS SUMMARY SAID IT WAS OUTSTANDING. Do not re-quote the closing sentence preserved below; a session reading it would go and ask the operator for a Tier-2 they have already granted. MEASURED on the live VM at 2026-09-12T18:16:37Z (ict-claude-bridge.service, pid 1777212, a process started TODAY): 'claude ping route -> claude: token=TELEGRAM_CLAUDE_BOT_SECRET[dedicated] chat=TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID[fallback] deliverable=True isolated=True own_chat=False'. That is clause (a) of clears_when, verbatim, and it is MET -- against the isolated=False the row was opened on. All three units are active (ict-telegram-bot, ict-claude-bridge, ict-claude-decision-bot). !! THE ROW DOES NOT CLEAR: clause (b) needs a HUMAN to say which conversation the ping appeared in, and the row's own text explains why no VM-side read can substitute -- in a DM the chat_id is the operator's own id (365546917) for every bot by construction. 'chat=TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID[fallback]' is therefore EXPECTED and is NOT a defect; do not file it as one. === THE ORIGINAL SUMMARY FOLLOWS AND IS PRESERVED AS THE RECORD OF WHAT WAS TRUE WHEN THE ROW WAS OPENED: The dedicated Claude bot (@ict_cluade_bot) is CONFIGURED and its drain is FIXED and DEPLOYED, and pings still land in the TRADER chat. #10674 is merged (5c45ca52) and live on the VM (1bae542a); the bridge now resolves via claude_route() and at 21:37:08Z logged isolated=False with its own WARNING naming the missing key. What remains is one Tier-2 set-env putting TELEGRAM_CLAUDE_BOT_SECRET in the VM .env -- which this session could NOT dispatch (issue_write create -> 403) and which needs an operator OK it does not have."}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 7, "clears_when": "A REAL ict_scalp exit-head round runs on the trainer and its build_report.json is read, showing BOTH: (a) `legs_filter_state: applied` with `legs_filter` listing exactly the round's `--legs`, and (b) a families dict containing NO family the round did not ask for -- no `donchian`/`pullback` bucket on a scalp round. A NON-ZERO LIVE COUNT IS NOT THE CRITERION and must not be treated as one: the `no_candles` stage is a separate, untouched condition, so a round can be correctly scoped and still read live=0, and reading that as this fix having failed is the exact conflation the three-state `legs_filter_state` exists to prevent. A PASSING UNIT TEST CLEARS NOTHING -- the tests already pass; that is what makes this `awaiting_verification` rather than unproven in principle.", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": null}, "id": "OI-20260902-E0-LIVE-ARM-LEG-SCOPING-SHIPPED-AND-NEVER-RUN-ON-THE-TRAINER", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "audit", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/OPEN-ITEMS.json#OI-20260902-E0-LIVE-ARM-LEG-SCOPING-SHIPPED-AND-NEVER-RUN-ON-THE-TRAINER", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/OPEN-ITEMS.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='OI-20260902-E0-LIVE-ARM-LEG-SCOPING-SHIPPED-AND-NEVER-RUN-ON-THE-TRAINER'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[NEVER VERIFIED] The M20 E0 live arm now scopes to the round's own legs (`build_exit_head_dataset.py --legs`, passed by `m20_exit_head_round.py` wherever it passes `--db`), fixing defect 1 of BL-20260813-EXIT-HEAD-LIVE-ARM-DROPPED-ON-NO-CANDLES: without it the builder loaded EVERY strategy-attributed closed trade and `family_of()` bucketed same-symbol siblings into families the round never asked for. IT HAS NEVER RUN ON THE TRAINER. The session that shipped it had no `issue_write` (403), so the `trainer-vm-diag-request` relay was unreachable and no round has exercised the flag against a real journal. Shipped and exercised are different states and only the first happened."}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 7, "clears_when": "The operator's OWN acceptance test completes, end to end, and is RECORDED: (1) a decision request reaches the operator's Telegram as a message with buttons; (2) they tap one; (3) GET /api/bot/work/decisions shows that request as `in_transit` with the transit window open; (4) a LATER read of the same route grades that same request `committed`, with the `answer` block visible in docs/claude/work/objects/<id>.yaml on `main`. [!] STAGES (1)-(3) AND STAGE (4) ARE DIFFERENT FACTS AND MUST BE REPORTED SEPARATELY -- (1)-(3) prove the Telegram half; (4) is the committer, which is OI-20260901-DECISION-ROUNDTRIP-SHIPPED-AND-NO-DECISION-HAS-EVER-MADE-THE-ROUND-TRIP's business and is NOT cleared by this row. Say which stages you actually cleared. [!] A GREEN TEST SUITE CLEARS NOTHING HERE -- the harness already passes and is precisely what cannot reach a rendered message or a real tap. [!] NEITHER DOES MERGING: a merge is not a deploy, and this needs an ict-telegram-bot.service RESTART before the handler or the sweep exists in the running process. [!] AND A QUIET CHANNEL IS NOT EVIDENCE: if no prompt arrives, read /api/diag/log_file?name=work_decision_prompted and the bot's journal before concluding anything -- the sweep HOLDS (with a WARNING naming the reason) when the API's write gate is closed or when no polled bot can carry the buttons, and a hold and an outage are opposite facts that look identical from the operator's phone.", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-06"}, "id": "OI-20260902-TELEGRAM-DECISION-ROUNDTRIP-SHIPPED-AND-NO-TAP-HAS-EVER-BEEN-MADE", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "audit", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/OPEN-ITEMS.json#OI-20260902-TELEGRAM-DECISION-ROUNDTRIP-SHIPPED-AND-NO-TAP-HAS-EVER-BEEN-MADE", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/OPEN-ITEMS.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='OI-20260902-TELEGRAM-DECISION-ROUNDTRIP-SHIPPED-AND-NO-TAP-HAS-EVER-BEEN-MADE'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[LOUD] The TELEGRAM half of the decision round-trip is built (src/runtime/telegram_decisions.py + a `wdec:*` branch in telegram_query_bot.callback_handler + a job-queue sweep): an unanswered `decision_requests[]` entry is sent to the operator as an inline-keyboard prompt, and a tap POSTs to /api/bot/work/decision over loopback. NOT ONE TAP HAS EVER BEEN MADE. Built, and exercised, are different states and only the first happened. It is also NOT DEPLOYED: the code reaches the VM via ict-git-sync, but `ict-telegram-bot.service` only picks up a new handler and a new job on a RESTART, which is Tier-2 and is the manager's to dispatch."}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 3, "clears_when": "THE SOAK THRESHOLD IS STATED SO THIS CANNOT BE CLEARED BY IMPATIENCE OR RUN FOREVER. Clears when BOTH: (a) the soak log holds at least 20 graded rows spanning at least 3 distinct UTC days on at least one Bybit account, AND at least one row where the side-aware grade DIFFERS from the side-blind sum (verdicts_differ true); and (b) the operator, shown that row count and at least one concrete disagreement, decides to arm or not to arm. [!] A COUNT OF ROWS IS NOT THE CRITERION ON ITS OWN: 20 rows in which every grade agrees means the gate is inert on this book, and arming it would be a change with no measured effect. Record that and say so rather than arming to close the row. [!] DECIDING NOT TO ARM CLEARS THIS ROW TOO, and must be RECORDED as a decision rather than left looking like a soak still running: that is the failure mode the rule exists to catch. [!] AN EMPTY SOAK DOES NOT CLEAR IT AND IS ITSELF THE FINDING: zero rows after 3 days means the code is not deployed or not reached, which is a deploy defect, not a quiet book. Zero rows and twenty-agreeing rows are opposite facts and must never both be reported as 'the soak is quiet'.", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-12"}, "id": "OI-20260902-BYBIT-COVERAGE-BASIS-MERGED-WITH-ARMING-DELIBERATELY-HELD-FOR-A-SOAK", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "audit", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/OPEN-ITEMS.json#OI-20260902-BYBIT-COVERAGE-BASIS-MERGED-WITH-ARMING-DELIBERATELY-HELD-FOR-A-SOAK", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/OPEN-ITEMS.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='OI-20260902-BYBIT-COVERAGE-BASIS-MERGED-WITH-ARMING-DELIBERATELY-HELD-FOR-A-SOAK'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[LOUD] #10746 (src/runtime/bybit_coverage_basis.py) is MERGED to main (af9af5e3) and its ARMING IS DELIBERATELY HELD, on an operator instruction given in-conversation 2026-09-02: 'hold it until the soak'. The gate grades a Bybit re-arm against the book actually being protected instead of a side-blind leg sum. It is held at the measurement stage: BYBIT_GRADED_COVERAGE_ACCOUNTS ships EMPTY, and for this knob an empty allowlist means NONE (the PROTECTION_REASSERT_ACCOUNTS polarity, deliberately opposite to CONVICTION_SIZING_ACCOUNTS). [!] THIS ROW EXISTS BECAUSE OF A STANDING OPERATOR RULE, not because the code is suspect: 'anything soaking needs to be logged with an alarm that has either a timer or a soak threshold, so that we know to get back to it when the soak is ready.' Without it the soak accrues and nobody returns, which is how a held decision becomes an abandoned one."}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 7, "clears_when": "A REAL TAP ON CLAUDEBOT PRODUCES A work_decision_transit.jsonl ROW, and it is recorded which request and who tapped. Concretely, all four: (1) ict-claude-decision-bot.service is enabled and ACTIVE on the VM (/api/diag/services), with its startup banner in `journalctl -u ict-claude-decision-bot` naming token_var=TELEGRAM_CLAUDE_BOT_SECRET and the wdec prefix; (2) /api/diag/log_file?name=telegram_poll_claude returns a FRESH heartbeat; (3) a decision prompt is observed arriving in @ict_cluade_bot and NOT in the trader chat -- the last hop is visible only on the operator's phone, the same limit that keeps OI-20260901-CLAUDE-CHANNEL-SEPARATION-SHIPPED-BUT-UNPROVEN open; (4) the operator taps a button and a row lands in /api/diag/log_file?name=work_decision_transit. !! MERGING DOES NOT CLEAR THIS -- a merge is not a deploy and a deploy is not a tap; the unit must be installed and enabled, which install_systemd_units.sh does only on the next deploy run. !! A GREEN TEST RUN DOES NOT CLEAR THIS, for the reason in `detail`. !! CLAUSE (1) OR (2) ALONE DOES NOT CLEAR IT EITHER: those prove the bot is POLLED, which is necessary and is NOT the same as a tap being RECEIVED and SUBMITTED -- that exact distinction is the subject of this change, and clearing on the cheaper half would reproduce it. !! IF THE OPERATOR DECIDES NOT TO PUT TELEGRAM_CLAUDE_BOT_SECRET ON THE VM, this row does not clear -- it CHANGES: the trader-bot fallback is then the DECLARED destination, which is a legitimate deci", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-06"}, "id": "OI-20260902-DECISION-PROMPTS-MOVED-TO-CLAUDEBOT-AND-NO-TAP-HAS-LANDED-THERE", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "audit", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/OPEN-ITEMS.json#OI-20260902-DECISION-PROMPTS-MOVED-TO-CLAUDEBOT-AND-NO-TAP-HAS-LANDED-THERE", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/OPEN-ITEMS.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='OI-20260902-DECISION-PROMPTS-MOVED-TO-CLAUDEBOT-AND-NO-TAP-HAS-LANDED-THERE'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[LOUD] Work-decision prompts are re-pointed from the TRADER bot to the dedicated Claude bot (@ict_cluade_bot), and the thing that makes that safe ships in the SAME PR: src/bot/claude_decision_bot.py + deploy/ict-claude-decision-bot.service POLL TELEGRAM_CLAUDE_BOT_SECRET and register the same `wdec` handler, so the bot is ANSWERABLE before anything is routed to it. Operator, 2026-09-02: \"that's supposed to be showing up in Cloudbot. Right? Not on the trader one, the decisions.\" !! NO TAP HAS EVER BEEN MADE ON CLAUDEBOT. The PR is a DRAFT, the new unit has never run, and whether TELEGRAM_CLAUDE_BOT_SECRET is present in the VM .env is NOT something this session could read -- that is an operator/manager action, not a bug to work around, and it must not be substituted with TELEGRAM_CLAUDE_BOT_TOKEN (which drives the PROP bot despite its name)."}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 3, "clears_when": "A run of constraint-readout with `event=schedule` is OBSERVED to have completed, AND its commit is on main -- i.e. docs/claude/CONSTRAINT.json's `generated_at` advanced without a session having typed the command. Record the run number and the timestamp. !! A workflow_dispatch RUN DOES NOT CLEAR THIS, and reading one as the other is exactly the substitution that left two Routines dead in plain sight for four months. !! NEITHER DOES THE FILE EXISTING ON MAIN -- that is a deploy, not a firing; it is the same distinction that keeps OI-20260826-SESSION-BRIEF-NEVER-READ-BY-A-FRESH-SESSION open. !! AND A RUN THAT FIRES BUT COMMITS NOTHING STILL CLEARS IT: an unchanged readout is a legitimate outcome (the work store did not move that day), so the criterion is that the CADENCE ran, not that the content changed. !! ADDED 2026-09-08 AFTER THE FIX. A workflow_dispatch run of the REPAIRED workflow proves the DEPENDENCY end to end -- that the runner completes --write and commit-to-main, which the local yaml-blocking reproduction does NOT establish -- and it still clears NOTHING here, because the failure class this row watches is a CADENCE that does not deliver. Run it if you like; record it as a dispatch, explicitly. !! `fired and failed` is now a MEASURED third state beside `never fired` and `fired and legitimately committed nothing`. Do not read an unadvanced generated_at as absence again without reading the run history -- that substitution is what made the 2026-09-06 reading wrong. !! I", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-18"}, "id": "OI-20260902-CONSTRAINT-READOUT-CRON-SHIPPED-AND-HAS-NEVER-FIRED-ON-SCHEDULE", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "audit", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/OPEN-ITEMS.json#OI-20260902-CONSTRAINT-READOUT-CRON-SHIPPED-AND-HAS-NEVER-FIRED-ON-SCHEDULE", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/OPEN-ITEMS.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='OI-20260902-CONSTRAINT-READOUT-CRON-SHIPPED-AND-HAS-NEVER-FIRED-ON-SCHEDULE'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[LOUD] [!] THE HEADLINE IN THIS ROW'S OWN ID IS NOW FALSE AND THE ID IS KEPT ONLY BECAUSE OTHER ROWS AND DOCS LINK IT BY NAME -- the same treatment OI-20260902-PR-QUEUE-WATCHER-SHIPPED-AND-ITS-CRON-HAS-NEVER-FIRED already carries. THE CRON HAS FIRED. Measured 2026-09-08 over the workflow's COMPLETE run history: 6 runs exist, ALL SIX carry event=schedule (there has never been a workflow_dispatch run), runs #1-#6 spanning 2026-09-03T11:05:18Z..2026-09-08T11:07:32Z. `never fired` is no longer the accurate reading and must not be re-derived from the id. [!] AND ALL SIX CONCLUDED `failure` -- which here is NOT the watcher working. CLAUDE.md warns that pr-queue-watch.yml exits 1 ON PURPOSE to page, so a red run THERE means the mechanism functioning; this is the opposite case and the distinction is the whole reading. Each run lasted ~14 seconds and died at the self-test gate. WHAT THE ROW WAS FOR STILL STANDS: A1's readout has never been regenerated by its own cadence, so docs/claude/READOUT.md + CONSTRAINT.json are fresh only when a session types the command -- and this workflow also re-renders the CLAUDE.md session brief, so the staleness reaches the one surface that reaches a session before it acts."}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 7, "clears_when": "A session that was NOT pointed here by its prompt cites one of these packs as WHERE it learned about the work store / cycle priority / WIP ceiling, AND says what it did differently because of it. Record which session and which pack. !! A GREEN GUARD DOES NOT CLEAR THIS -- it proves the pointer exists, which is a deploy, not a reading; the same distinction that keeps OI-20260826-SESSION-BRIEF-NEVER-READ-BY-A-FRESH-SESSION open. !! A SESSION POINTED HERE BY ITS PROMPT CANNOT CLEAR IT, for the reason the MHG row names: it has been told. !! AND 'a session read it and ignored it' does NOT clear this either -- that is a different and more useful finding, and belongs in `observation` rather than being treated as a pass.", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-12"}, "id": "OI-20260902-ROLE-PACKS-NOW-REACH-THE-OPERATING-LAYER-AND-NO-SESSION-HAS-BEEN-OBSERVED-USING-IT", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "audit", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/OPEN-ITEMS.json#OI-20260902-ROLE-PACKS-NOW-REACH-THE-OPERATING-LAYER-AND-NO-SESSION-HAS-BEEN-OBSERVED-USING-IT", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/OPEN-ITEMS.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='OI-20260902-ROLE-PACKS-NOW-REACH-THE-OPERATING-LAYER-AND-NO-SESSION-HAS-BEEN-OBSERVED-USING-IT'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "The operating model's anti-silo mechanism is `context = work object + role pack`, and its two halves were wired to different systems: the object half shipped 2026-09-01 and not one role pack knew it existed. Six situating packs (session-coordination, session-handoff, doc-freshness, duty, research-driver, delegate-work) now name the work store, the cycle priority, the constraint readout and the enforced WIP ceiling -- and scripts/ci/check_role_pack_operating_layer.py KEEPS them pointing there, because a one-time prose edit decays silently on the next rewrite. !! NO SESSION HAS BEEN OBSERVED ACTING ON ANY OF IT."}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 7, "clears_when": "A session works at least one `sunset` row to a recorded disposition in docs/claude/SUNSET-DISPOSITIONS.json, and the row is observed to DROP OFF the next render of DUE.md. Record which candidate and which disposition. !! A DISPOSITION IS NOT A RETIREMENT and this row must not be read as asking for one: retiring a strategy leg is Tier-3, `retire_proposed` is the furthest a session may take one alone, and 'keep' closes a row exactly as legitimately as 'retire_proposed'. The gap being closed is that NOBODY LOOKED. !! THE SOURCE RENDERING ROWS DOES NOT CLEAR THIS -- that is a deploy. The observable is a disposition being WRITTEN. !! AND IF ALL NINE ARE DISPOSITIONED THE ROW CLEARS EVEN IF NOTHING IS RETIRED; a fleet where every candidate was examined and kept is the success case, not a failure to act.", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-06"}, "id": "OI-20260902-SUNSET-CANDIDATES-ARE-NOW-DUE-AND-NINE-ARE-UNDISPOSITIONED", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "audit", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/OPEN-ITEMS.json#OI-20260902-SUNSET-CANDIDATES-ARE-NOW-DUE-AND-NINE-ARE-UNDISPOSITIONED", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/OPEN-ITEMS.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='OI-20260902-SUNSET-CANDIDATES-ARE-NOW-DUE-AND-NINE-ARE-UNDISPOSITIONED'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "E3's retirement candidates now REACH a session. scripts/ops/render_due_list.py gained a `sunset` source: every `retire_candidate` in the newest comms/sunset/<date>/INDEX.json with no row in docs/claude/SUNSET-DISPOSITIONS.json. Measured on the 2026-09-01 pass: 10 candidates, 1 dispositioned (strategy:turtle_soup), so NINE now appear on DUE.md. Before this they appeared nowhere a session was required to look -- E3's machinery all shipped and ZERO of the 32 role packs referenced any of it, so candidates accumulated on a cadence with nobody made to look."}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 3, "clears_when": "EITHER (a) a run of replay-pregate-nightly completes all 22 heads and its report lands under runtime_logs/replay_pregate/ ON MAIN -- both halves, because the commit-back has never once worked and a green run that commits nothing would leave the header's claim still false; OR (b) the operator decides the trainer's memory is not going to be increased and the fleet run is re-scoped to fit it (fewer heads per run, or batched across runs), and that decision is RECORDED as a decision rather than left looking like a repair that is about to happen. [!] A PARTIAL RUN DOES NOT CLEAR THIS however many heads it salvages -- salvage is the failure being survived, not the failure being fixed. [!] NEITHER DOES THE TRANSPORT REPAIR, which is already proven and is a DIFFERENT fact: #4390 held the connection for 30 minutes with zero transport failures and the workflow was still red. [!] AND DO NOT CLEAR THIS ON A QUIET ALARM. The workflow is in claude-run-failure-alert.yml's watch list, so it pages nightly; the pages going quiet would mean it stopped RUNNING, not that it started passing. Read the run history.", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-12"}, "id": "OI-20260902-REPLAY-PREGATE-CANNOT-FINISH-BECAUSE-THE-TRAINER-IS-OUT-OF-MEMORY", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "audit", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/OPEN-ITEMS.json#OI-20260902-REPLAY-PREGATE-CANNOT-FINISH-BECAUSE-THE-TRAINER-IS-OUT-OF-MEMORY", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/OPEN-ITEMS.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='OI-20260902-REPLAY-PREGATE-CANNOT-FINISH-BECAUSE-THE-TRAINER-IS-OUT-OF-MEMORY'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[LOUD] replay-pregate-nightly's nightly red is NOT a network fault and NOT an OOM kill -- the trainer VM is out of memory and swap-thrashing, and the fleet run cannot finish. MEASURED on run #4390 (workflow_dispatch, 2026-09-02, the first run with the detach+poll repair): `free -m` on the trainer read total 5909 / used 5508 / free 70 / available 204 MB, with 4095 MB of 8191 MB swap consumed. Both OOM greps -- `dmesg -T` and `journalctl --since -2h` -- returned EMPTY, so nothing was killed; the driver is alive and crawling. It scored 10 of 22 heads in 30 minutes and was still going."}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 7, "clears_when": "THREE PARTS, and they are different facts. (1) The reaper's SCHEDULE is OBSERVED firing -- a `session-reaper` run with event=schedule in actions_list, AND a REAPER-OBSERVATIONS.json whose `generated_at` advanced because of it. !! MERGING IS NOT THIS. This repo has scheduled workflows that are merged, enabled, correct and fire LATE AND ERRATICALLY: probes.yml's first-ever scheduled run came ~4h50m late against `20 5 * * *`, once rather than daily. Correct cron syntax is not evidence of a run -- read the run history. (2) A session OTHER than MI-70 reads the ledger and ACTS on a row in it. A producer with no consumer is the exact shape provenance-consumer-guard exists to catch, and is how the `Claude-Session:` trailer sat unread in the first place. Record WHICH session and WHICH row. (3) The operator or the manager rules on whether Phase E's done-condition is AMENDED to the demonstrated truth, or whether the unpushed tiers are to be closed some other way. !! DO NOT CLOSE PHASE E ON THIS DEMONSTRATION ALONE: it shows a PARTIAL loss, and closing an object on evidence that contradicts its own done-condition is worse than leaving it open. !! AND A CLEAN REAPER REPORT DOES NOT CLEAR THIS: the reaper is blind to unpushed work by construction and cannot tell alive from dead, so its silence is never evidence that nothing was lost.", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": null}, "id": "OI-20260902-REAPER-SHIPPED-AND-THE-KILL-PROVED-A-PARTIAL-LOSS-NOT-A-CLEAN-ONE", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "audit", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/OPEN-ITEMS.json#OI-20260902-REAPER-SHIPPED-AND-THE-KILL-PROVED-A-PARTIAL-LOSS-NOT-A-CLEAN-ONE", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/OPEN-ITEMS.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='OI-20260902-REAPER-SHIPPED-AND-THE-KILL-PROVED-A-PARTIAL-LOSS-NOT-A-CLEAN-ONE'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[LOUD] [NEVER VERIFIED] MI-70 built the missing Phase E reaper (scripts/ops/session_reaper.py + .github/workflows/session-reaper.yml) and RAN THE KILL. !! THE HEADLINE IS THAT WO-20260901-PHASE-E'S DONE-CONDITION AS WRITTEN IS FALSE, and the measurement says so rather than being shaped to pass. A purpose-made expendable subject (session_01NN97cVYW5dmiNNXHfsu7Nn) was archived at 2026-09-02T13:49:13Z while SESSION_STATUS_RUNNING, holding three tiers of state. SURVIVED: the pushed tier, entirely -- branch claude/mi70-kill-subject @ 4d9c0c48, content intact. LOST: the committed-but-unpushed commit (its own final status_detail confirms it existed -- 'local phase 2 commit held') and the uncommitted file. So the demonstrated statement is 'killing a session loses everything it had not pushed, and nothing it had', NOT 'loses nothing'. Neither loss is recoverable by any reaper, ever; it lived in a released container. !! THE RECOVERY DID NOT COME FROM SESSIONS.json -- the subject was deliberately never registered, so the registry contributed nothing. The reaper located it by the `Claude-Session:` commit trailer 62s after the kill. What it could NOT recover is what the work was FOR: `owns_object` exists only in the registry."}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 2, "clears_when": "ONE committed answer is OBSERVED reaching the session that asked it, end to end: (1) a decision request carries an `asked_by` block naming a real session; (2) it is answered and the answer COMMITS to main; (3) the drain Routine fires and records `pushed` for it; and (4) THE ASKING SESSION IS OBSERVED WAKING -- idle/disconnected before and running/connected after via get_session, doing something ATTRIBUTABLE to the pushed answer. Record which request and which session. [!] STAGE (4) IS THE ONE THAT MATTERS. `pushed` means the drain reported firing, which is a claim about the CALL, not an observation of a wake. [!] A GREEN PROBE CLEARS NOTHING. check_drain_liveness.py grading `fresh` says the drain RAN; it says nothing about whether a delivery succeeded or whether the woken session acted. That is stated in the probe's own is_not for exactly this reason. [!] `never_ran` IS NOT A FAILURE OF THIS ROW TODAY -- it is the accurate reading until the Routine is created, and the row should not be re-filed as broken on the strength of it. [!] AND IF THE OPERATOR DECIDES NOT TO CREATE THE DRAIN ROUTINE, THIS ROW DOES NOT CLEAR -- IT CHANGES: push-back is then carried by mechanism A alone (a manager poking while alive), which is a legitimate decision and must be RECORDED as one, with the consequence stated -- there is then no floor, and a committed answer whose asker is idle when no manager is alive is never delivered.", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-12"}, "id": "OI-20260902-DECISION-DRAIN-ROUTINE-DOES-NOT-EXIST-AND-NOTHING-HAS-EVER-DRAINED", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "audit", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/OPEN-ITEMS.json#OI-20260902-DECISION-DRAIN-ROUTINE-DOES-NOT-EXIST-AND-NOTHING-HAS-EVER-DRAINED", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/OPEN-ITEMS.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='OI-20260902-DECISION-DRAIN-ROUTINE-DOES-NOT-EXIST-AND-NOTHING-HAS-EVER-DRAINED'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[LOUD] The decision push-back's REPO half is built and its DELIVERY half has never run once. asked_by records which session asked (measured before: of 584 objects, ZERO requests named their asker, so every committed answer had NO ADDRESS); push_decisions_back.py exposes the queue with the message pre-rendered, writes the repo-sourced idempotence marker, and records a per-run receipt; check_drain_liveness.py grades that receipt. [!] THE DRAIN ROUTINE DOES NOT EXIST. [!] THIS ROW SAID 'Nothing in the repo can create it -- a Routine is made from the web UI or /schedule in an interactive CLI session'. THAT PREMISE IS FALSE AND WAS DISPROVEN 2026-09-05T10:39Z, incidentally, while building the manager wake: `create_trigger` through the Claude Code Remote MCP SUCCEEDS from an ordinary session (trig_018AkCBVYcWGCXujNQVrZ92h, cron `39 * * * *`, create_new_session_on_fire=true, verified by firing it). What IS refused is the `connectors` parameter, org-wide and verbatim. So a session CAN create this drain Routine; what it cannot do is give the fired session tools. [!] CREATING IT TODAY WOULD THEREFORE BE HARMFUL, not progress: a fired session gets no mcp__* tools, so it could not poke anything, and it would ALSO block on a permission prompt with no human to answer -- both measured on the wake fire, which landed nothing at all. A drain that exists and delivers nothing is precisely the looks-armed-is-not failure this row already warns about, one level up. [!] THE CORRECTION MATTERS BECAUSE THE REASON CHANGED, NOT THE VERDICT: the blocker is not 'no session can create a Routine', it is 'a Routine-fired session has no tools and cannot answer a prompt'. A session reading the old sentence would conclude the wrong thing about what is possible and would not look at the real obstacle. See BL-20260905-THE-MANAGER-WAKE-ROUTINE-IS-CREATABLE-BUT-CAN-NEITHER-POKE-NOR-LAND-A-RECEIPT -- so the probe grades `never_ran` today and that is CORRECT, not a bug. [!] THE OPERATOR RULED OUT THE CREDENTIAL PATH (2026-09-02): no minted tokens, ever. The claude -p --cloud steps were DELETED rather than left behind an unset secret, because a step wired to a credential nobody will mint is the looks-armed-is-not failure this row exists to watch for."}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 7, "clears_when": "A session that was NOT pointed at this change by its prompt needs an endpoint's shape, payload contract or field caveat, REACHES docs/reference/bot-api-reference.md, and says so — naming the route it looked up and where it saw the pointer. Record WHICH session. ⚠️ THE FILE EXISTING IS NOT EVIDENCE, and neither is CI passing: that is a deploy, not an observation — the same distinction that keeps OI-20260826-SESSION-BRIEF-NEVER-READ-BY-A-FRESH-SESSION open. ⚠️ A SESSION POINTED HERE BY ITS PROMPT CANNOT CLEAR THIS; it has been told. ⚠️ AND THE INFORMATIVE FAILURE MUST NOT BE SCORED AS A PASS: if a session needed one of these rows, did NOT find it, and reasoned from stale memory or re-derived it from the code instead, that is the defect this row watches — write it into `observation` and leave the row OPEN rather than treating 'a session coped' as success. That finding is more valuable than a clean clear, and it is the signal to move content back.", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-06"}, "id": "OI-20260902-API-REFERENCE-SPLIT-OUT-OF-CLAUDE-MD-AND-NOT-YET-FOUND-AT-USE", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "audit", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/OPEN-ITEMS.json#OI-20260902-API-REFERENCE-SPLIT-OUT-OF-CLAUDE-MD-AND-NOT-YET-FOUND-AT-USE", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/OPEN-ITEMS.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='OI-20260902-API-REFERENCE-SPLIT-OUT-OF-CLAUDE-MD-AND-NOT-YET-FOUND-AT-USE'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[LOUD] The per-endpoint API reference (156,448 B: the /api/bot/* route table, BotStats, Position, CORS, and the /api/diag/* table) was moved VERBATIM out of CLAUDE.md into docs/reference/bot-api-reference.md, taking CLAUDE.md 430,453 -> 283,388 B (-34.2%). CLAUDE.md is the ONLY surface that reaches a session before it acts, so this deliberately removed that content from the pre-act channel. ⚠️ NOTHING HAS BEEN OBSERVED FINDING IT IN ITS NEW HOME. The whole risk of this change is the one the dispatch named: moving reference material into a file nobody opens is not a win if that material was preventing mistakes. Shipped and working are different states and only the first happened. verified_at is deliberately null: no observation has been made, and dating it today would be a claim of one."}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 2, "clears_when": "TWO PARTS, and they are different facts — say which you cleared rather than clearing the row on the easier one. (1) A SCHEDULED run of pr-queue-watch.yml is OBSERVED completing and its receipt docs/claude/work/PR-QUEUE-WATCH.json appears ON MAIN carrying a `generated_at` from that run — read the run history and that stamp, NEVER the cron expression. A workflow_dispatch run clears the WORKFLOW half only and must be recorded as such, because the failure class being watched is a cron that never fires. (2) check_pr_queue_watch.py is observed grading `fresh` on a PR OTHER than the one that introduced it, which is what proves the guard armed itself rather than merely being registered. ⚠️ MERGING THIS CLEARS NOTHING — a merge is not a deploy and a deploy is not a run; that distinction is why OI-20260826-SESSION-BRIEF-NEVER-READ-BY-A-FRESH-SESSION is still open. ⚠️ A GREEN SELF-TEST CLEARS NOTHING EITHER: 33 + 17 cases already pass in a harness, and a harness is exactly what cannot reach a cron. ⚠️ AND `never_ran` PERSISTING IS NOT A FAILURE OF THIS ROW TODAY — it is the accurate reading until the workflow first fires, and the row must not be re-filed as broken on the strength of it. ⚠️ IF THE OPERATOR DECIDES THE WATCHER SHOULD NOT RUN, this row does NOT clear — it CHANGES: the PR queue is then unwatched from CI, which is a legitimate decision and must be RECORDED as one rather than left looking like a cadence about to start.", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-11"}, "id": "OI-20260902-PR-QUEUE-WATCHER-SHIPPED-AND-ITS-CRON-HAS-NEVER-FIRED", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "audit", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/OPEN-ITEMS.json#OI-20260902-PR-QUEUE-WATCHER-SHIPPED-AND-ITS-CRON-HAS-NEVER-FIRED", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/OPEN-ITEMS.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='OI-20260902-PR-QUEUE-WATCHER-SHIPPED-AND-ITS-CRON-HAS-NEVER-FIRED'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[LOUD] ⚠️ CORRECTED 2026-09-04 — THE HEADLINE IN THIS ROW'S OWN ID IS NOW FALSE AND THE ID IS KEPT ONLY BECAUSE OTHER ROWS LINK IT BY NAME: THE CRON HAS FIRED. Measured over the workflow's COMPLETE scheduled-run history (population: all 6 runs of .github/workflows/pr-queue-watch.yml with event=schedule) — runs #3 #4 #5 #6 #7 #8, 2026-09-02T21:16:26Z through 2026-09-04T05:04:47Z. `never_ran` is no longer the accurate reading and must not be re-derived from the id. ⚠️ AND FOUR OF THOSE SIX CONCLUDED `failure` (#3 #5 #7 #8) — WHICH IS THE WATCHER WORKING, NOT BREAKING, and a session must not read it the other way. I nearly filed the opposite finding: the job exits 1 DELIBERATELY to page, and run #8's log reads `escalate: True -- the condition has stood 17.4h since the last page (floor 12.0h)` / `receipt : written` / `##[error]PR-QUEUE ESCALATION -- open PRs are waiting on a merge decision past the threshold`. So a red run here means THE QUEUE IS UNWORKED, not that the watcher is down — reading `conclusion: failure` as 'this mechanism is broken' is the unprovenanced-diagnostic trap this repo has a guard for. The real finding is what it was paging ABOUT, filed as MI-115. THE TEXT BELOW IS THE ORIGINAL AND IS PRESERVED AS THE RECORD OF WHAT WAS TRUE WHEN THE ROW WAS OPENED. === The MANAGER-SIDE queue watcher is merged and its clock has never run. scripts/ops/pr_queue_latency.py times how long an open, unmerged PR has sat with no push to its head branch — the MCP-free half of queue_latency.py, which asks the same question about SESSIONS, needs list_sessions (an mcp__* tool CI does not hold) and therefore reports `unknown` permanently. Fired ONLY by .github/workflows/pr-queue-watch.yml (cron 41 */6 * * * + dispatch): deliberately not reachable from a prompt, a skill or a checklist step, because every mechanism the manager had to CHOOSE to run went unused while every mechanism that STOOD IN THE WAY worked. ⚠️ THE CRON HAS NEVER FIRED. scripts/ci/check_pr_queue_watch.py grades `never_ran` today and that is the CORRECT reading, not a bug — and it PASSES on it deliberately, because failing would red every PR in the repo on merge day, which is how a guard gets disabled instead of fixed. The guard ARMS ITSELF on the first successful run: once a receipt exists, `stale` becomes reachable and there is no flag to unset. ⚠️ A CRON IS NOT EVIDENCE OF A RUN — probes.yml's first scheduled run fired ~4h50m late and once instead of daily, and session-reaper.yml failed five runs before its first success. This one lands its receipt through .github/actions/commit-to-main with verify-merged, so an unmerged receipt fails the run rather than exiting 0 on a PR that merely exists. verified_at is deliberately null: no observation has been made, and dating it today would be a claim of one."}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 2, "clears_when": "THREE PARTS, and they are different facts — say which you cleared rather than clearing the row on the easiest. (1) The operator gives the Tier-2 OK and PR #10858 merges, at which point install_systemd_units.sh's deploy/*.timer glob installs and enables the units on the next deploy. ⚠️ MERGING IS THE VM MUTATION HERE — there is no separate install step — which is exactly why it is Tier-2 and must not be merged on a session's own judgement. (2) The timer is OBSERVED firing: /api/diag/services shows ict-work-digest.timer enabled AND runtime_logs/work_digest_receipt.json carries `outcome: sent` from a SCHEDULED firing. Read /api/diag/log_file?name=work_digest_receipt, never the OnCalendar= line — a manual run writes an identical receipt, and the failure class being watched is a cadence that does not fire. (3) The now-redundant `schedule:` block is removed from work-digest.yml, leaving workflow_dispatch. ⚠️ (3) IS DELIBERATELY NOT IN THE SAME PR, so that a timer which fails to install cannot take the digest to ZERO. Until it lands BOTH carriers run and the operator sees ~5 duplicate digests a day — that is the accepted cost of not risking a silent outage, and it is a KNOWN state, not a defect to re-file. ⚠️ IF THE OPERATOR DECLINES the timer, this row does NOT clear — it CHANGES: the digest is then carried by an Actions cron measured at 5 firings a day against 24 declared, which is a legitimate decision and must be RECORDED as one with that consequence stated.", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-12"}, "id": "OI-20260902-HOURLY-DIGEST-CARRIER-PREPARED-AND-HELD-FOR-A-TIER-2-OK", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "audit", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/OPEN-ITEMS.json#OI-20260902-HOURLY-DIGEST-CARRIER-PREPARED-AND-HELD-FOR-A-TIER-2-OK", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/OPEN-ITEMS.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='OI-20260902-HOURLY-DIGEST-CARRIER-PREPARED-AND-HELD-FOR-A-TIER-2-OK'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[LOUD] The hourly work digest has NO durable carrier, and the replacement is PREPARED BUT NOT APPLIED, held on a Tier-2 operator OK. .github/workflows/work-digest.yml declares `20 * * * *`; measured over its COMPLETE run history it fired 5 times in a day, at :19, :10, :33 and :47 — never on its declared minute. deploy/ict-work-digest.{service,timer} moves it onto the VM's own clock (OnCalendar=hourly), which is what demonstrably fires here — ict-git-sync pulls every ~5 min."}
