{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 1, "clears_when": "THREE things, and they are different facts — say which you cleared rather than clearing on the easiest. (1) SCHEDULER EXISTS: a `manager-wake` Routine is listed by list_triggers with a real cron schedule AND carries a connector, so its fired sessions actually hold create_trigger/fire_trigger. (2) IT FIRED ON ITS OWN: `check_wake_liveness.py` grades `fresh` on a receipt whose newest run was written by a SCHEDULED firing, not a hand-fired one — read the receipt's `detail`, since a hand-fired run writes a byte-similar entry, and this is the same event-vs-artifact distinction that keeps OI-20260902-DIGEST-MOVED-OFF-A-CRON-THAT-DOES-NOT-FIRE-AND-THE-NEW-TRIGGER-HAS-NEVER-FIRED open. (3) IT WOKE SOMEBODY UNATTENDED: a receipt entry with outcome `poked` from a scheduled fire, AND that manager observed running afterwards via get_session, with NO human in the loop for any step. ⚠️ THE MERGE OF #11014 CLEARS NOTHING — a merge is not a deploy and a deploy is not a fire. ⚠️ A GREEN CI CLEARS NOTHING: 28 tests and PASS 65 are a harness, and a harness is exactly what cannot reach a Routine. ⚠️ THE 2026-09-04T23:11:26Z RECEIPT ON MAIN CLEARS NOTHING — that fire was triggered by hand from the MI-123 session and proves DELIVERY, not CADENCE, which is the whole thing this row is open about. ⚠️ AND IF THE OPERATOR DECIDES NOT TO CREATE THE ROUTINE, THIS ROW DOES NOT CLEAR — IT CHANGES: a manager's idle time is then bounded by nothing, which is a legitimate decision and must be RECORDED as one w", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-05"}, "id": "OI-20260904-MANAGER-WAKE-BUILT-AND-ITS-SCHEDULER-DOES-NOT-EXIST", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "audit", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/OPEN-ITEMS.json#OI-20260904-MANAGER-WAKE-BUILT-AND-ITS-SCHEDULER-DOES-NOT-EXIST", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/OPEN-ITEM