{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 14, "clears_when": "BASIS: the percentages here are ARITHMETIC on two constants (29.0 / 60.0), not a\nsample - they state what one timeout WOULD cost whenever it fires. The observations\nthey are set against are max pass 58940.8ms over n=694 passes (process started\n2026-08-15T23:06:29Z) and max pass 40872.5ms over n=11 (process started\n2026-08-16T06:24:32Z). How OFTEN it fires is UNMEASURED, which is criterion 1 and the\nreason this row proposes no value.\n\n1. COUNT IT FIRST. Add a counter for the _IB_FETCH_QUEUE_TIMEOUT_S and\n_IB_USAGE_LOCK_WAIT_S paths (both currently log-only) so the firing RATE is a number\nrather than an inference from a max. Tier-1, and it is the prerequisite for the rest.\n2. Only then decide whether 29.0s belongs inside a 60s budget, and state explicitly\nwhat the alternative costs in degraded fetches - do not optimise the interval into a\nMONITOR BLIND.\n3. Consider decoupling the derivation: the queue timeout is currently a function of\nIB_FETCH_TIMEOUT_S, so tuning the per-request cap silently moves the exit budget too.", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-21"}, "id": "BL-20260816-IB-QUEUE-TIMEOUT-EXCEEDS-EXIT-BUDGET", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "review", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json#BL-20260816-IB-QUEUE-TIMEOUT-EXCEEDS-EXIT-BUDGET", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='BL-20260816-IB-QUEUE-TIMEOUT-EXCEEDS-EXIT-BUDGET'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[health] A single IB fetch queue timeout consumes 48% of the 60s exit-evaluation budget, and the two numbers were set in different sessions with no reference to each other"}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 14, "clears_when": "(1) Measure cap_R per trade for all 6 legs from the live journal and/or the canonical CSVs (scripts/research/m20_ladder_headroom.py already computes cap_r_p10/p50/p90 per leg — extend it to compare against each leg's declared arm_r rather than adding a second definition); (2) for every leg where arm_r exceeds the cap_R the leg actually attains, EITHER re-sweep the arm on a live-parity (0.099) book and propose the corrected value to the operator, OR record the lever as inert so the coverage matrix stops counting it as shipped; (3) add a check (CI guard or declare-time refusal) that a declared R-threshold lever is reachable under the leg's own TP geometry, so this class cannot be re-declared silently; (4) Tier-3: any arm_r change is an operator decision — this item proposes, it does not flip.", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-21"}, "id": "BL-20260816-TRAIL-DECAY-ARM-R-SITS-ABOVE-THE-VENUE-TP-CAP", "next_action": "ask_operator", "origin": {"kind": "review", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json#BL-20260816-TRAIL-DECAY-ARM-R-SITS-ABOVE-THE-VENUE-TP-CAP", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='BL-20260816-TRAIL-DECAY-ARM-R-SITS-ABOVE-THE-VENUE-TP-CAP'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[health] [exit-levers] A declared trail_decay_arm_r can sit ABOVE the R the 9.9% TP cap allows, making the lever silently inert on the live leg"}
