{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 30, "clears_when": "1. Add a fourth state (`partially_filled`) carrying the filled qty, and surface it in the close's retMsg so a monitor retry's log says the size changed.\n2. Read `filled`/`remaining` explicitly rather than inferring from `status`.\n3. Separately, MEASURE IB futures fill latency on an open venue before treating 6s as validated -- it is a chosen value, not a measured one.", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-21"}, "id": "BL-20260816-IB-CLOSE-CANCEL-IGNORES-PARTIAL-FILL", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "review", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json#BL-20260816-IB-CLOSE-CANCEL-IGNORES-PARTIAL-FILL", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='BL-20260816-IB-CLOSE-CANCEL-IGNORES-PARTIAL-FILL'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[health] _cancel_own_close_order has no PARTIAL-fill state: a close order half-filled at the 6s deadline reads as fully 'cancelled'"}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 30, "clears_when": "1. The deferral survives a restart -- persisted to `runtime_logs/` keyed on (account, symbol, direction) with its set-time, in the shape the three *_alert_state.json latches already use, and reloaded on first read rather than assumed empty.\n2. The persisted read is three-state and says which: `deferral_active` / `no_deferral` (we looked, nothing is deferred) / `could_not_read` (we did not look). A read failure MUST NOT resolve to 'no deferral' -- that is the current behaviour dressed up, and it is the collapsed-state failure the guard exists to catch. Register with collapsed-state-guard.\n3. A test that asserts a simulated restart INSIDE the cooldown window does not re-attempt the close: set the marker, drop the module state, reload, assert the monitor defers. The existing tests cannot catch this because they never cross a process boundary.\n4. CLAUDE.md's `IB_CLOSE_RETRY_COOLDOWN_S` row loses the unqualified 'fail-safe' claim and states both directions explicitly.\n5. MEASURED, not merely shipped: across at least one real deploy while a deferral is active on ib_paper, `/api/diag/ib_open_orders` shows no new MKT close order for that (account, symbol, direction). Verifying this requires the gateway reachable -- a `read_state: could_not_look` run is NOT evidence of absence.", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-21"}, "id": "BL-20260816-IB-CLOSE-RETRY-COOLDOWN-PROCESS-SCOPED", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "review", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json#BL-20260816-IB-CLOSE-RETRY-COOLDOWN-PROCESS-SCOPED", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='BL-20260816-IB-CLOSE-RETRY-COOLDOWN-PROCESS-SCOPED'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[health] IB_CLOSE_RETRY_COOLDOWN_S's deferral lives in a module global, so EVERY trader restart re-opens the close-retry churn window the cooldown exists to close -- and this repo restarts the trader on every merge to main"}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 14, "clears_when": "1. SHIPPED: `list_open_orders` rows carry `client_id` (null = IB did not report it, which is NOT 'client 0').\n2. SHIPPED: `scripts/ops/cancel_ib_order.py` + the Tier-2 `cancel-ib-order` system-action -- reads account-wide, resolves the owner, connects AS that clientId, cancels, re-reads to verify. Dry-run by default. Lookup is three-state and never collapsed (`could_not_look` / `not_found` / `found`), and an accepted cancel whose verification read fails reports `cancelled_unconfirmed` rather than success.\n3. SHIPPED: two default refusals with SEPARATE explicit overrides -- a protective order (OCA-grouped or a stop/trailing type) needs `--force-protective`; a clientId below 9000 (the trader's execution band) needs `--force-client-id`, because taking over that id would evict the trader's live IB session.\n4. OPEN -- LIVE VERIFICATION. Not yet exercised against a real broker: at 12:37:33Z the IB breaker was open and `/api/diag/ib_open_orders` returned `read_state: could_not_look`. The action must be run in DRY-RUN first against a real order and shown to resolve a real `owner_client_id`, then applied and verified `gone`. A `could_not_look` run is NOT evidence either way. Until then the 14 unit tests prove the DECISION logic only, never the IB semantics.\n5. OPEN -- the class this does NOT cover: an order owned by the trader's own live execution client cannot be cancelled by any ops session (connecting as that id evicts the trader). The trader can cancel its own orders; nothing else …[truncated]", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-21"}, "id": "BL-20260816-NO-PER-ORDER-IB-CANCEL", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "review", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json#BL-20260816-NO-PER-ORDER-IB-CANCEL", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='BL-20260816-NO-PER-ORDER-IB-CANCEL'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[health] No way to cancel a SINGLE IB order from any session -- the only two mechanisms either PLACE another order or strip every protective stop on the account"}
