{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 30, "clears_when": "A session that reads the board and reports 'no open MERGE SLOT CLAIM' can point to the evidence that its read reached the END -- a short page, or an empty probe past the last index -- and not merely to the page it happened to fetch. Concretely closeable by either: (a) both protocol surfaces carrying the short-page assertion AND a subsequent session's claim comment citing it (e.g. 'perPage=3 page=N returned 2 => tail'), or (b) a helper that establishes the end programmatically and is actually invoked by the merge preflight. Note what does NOT close this: adding a sentence telling sessions to 'be careful' or 'read the newest comments', which is what both docs already said while the violation happened.", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-21"}, "id": "BL-20260817-BOARD-TAIL-READ-CANNOT-ASSERT-IT-REACHED-THE-END", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "review", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json#BL-20260817-BOARD-TAIL-READ-CANNOT-ASSERT-IT-REACHED-THE-END", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='BL-20260817-BOARD-TAIL-READ-CANNOT-ASSERT-IT-REACHED-THE-END'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[health] The coordination-board tail read has no way to assert it reached the END, so a full page of comments is byte-indistinguishable from the real tail -- and 'no open MERGE SLOT CLAIM' gets reported off a page boundary"}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 30, "clears_when": "OBSERVABLE: `m20_coverage_rollup.py --done-condition` prints a movable count that is a MEASURED cut -- every cell it calls movable has a sweep arm a session could actually invoke -- and names any cell whose status implies a remedy that does not exist, with the route that does. Closes when that holds AND the claim is checked against the sweep/harness surfaces by a test rather than by a constant a session maintains by hand (a hardcoded list goes stale exactly the way the '4 movable' figure did). Does NOT close by correcting the number in a doc, a ping, or a roadmap entry: three of those already carried the wrong figure while the tool kept emitting it.", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-21"}, "id": "BL-20260817-ROLLUP-CALLS-A-CELL-MOVABLE-WHEN-NO-SWEEP-PATH-EXISTS", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "review", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json#BL-20260817-ROLLUP-CALLS-A-CELL-MOVABLE-WHEN-NO-SWEEP-PATH-EXISTS", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='BL-20260817-ROLLUP-CALLS-A-CELL-MOVABLE-WHEN-NO-SWEEP-PATH-EXISTS'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[health] m20_coverage_rollup called 4 done-condition cells 'movable by a session' when ZERO were: never_attempted prints 'no sweep has been run' for levers that have no sweep arm to run"}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 30, "clears_when": "1. Gather evidence from CODE, not raw text: strip comments and string bodies before the scan (`tokenize`, or `ast` with docstring nodes dropped) so a state counts only where it appears as a literal in executable position. Reproduce case (b) as a test FIRST — a prose-only file must go from 'all states read' to 'no consumer branches on them' — otherwise the fix is unverified against the failure it targets.\n2. Then handle the constants half, which the same pass makes tractable: resolve `th.UNKNOWN`-style attribute references against the producer module's own declared constants, so a constants-based consumer stops reading as zero evidence. Until this lands, contracts over constants-based APIs are satisfied only by whichever files happen to use literals, which is luck, not coverage.\n3. Re-run `--all` across the existing 13 contracts and REPORT THE DELTA. Do not assume the change is inert: any contract whose coverage was silently resting on a docstring will newly fail, and each such failure is a REAL finding this guard has been missing — triage them, do not annotate them away.\n4. If a state is genuinely unread, that is the collapse the guard names; fix the consumer, not the guard.", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-21"}, "id": "BL-20260817-COLLAPSED-STATE-GUARD-READS-PROSE", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "review", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json#BL-20260817-COLLAPSED-STATE-GUARD-READS-PROSE", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='BL-20260817-COLLAPSED-STATE-GUARD-READS-PROSE'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[health] collapsed-state-guard counts PROSE as a branch — a docstring naming every state satisfies the coverage it exists to enforce, and one quoted state in a comment fails a file that branches correctly"}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 30, "clears_when": "Something must be able to notice a committed artifact that exists only on an unmerged branch, OR the artifact-producing workflow must not be able to leave one behind. Two candidate shapes, neither chosen here: (a) the sweep-corpus workflow fails loudly if its PR is not merged within N hours instead of pushing and forgetting; (b) a periodic check compares artifact branches' committed artifacts against main by CONTENT and alerts on a non-empty difference. Do NOT implement it as an ancestry check -- see the note in `detail`. Closing this by hand-landing one branch is NOT a resolution; that already happened in #9896 and the mechanism is still unguarded.", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-21"}, "id": "BL-20260817-AN-UNMERGED-ARTIFACT-BRANCH-SILENCES-A-GUARD-THAT-READS-MAIN", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "review", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json#BL-20260817-AN-UNMERGED-ARTIFACT-BRANCH-SILENCES-A-GUARD-THAT-READS-MAIN", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='BL-20260817-AN-UNMERGED-ARTIFACT-BRANCH-SILENCES-A-GUARD-THAT-READS-MAIN'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[health] A committed artifact left on an unmerged branch silences every guard that grades against main"}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 14, "clears_when": "Two independent fixes, both wanted. (1) DOC: system-actions.md and the confirmation guidance must state that a run conclusion of failure does NOT mean the action did not run, and that the authoritative signal is the 'Wrapper exit code:' line in the run log — especially before re-dispatching any MUTATING action. (2) WORKFLOW: make the comment-back step resilient (retry on 5xx, and/or continue-on-error so a comment failure cannot mark a successful action red). Do NOT resolve this by making the workflow retry the ACTION on a comment failure — that is the double-apply bug this row exists to prevent.", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-21"}, "id": "BL-20260817-SYSTEM-ACTION-RUN-CONCLUSION-CAN-BE-FAILURE-AFTER-THE-ACTION-SUCCEEDED", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "review", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json#BL-20260817-SYSTEM-ACTION-RUN-CONCLUSION-CAN-BE-FAILURE-AFTER-THE-ACTION-SUCCEEDED", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='BL-20260817-SYSTEM-ACTION-RUN-CONCLUSION-CAN-BE-FAILURE-AFTER-THE-ACTION-SUCCEEDED'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[health] A system-action run can report conclusion=failure AFTER the action succeeded — re-running on that signal double-applies it"}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 45, "clears_when": "ORDER MATTERS. First make the id scan wrap-tolerant (join '-\\n\\s*' before matching, or match across the newline) and prove it with BOTH controls: the regime-selectivity SKILL.md wrapped id must read as FILED, and a planted genuinely-unfiled wrapped id must still FAIL. Only then add '.claude' to SEARCH_DIRS. Adding the directory first is a known false-failure and must not be done. Do NOT resolve this by reflowing the SKILL.md prose -- the document is correct; the scanner is the thing that is wrong.", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-21"}, "id": "BL-20260817-BACKLOG-REF-GUARD-SKIPS-DOT-CLAUDE-AND-CANNOT-SEE-A-WRAPPED-ID", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "review", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json#BL-20260817-BACKLOG-REF-GUARD-SKIPS-DOT-CLAUDE-AND-CANNOT-SEE-A-WRAPPED-ID", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='BL-20260817-BACKLOG-REF-GUARD-SKIPS-DOT-CLAUDE-AND-CANNOT-SEE-A-WRAPPED-ID'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[health] check_backlog_refs does not scan .claude/, and its id regex cannot span a hard-wrapped line — fix the wrap FIRST or adding the dir false-fails a correct skill"}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 30, "clears_when": "1. Do NOT widen the window into a blocking wait on the live path without a bound — the point of the verification is that it is cheap and honest.\n2. Make PendingCancel its OWN state rather than folding it into cancel_not_effective. `still_present` currently conflates 'the broker refused / did nothing' with 'the broker ACCEPTED and is working on it' — two different facts with different correct responses (investigate vs wait). This is the collapsed-states rule applied to the ops layer: a `cancel_pending` verdict should exit 0 with a clear 're-verify, do not re-issue' instruction.\n3. Exit code should reflect that distinction, since the issue comment renders exit != 0 as a red FAILED and that is what a reader acts on.\n4. Add the post-check the session had to do by hand: a short bounded re-read (or an explicit instruction naming /api/diag/ib_open_orders) so the operator is not left to invent the verification step.", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-21"}, "id": "BL-20260817-CANCEL-IB-ORDER-VERIFY-WINDOW-TOO-SHORT", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "review", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json#BL-20260817-CANCEL-IB-ORDER-VERIFY-WINDOW-TOO-SHORT", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='BL-20260817-CANCEL-IB-ORDER-VERIFY-WINDOW-TOO-SHORT'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[health] cancel-ib-order reports cancel_not_effective + exit 1 for a cancel that DOES complete moments later — correct at the instant it checks, wrong as a final verdict, and it invites a blind retry"}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 14, "clears_when": "Add a fifth refusal: read the current price and refuse when the declared target is already through the market for the position's direction (long: target <= last; short: target >= last), reporting the gap and naming the consequence ('this would fill immediately at market'). Refuse by default and require an explicit separate opt-in flag whose NAME says flatten, not attach — or route the caller to `flatten-ib-position`, which already exists and is honestly named. The price read must be three-state: a price we could not read is `could_not_look` and must also refuse, never fall through to placing. DONE when a dry-run against trade 4487 reports the through-market refusal instead of a clean plan.", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-21"}, "id": "BL-20260818-ATTACH-IB-TARGET-NO-THROUGH-MARKET-REFUSAL", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "review", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json#BL-20260818-ATTACH-IB-TARGET-NO-THROUGH-MARKET-REFUSAL", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='BL-20260818-ATTACH-IB-TARGET-NO-THROUGH-MARKET-REFUSAL'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[health] attach-ib-target has four refusals but none for a declared target already THROUGH the market — on such a position it is a market exit wearing the label of a protective repair"}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 30, "clears_when": "Add `detail` (and any other free-text key the schema admits) to `_row_text`, then re-run `--base origin/main` over a window of recent merges to size the fallout before enforcing — some existing rows will newly trip, and that population should be counted and triaged, not discovered one PR at a time. Add a self-test pinning both directions: a row with its basis ONLY in `detail` must PASS, and a row with a bare claim ONLY in `detail` must FAIL. DONE when both self-test cases hold.", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-21"}, "id": "BL-20260818-CLAIM-BASIS-GUARD-DOES-NOT-READ-THE-DETAIL-FIELD", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "review", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json#BL-20260818-CLAIM-BASIS-GUARD-DOES-NOT-READ-THE-DETAIL-FIELD", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='BL-20260818-CLAIM-BASIS-GUARD-DOES-NOT-READ-THE-DETAIL-FIELD'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[health] claim-basis-guard's _row_text reads title/description/source/action/resolution/resolution_criteria but NOT `detail` — the field recent backlog rows actually put their evidence in, so it fails well-evidenced rows and passes bare claims"}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 30, "clears_when": "RE-POINTED 2026-08-23 (the original source, the 22:30Z MES run, is verify-only now and both IB positions already hold resting targets - see progress). Satisfied by the NEXT genuine target-naked occurrence on any IB position repaired via `attach-ib-target --apply`, graded correctly and checkably: `target_resting` with the order on the book, or `target_filled` with position_after flat/reduced, or `absent_unexplained` with position_after showing the position standing at full size. A run graded `could_not_look` does NOT satisfy it - that is the state that says we did not look. This is opportunistic by design: the alternative is creating an unprotected position deliberately, which is not an acceptable way to earn evidence.", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-21"}, "id": "BL-20260818-ATTACH-IB-TARGET-VERIFY-CANNOT-EXPRESS-FILLED", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "review", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json#BL-20260818-ATTACH-IB-TARGET-VERIFY-CANNOT-EXPRESS-FILLED", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='BL-20260818-ATTACH-IB-TARGET-VERIFY-CANNOT-EXPRESS-FILLED'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[health] attach-ib-target verifies by asking whether a target is RESTING, so a marketable target that FILLS reports `place_not_effective` / `still_absent` + exit 1 — a red on a successful flatten, and a retry on that signal opens a naked reverse position"}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 14, "clears_when": "Next phase, Tier-3. Design first, in this order: (1) pct-per-leg against each leg's own size, never a shared qty; (2) per-leg cumulative-partial state, with a leg reaching 100% closing THAT LEG and the package draining exactly as the full-close path now does; (3) a non-idempotent partial-application story — a retry must not re-reduce a leg that already succeeded, which rules out copying the full-close path's blanket 'leave it and retry next tick'. Likely needs a per-leg applied-marker keyed on (package, leg, verdict instance). Then: tests pinning unequal leg sizes (use the measured 149.5-vs-48,625.8 XRP shape), a partial that lands on one leg and fails on another, and a leg crossing 100% while a sibling does not. DONE when all three effectuation branches resolve legs through `_package_open_legs` and no branch is silently single-leg. Also worth measuring on the way in (cheap, and it sizes the backlog): has a partial-close verdict ever fired on a multi-leg package? — packages with >1 trade row whose legs' position_size diverges from entry size.", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-21"}, "id": "BL-20260818-PARTIAL-CLOSE-STILL-SINGLE-LEG", "next_action": "ask_operator", "origin": {"kind": "review", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json#BL-20260818-PARTIAL-CLOSE-STILL-SINGLE-LEG", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='BL-20260818-PARTIAL-CLOSE-STILL-SINGLE-LEG'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[health] _apply_partial_close still resolves only linked_trade_id — the last effectuation branch not yet package-wide; QUEUED for the next phase so the exit path is comprehensively multi-leg rather than multi-leg in two branches of three"}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 30, "clears_when": "Add a two-line interpreter guard at the top of the sweep and the backtest harnesses: if the required third-party imports are unavailable, exit non-zero with the exact venv path to use rather than letting the ImportError escape into a subprocess capture. Also state the venv invocation in the `backtesting` and `exit-refinement` skills, which are what a session reads before dispatching one. DONE when a bare-python3 invocation prints the correct command.", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-21"}, "id": "BL-20260818-RESEARCH-HARNESSES-HAVE-NO-VENV-GUARD", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "review", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json#BL-20260818-RESEARCH-HARNESSES-HAVE-NO-VENV-GUARD", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='BL-20260818-RESEARCH-HARNESSES-HAVE-NO-VENV-GUARD'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[health] the sweep and every backtest harness require the repo venv and neither state nor check it — bare python3 fails deep, as a swallowed ImportError"}
