{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 14, "clears_when": "1. THE GC'S DOCSTRING IS CORRECTED so the next session does not repeat this. It describes a 15 GB tree of unpinned probe arms; the measured state is 111 of 115 dirs pinned and 0.09 GB reclaimable. Leaving that text is how the inert remedy gets reached for again.\n2. RETENTION EXISTS FOR THE TWO UNCOVERED TREES — `runtime_logs/m2*_head` and `ml/experiments-runs` — or a recorded decision that they are kept indefinitely on purpose. They are 7.0 GB combined with no tool and no policy.\n3. THE FILL RATE IS MEASURED (GB/day over at least a week) so urgency rests on a trend rather than on one 91% reading.\n4. Any deletion is Tier-2, runs report-only first, and states bytes reclaimed against bytes scanned — the GC's own summary shape, which is the right one.", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-21"}, "id": "BL-20260825-TRAINER-DISK-AT-91PCT-AND-ITS-OWN-GC-RECLAIMS-ALMOST-NOTHING", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "review", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json#BL-20260825-TRAINER-DISK-AT-91PCT-AND-ITS-OWN-GC-RECLAIMS-ALMOST-NOTHING", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='BL-20260825-TRAINER-DISK-AT-91PCT-AND-ITS-OWN-GC-RECLAIMS-ALMOST-NOTHING'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[health] Trainer root is at 91% with 4.5 GB left, and `trainer_dataset_gc.py` — the tool written for exactly this — would reclaim 0.09 GB, not gigabytes; at its own 30-day setting it reclaims NOTHING"}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 14, "clears_when": "1. `_probe_liveness` returns a THREE-STATE result — `ok` / `timed_out` / `disconnected` — instead of a bare bool, so the caller can branch on which failure occurred. Registering it with `collapsed-state-guard` is the natural home.\n2. A peer-close does NOT open the breaker for the full cooldown. The minimum honest change: allow ONE immediate reconnect attempt on `disconnected` before tripping, so the common case costs a reconnect rather than 120s of fleet blindness. It MUST stay bounded — an unbounded retry against a wedged gateway is the failure class the breaker exists for.\n3. Re-measure the episode count over a comparable window and quote it with its denominator. The claim to beat: 74 episodes / 235 failures over 2026-08-20T07:01Z–2026-08-25T09:28Z.\n4. The monitor-blindness consequence is checked, not assumed: confirm an open IB position's dynamic exit runs through a peer-close episode after the change.", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-21"}, "id": "BL-20260825-IB-BREAKER-CANNOT-TELL-A-PEER-CLOSE-FROM-A-WEDGE", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "review", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json#BL-20260825-IB-BREAKER-CANNOT-TELL-A-PEER-CLOSE-FROM-A-WEDGE", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='BL-20260825-IB-BREAKER-CANNOT-TELL-A-PEER-CLOSE-FROM-A-WEDGE'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[health] The IB circuit breaker treats a PEER-CLOSE identically to a WEDGE and suppresses ALL IB calls for 120s — for the one condition a reconnect clears in milliseconds. 74 episodes in 5.4 days, each blinding every IB leg and short-circuiting the monitor on an OPEN position"}
