{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 30, "clears_when": "1. _cancel_resting_orders_for_symbol reports its EFFECT, not its calls: after cancelling it re-reads account-wide and returns a per-leg three-state result -- confirmed_gone / refused_not_found (IBKR 10147) / unverified (we could not look) -- never collapsing the last two. Register the contract with collapsed-state-guard.\n2. place_protective BRANCHES on that result. The invariant to pin: never place a fresh bracket while legs it attempted to cancel are still resting. Refusing is safe precisely because those legs ARE the protection -- the cost is that the trail does not advance, which is visible and alertable, whereas arming anyway is the accumulation this row describes. The genuinely-naked case (pre-cancel found nothing) must still arm.\n3. Thread oca_key through the modify path so the ordinary re-arm is idempotent: order_monitor._apply_update -> execute.modify_open_order -> IBClient.modify_protective -> place_protective. Today exactly ONE of the two place_protective call sites passes it (order_monitor:6950, naked autoprotect); execute.py:2536 passes symbol/direction/qty/sl/tp and nothing else. MIND THE MIGRATION -- order_monitor:7670 already records that a scoped cancel will not match legs resting in legacy oca-protect-<reqId> groups, so switching the modify path to the scoped key WITHOUT criterion 2 in place makes existing positions worse, not better. Criterion 2 lands first.\n4. A regression test that plants a resting leg the cancel cannot remove and asserts place_protective  …[truncated]", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-21"}, "id": "BL-20260825-PLACE-PROTECTIVE-COUNTS-THE-CANCEL-CALL-NOT-ITS-EFFECT", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "review", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json#BL-20260825-PLACE-PROTECTIVE-COUNTS-THE-CANCEL-CALL-NOT-ITS-EFFECT", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-