{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 30, "clears_when": "1. Every one of the 42 listed rows either gains a concrete exit condition (what measurement, deploy or decision retires it) or is re-dispositioned - resolved / wont_fix / superseded / moved to ROADMAP.md. Do NOT bulk-add a boilerplate criterion: a criterion nobody would ever check is the same permanent resident wearing a field.\n2. The 8 ml MILESTONE-PROGRAM rows move to ROADMAP.md and are superseded in the backlog with a pointer, so each milestone has exactly one home (two homes is how they drift).\n3. A guard or the session-end routine refuses a NEW kept_open row carrying no exit condition - filing is cheap, and this class grew because nothing checked at file time. Scope it diff-wise (the artifact-validity-guard shape) so the 42-row backlog is grandfathered rather than blocking every unrelated PR.\n4. Re-measure with the same predicate and report the count; the number, not a claim, closes this row.", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-21"}, "id": "BL-20260825-KEPT-OPEN-ROWS-WITH-NO-EXIT-CONDITION-CAN-NEVER-BE-RETIRED", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "review", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json#BL-20260825-KEPT-OPEN-ROWS-WITH-NO-EXIT-CONDITION-CAN-NEVER-BE-RETIRED", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='BL-20260825-KEPT-OPEN-ROWS-WITH-NO-EXIT-CONDITION-CAN-NEVER-BE-RETIRED'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[health] 42 kept_open rows across the three backlogs state NO condition that would ever close them - a permanent-resident class, and 8 of the 11 ml ones are milestone PROGRAMS filed as backlog items"}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 30, "clears_when": "1. The trainer's disk reading is APPENDED somewhere durable, one row per publish or per cadence window — the exposure_soak.jsonl shape — carrying the same uncollapsed {measured,reason} the current block already gets right. Cheapest correct option is a `disk` key on the existing training_cycle.jsonl rows or a sibling jsonl in the mirror; both already rsync to the live VM and are already readable via /api/bot/ml/* and the diag log_file allowlist.\n2. A reader can compute GB/day over a stated window, and reports it BESIDE the number of samples and the window length — a rate over 3 points is not the claim a rate over 300 is.\n3. WITH the rate in hand, the parent row's urgency question is answered one way or the other and recorded: either a projected date the root fills, or a measured statement that it oscillates within a band and does not trend. Either answer closes it; neither may be asserted without the series.\n4. NOT a criterion, stated so it is not mistaken for one: nothing here proposes DELETING anything. Retention for the two uncovered trees (ml/experiments-runs, runtime_logs/m2*_head) is the parent row's criterion 2 and is Tier-2 on the trainer.", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-21"}, "id": "BL-20260825-TRAINER-DISK-IS-MEASURED-EVERY-2-MINUTES-AND-ACCUMULATED-NEVER", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "review", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json#BL-20260825-TRAINER-DISK-IS-MEASURED-EVERY-2-MINUTES-AND-ACCUMULATED-NEVER", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='BL-20260825-TRAINER-DISK-IS-MEASURED-EVERY-2-MINUTES-AND-ACCUMULATED-NEVER'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[health] The trainer's disk fill RATE is structurally unknowable — the figure is recomputed every ~2 minutes into a file that is overwritten, so every reading is discarded"}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 14, "clears_when": "(a) A durable register exists in which every operator-owed item carries an OWNER-CLASS (genuinely-human: secret origination / physical-or-broker / judgement — vs defaulted-to-human: a wire exists or could) and an age; (b) an item aging past a stated threshold ESCALATES rather than being re-listed, and the escalation is measured not asserted; (c) every defaulted-to-human item is either automated or carries a recorded reason it cannot be, with the anti-pattern named — 'one remediation attempt failed' is not sufficient on its own, because the tested-pure-function remedy exists and has a precedent in this repo; (d) ⚠️ THE STANDING PART: a check that FAILS when an item has been carried across N sessions without a state change. Without (d) this closes and silently regresses, which is the same failure the register exists to prevent. Closing on 'a register was created' alone is NOT sufficient — the register must be shown to have MOVED an item at least once.", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-21"}, "id": "BL-20260825-OPERATOR-OWED-ITEMS-HAVE-NO-REGISTER-NO-AGE-AND-NO-ESCALATION", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "review", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json#BL-20260825-OPERATOR-OWED-ITEMS-HAVE-NO-REGISTER-NO-AGE-AND-NO-ESCALATION", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='BL-20260825-OPERATOR-OWED-ITEMS-HAVE-NO-REGISTER-NO-AGE-AND-NO-ESCALATION'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[health] Operator-owed items are handed forward in PROSE only: no register, no age, no escalation, and no distinction between genuinely-human and defaulted-to-human — so they accumulate silently across sessions"}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 30, "clears_when": "Either (a) the backlog guards detect a whole-file re-serialization and say so explicitly — e.g. when the added-line count exceeds some large fraction of the file, report 'this looks like a reformat, not N new rows' and grade against the PARSED rows rather than the diff lines, so the message names the real cause; or (b) a guard fails a PR that re-serializes a backlog with different json.dumps options, pointing at scripts/ops/backlog_append.py, which already carries a style-detection candidate list for exactly this and is the tool a session should be reaching for. (a) is the more useful of the two because it fixes the misleading MESSAGE, which is the costly part. Verified by a test that reformats a fixture backlog and asserts the guard's output names the reformat rather than listing rows.", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-21"}, "id": "BL-20260825-A-BACKLOG-REFORMAT-COLLAPSES-A-DIFF-SCOPED-GUARDS-GRANDFATHERING", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "review", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json#BL-20260825-A-BACKLOG-REFORMAT-COLLAPSES-A-DIFF-SCOPED-GUARDS-GRANDFATHERING", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='BL-20260825-A-BACKLOG-REFORMAT-COLLAPSES-A-DIFF-SCOPED-GUARDS-GRANDFATHERING'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[health] A whole-file reformat of a backlog silently converts every diff-scoped backlog guard from grandfathered to whole-tree, and the failure names the wrong cause"}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 30, "clears_when": "Decide and DOCUMENT which history is authoritative, then make the check say so rather than leaving a reader to infer it. Candidates, none yet chosen: count only commits on the default branch (stable, but blind during a PR, which is when the pressure is meant to be felt); count distinct PRs rather than commits; or keep commits and have the check PRINT that an in-branch count is provisional and will collapse on squash. Whatever is chosen, the check must not report a carry number whose meaning changes under the reader without saying so — that is the unstated-population problem this repo already has a rule for.", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-21"}, "id": "BL-20260825-CARRY-COUNT-RESETS-ACROSS-A-SQUASH-MERGE", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "review", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json#BL-20260825-CARRY-COUNT-RESETS-ACROSS-A-SQUASH-MERGE", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='BL-20260825-CARRY-COUNT-RESETS-ACROSS-A-SQUASH-MERGE'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[health] operator-owed carry count is not monotonic across a squash merge — N in-branch register commits collapse to 1"}
