{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 14, "clears_when": "An automation PR is OBSERVED merging green -- not the guard passing in a self-test, and not a hand-written declaration dropped onto one branch. The two candidate fixes are NOT equivalent and the choice must be recorded: (a) make each generating workflow WRITE its own .github/pr-landing/<slug>.json (tier 1, landing self) as part of the commit it already makes -- keeps the invariant that no PR lands undeclared, and is the recommended route; or (b) exempt automation/** in the guard -- cheaper, and it creates an UNGUARDED LANE on the guard's first day, which is the failure this repo names as 'a guard cheaper to lie to than to satisfy is worse than no guard'. If (b) is chosen anyway, the exemption must be NARROW (a named branch-prefix allowlist, never a bot-author test) and must be recorded as a deliberate decision rather than a fix. ALSO REQUIRED EITHER WAY: the stacked duplicates must be drained, and #10398 checked separately -- it was cut 2026-08-29, BEFORE the guard, so if it fails R11 too then the 'branch could have known' test is not doing what its docstring claims and that is a SECOND, worse finding.", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-21"}, "id": "BL-20260903-PR-LANDING-GUARD-RED-LOCKS-EVERY-AUTOMATION-PR", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "review", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json#BL-20260903-PR-LANDING-GUARD-RED-LOCKS-EVERY-AUTOMATION-PR", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='BL-20260903-PR-LANDING-GUARD-RED-LOCKS-EVERY-AUTOMATION-PR'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[health] pr-landing-guard R11 red-locks the entire automation PR lane, because a generating workflow cannot know to write a landing declaration"}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 45, "clears_when": "The header no longer contains the sentence \"removes the request\", and states instead that the request file is deliberately left in place and that success is read from the RESULT file (a bare comment URL, run exit 0) versus failure (a `FAILED:` prefix, run exit 1). Verified by two commands rather than by reading the prose: `grep -c 'removes the request' .github/workflows/board-post.yml` returns 0, and `grep -c 'FAILED:' .github/workflows/board-post.yml` returns a non-zero count inside the header block. NOT sufficient: the workflow continuing to work -- it already works, and the defect is in what it TELLS a session about itself. NOT sufficient either: this row being filed. The observable that would reopen it is a later session again reporting a successful board post as failed, or re-posting a duplicate on #6927, on the strength of the request file's presence.", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-21"}, "id": "BL-20260903-BOARD-POST-HEADER-CLAIMS-IT-REMOVES-THE-REQUEST-AND-THE-CODE-DELIBERATELY-DOES-NOT", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "review", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json#BL-20260903-BOARD-POST-HEADER-CLAIMS-IT-REMOVES-THE-REQUEST-AND-THE-CODE-DELIBERATELY-DOES-NOT", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='BL-20260903-BOARD-POST-HEADER-CLAIMS-IT-REMOVES-THE-REQUEST-AND-THE-CODE-DELIBERATELY-DOES-NOT'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[health] board-post.yml's header says it removes the request file; the code deliberately keeps it, so a session verifying its own post reads the surviving file as a FAILED post"}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 14, "clears_when": "BOTH halves, and say which you cleared. (a) The digest and any other reader resolve this artifact through the canonical $DATA_DIR-based resolver rather than a repo-relative path -- verified by the digest naming /data/bot-data/... in its own output, not by reading the code. (b) The absence is DISAMBIGUATED. `present: false` today cannot distinguish 'no wedge has occurred since the writer shipped' from 'the writer is not running', and those are opposite facts -- the collapsed-state failure this repo files bugs about. The writer must emit an empty-but-present ledger with a timestamp so that `wedges: []` means WE LOOKED AND FOUND NONE, distinct from a missing file meaning WE DID NOT LOOK. ⚠️ NOT SUFFICIENT: creating the file by hand; the writer must produce it. ⚠️ AND CHECK THE DOWNGRADE IS STILL SAFE: while (b) is unresolved, confirm whether any broker_cancel_wedged close failure has occurred since MI-34 shipped -- if one has, it was silently in neither channel and that is an incident, not a gap.", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-21"}, "id": "BL-20260903-CLOSE-WEDGE-LEDGER-IS-ABSENT-SO-DOWNGRADED-WEDGES-ARE-IN-NEITHER-CHANNEL", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "review", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json#BL-20260903-CLOSE-WEDGE-LEDGER-IS-ABSENT-SO-DOWNGRADED-WEDGES-ARE-IN-NEITHER-CHANNEL", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='BL-20260903-CLOSE-WEDGE-LEDGER-IS-ABSENT-SO-DOWNGRADED-WEDGES-ARE-IN-NEITHER-CHANNEL'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[health] The standing close-wedge ledger does not exist, and the digest that replaced the pager reads a non-canonical path for it"}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 14, "clears_when": "The lease's freshness on origin/main is bounded by the heartbeat cadence rather than by a PR's merge latency. Candidates, not yet chosen: (a) heartbeat commits directly to main through the existing commit-to-main action, the way other receipts do — the lease is a receipt, not reviewable content, so a PR adds nothing; (b) the reader treats a lease found on a live manager BRANCH as authoritative, which is more complex and re-introduces 'which branch do I trust'; (c) the TTL is widened, which is the wrong direction — it makes a genuinely dead manager block its successor for longer. ⚠️ VERIFY BY MEASUREMENT, NOT BY CODE READING: after the fix, compare origin/main's heartbeat_at against the manager's own clock across at least three heartbeats and state the max lag. ⚠️ AND CHECK THE HISTORICAL QUESTION SEPARATELY: has any takeover ever occurred against a lease whose origin/main copy was stale while its holder was alive? MANAGER-LEASE.json's git history (59 revisions across 3 sessions as of 2026-09-03) is where that is answerable, and a `--force` claim carrying a reason is where it would show.", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-21"}, "id": "BL-20260903-LEASE-HEARTBEAT-REACHES-ORIGIN-MAIN-ONLY-WHEN-THE-MANAGER-PR-MERGES", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "review", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json#BL-20260903-LEASE-HEARTBEAT-REACHES-ORIGIN-MAIN-ONLY-WHEN-THE-MANAGER-PR-MERGES", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='BL-20260903-LEASE-HEARTBEAT-REACHES-ORIGIN-MAIN-ONLY-WHEN-THE-MANAGER-PR-MERGES'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[health] The manager lease heartbeat lands on the manager's BRANCH, so origin/main lags it by however long that PR takes to merge"}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 14, "clears_when": "The manager's wake cadence survives a single carrier failing, AND the failure is DETECTABLE. Interim mitigation already applied: two overlapping send_later wakeups (~4 min and ~7 min) so one miss does not open a gap -- that is redundancy, NOT a detector, and must not be reported as a fix. ⚠️ THE DETECTOR IS THE HARDER AND MORE IMPORTANT HALF: the gap must be measured from the OUTSIDE, against what the manager actually DID, never against what it scheduled. `MANAGER-LEASE.json`'s git history is the natural instrument because it records heartbeats that HAPPENED (59 revisions across 3 sessions as of 2026-09-03; gaps median 18 min, p90 66, max 480). A check that grades whether a timer was ARMED would have passed cleanly through this incident and is worse than nothing. ⚠️ ALSO CONSIDER whether the carrier should move off `send_later` entirely, the way the work digest was moved onto the VM's own systemd clock after cron proved unreliable here -- but note the operator's standing rule that nothing may depend on a mechanism that dies with the session.", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-21"}, "id": "BL-20260903-MANAGER-CADENCE-RIDES-A-SINGLE-UNMONITORED-TIMER-THAT-SILENTLY-DID-NOT-FIRE", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "review", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json#BL-20260903-MANAGER-CADENCE-RIDES-A-SINGLE-UNMONITORED-TIMER-THAT-SILENTLY-DID-NOT-FIRE", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='BL-20260903-MANAGER-CADENCE-RIDES-A-SINGLE-UNMONITORED-TIMER-THAT-SILENTLY-DID-NOT-FIRE'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[health] The manager's whole supervision cadence rode one send_later timer with no delivery guarantee and no detector, and it silently did not fire"}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 14, "clears_when": "A `landed_unproven` row must NAME its carrier or explicitly declare it has none. Concretely: (a) a `monitored_by` field naming the OI- row that will clear it, OR an explicit `monitored_by: null` WITH a reason -- and `null` must be a deliberate, readable state, never an absent field, because an absent field is precisely what makes carried and orphaned identical today; (b) a CHECK that fails when a row sits at `landed_unproven` with neither. ⚠️ THE CHECK IS THE POINT, NOT THE ONE-OFF LINKING PASS: the operator's standing rule is that these become hard mechanisms rather than habits, and a manual pass restores the same gap the next time a row lands. ⚠️ AND DO NOT INVENT A CARRIER TO SATISFY THE FIELD -- pointing a row at a monitoring row that does not actually cover it is worse than the honest null, because it converts 'nobody is watching' into a false claim that someone is. Where no monitoring row genuinely covers a landed_unproven row, the correct action is to FILE one with an honest clears_when, which is what the operator asked for.", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-21"}, "id": "BL-20260903-LANDED-UNPROVEN-ROWS-NAME-NO-CARRIER-SO-MONITORED-AND-FORGOTTEN-LOOK-IDENTICAL", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "review", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json#BL-20260903-LANDED-UNPROVEN-ROWS-NAME-NO-CARRIER-SO-MONITORED-AND-FORGOTTEN-LOOK-IDENTICAL", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='BL-20260903-LANDED-UNPROVEN-ROWS-NAME-NO-CARRIER-SO-MONITORED-AND-FORGOTTEN-LOOK-IDENTICAL'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[health] 25 of 26 landed_unproven rows name no monitoring carrier, so a row that IS being watched and a row nobody is watching render identically"}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 30, "clears_when": "check_lease distinguishes `released_by_me` from the other non-held states and treats it as a PASS for handover readiness, keeping `expired`, `unheld`, `held_by_other` and a release by a DIFFERENT session as FAIL. Evidence must be a planted positive in both directions: a fixture where THIS session released -> PASS, and one where ANOTHER session released -> FAIL. NOT SUFFICIENT: relaxing the check to accept any non-held state, which would let a displaced manager grade itself ready to hand over. NOT SUFFICIENT EITHER: instructing managers to run the check before releasing -- that is a reminder, and this repo has already paid twice for reminders standing in for mechanisms (MI-15).", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-21"}, "id": "BL-20260903-HANDOFF-CHECK-CANNOT-TELL-A-DELIBERATE-RELEASE-FROM-AN-EXPIRY-SO-A-CLEAN-HANDOVER-GRADES-FAIL", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "review", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json#BL-20260903-HANDOFF-CHECK-CANNOT-TELL-A-DELIBERATE-RELEASE-FROM-AN-EXPIRY-SO-A-CLEAN-HANDOVER-GRADES-FAIL", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='BL-20260903-HANDOFF-CHECK-CANNOT-TELL-A-DELIBERATE-RELEASE-FROM-AN-EXPIRY-SO-A-CLEAN-HANDOVER-GRADES-FAIL'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[health] handoff_check's lease check passes ONLY on held_by_me, so the outgoing manager's final act — releasing the lease — makes its own readiness verdict go red"}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 14, "clears_when": "R7's \"previous heartbeat\" is taken from the lease register's history REACHABLE FROM THE COMMIT (all parents), not from `sha~1`: the latest same-holder heartbeat strictly earlier than this commit's own. Evidence must be a plant AND a control in `check_manager_scope.py --self-test`: a manager merge commit whose first parent carries a stale lease blob must grade CLEAN when the register shows on-cadence heartbeats reachable through the second parent, and a genuine >TTL silence must still FAIL — the existing `210min since the previous heartbeat -> violation (R7 only)` case must keep passing unchanged. NOT SUFFICIENT: exempting merge commits from R7 altogether. A manager's merge commit legitimately advances the heartbeat, so exempting it would blind the duty check on the manager's most common commit shape — trading a false positive for a silent false negative, which is the worse of the two.", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-21"}, "id": "BL-20260904-MANAGER-SCOPE-R7-MEASURES-CADENCE-ALONG-THE-FIRST-PARENT-SO-A-MANAGER-MERGE-INTO-A-WORKER-BRANCH-READS-AS-A-LAPSE", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "review", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json#BL-20260904-MANAGER-SCOPE-R7-MEASURES-CADENCE-ALONG-THE-FIRST-PARENT-SO-A-MANAGER-MERGE-INTO-A-WORKER-BRANCH-READS-AS-A-LAPSE", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='BL-20260904-MANAGER-SCOPE-R7-MEASURES-CADENCE-ALONG-THE-FIRST-PARENT-SO-A-MANAGER-MERGE-INTO-A-WORKER-BRANCH-READS-AS-A-LAPSE'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[health] check_manager_scope R7 reads the previous heartbeat from `sha~1`, which on a merge commit is the WORKER branch's stale lease blob, so an on-cadence manager grades as silent for hours"}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 30, "clears_when": "TWO OBSERVABLE CONDITIONS, and they are different facts — say which you cleared rather than closing the row on the easier one.\n\n(1) THE DRAFT TRAP. A PR is observed reaching `merged: true` where ALL of: its branch carries a `.github/pr-landing/*.json` with `landing: \"self\"`; the PR was opened by the `claude-pr-automerge` workflow (NOT pr-opener); and NO human flipped its draft flag — verified by reading the PR's timeline for a `ready_for_review` event and finding none, never by asking whether anyone remembers clicking. Name the PR number. ⚠️ #11006 DOES NOT CLEAR THIS: it only exists because #10993 was closed and reopened by hand, which is the workaround, not the fix. What clears it is the NEXT web session opening a self-land PR and it landing with no rescue.\n\n(2) THE TITLE. A squash commit on `main` from such a PR is observed whose subject describes the CHANGE rather than whichever commit happened to be at HEAD when the relay first ran. Read `git log origin/main` for it. ⚠️ A PR that never received a second push cannot clear this — its title is trivially correct — so the qualifying observation is a PR that was pushed to AFTER the relay opened it.\n\n⚠️ NEITHER IS CLEARED BY A CODE CHANGE ALONE. Editing claude-pr-automerge.yml to refresh the title, or teaching check_pr_landing.py R6 to fail on a draft, is a DEPLOY — this repo's standing distinction — and the row stays open until a real PR is seen going through. ⚠️ AND A DECISION NOT TO FIX IT CLOSES THIS ROW TOO, recorded as a  …[truncated]", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-21"}, "id": "BL-20260904-A-TIER-1-SELF-LAND-PR-OPENED-BY-PR-OPENER-IS-A-DRAFT-AND-CAN-NEVER-SELF-LAND-FROM-A-WEB-SESSION", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "review", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json#BL-20260904-A-TIER-1-SELF-LAND-PR-OPENED-BY-PR-OPENER-IS-A-DRAFT-AND-CAN-NEVER-SELF-LAND-FROM-A-WEB-SESSION", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='BL-20260904-A-TIER-1-SELF-LAND-PR-OPENED-BY-PR-OPENER-IS-A-DRAFT-AND-CAN-NEVER-SELF-LAND-FROM-A-WEB-SESSION'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[health] a web session's Tier-1 self-land PR is opened as a DRAFT by pr-opener, and claude-pr-automerge refuses to un-draft a PR it did not open — so the PR is green, clean, declared self-land, and cannot land"}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 14, "clears_when": "An expired or released manager lease is OBSERVED being acted on by a mechanism rather than by the operator, within one bounded interval of the expiry, and the observation names WHICH lease expiry and WHAT acted. Either outcome counts: (a) `manager_lease.py status` read from origin/main shows a DIFFERENT holder with a heartbeat inside the TTL, and that successor's session id appears in SESSIONS.json or a commit trailer -- i.e. somebody actually took over; or (b) an operator-facing notification is recorded stating that management has stopped and nothing took over. NOT satisfied by: the lease file merely reading `expired` (that is the condition, not a response to it); a green test or self-test (a harness cannot reach the case); `manager_wake.py --assess` returning `no_manager` (that is this row's evidence, and the wake deliberately does nothing with it); or a manager happening to arrive and claim the lease for unrelated reasons with no mechanism attributable -- record WHICH mechanism acted, or the row stays open.", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-21"}, "id": "BL-20260904-NOTHING-REAPS-A-DEAD-MANAGER-OR-CLAIMS-AN-EXPIRED-LEASE", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "review", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json#BL-20260904-NOTHING-REAPS-A-DEAD-MANAGER-OR-CLAIMS-AN-EXPIRED-LEASE", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='BL-20260904-NOTHING-REAPS-A-DEAD-MANAGER-OR-CLAIMS-AN-EXPIRED-LEASE'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[health] an expired manager lease is claimable by anyone and claimed by nobody — nothing reads the expiry and nothing takes over, so a manager that DIES stops management indefinitely"}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 30, "clears_when": "EITHER check_manager_scope's MANAGER_SURFACE is widened to the other three backlogs with the widening argued in its docstring the way the health-review entry already is, OR CLAUDE.md's 'filing is permitted with no cap' is narrowed in writing to 'the health-review backlog only, and anything else is routed to a session'. What must NOT persist is the current state where the canonical doc says one thing and the guard enforces another, because a manager reading the doc will keep hitting the refusal and the cheapest way out is to mis-file. [!] A row filed in health-review-backlog ABOUT an ml-review subject does not satisfy this and is the failure mode, not the fix. [!] AND SEPARATELY: the MI-108 local-LLM row still needs filing in ml-review-backlog.json by a SESSION -- this row records the blocker, it does not carry the direction.", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-21"}, "id": "BL-20260904-MANAGER-MAY-FILE-TO-ONE-BACKLOG-OF-FOUR-SO-A-ROUTED-ITEM-CANNOT-BE-FILED-WHERE-IT-BELONGS", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "review", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json#BL-20260904-MANAGER-MAY-FILE-TO-ONE-BACKLOG-OF-FOUR-SO-A-ROUTED-ITEM-CANNOT-BE-FILED-WHERE-IT-BELONGS", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='BL-20260904-MANAGER-MAY-FILE-TO-ONE-BACKLOG-OF-FOUR-SO-A-ROUTED-ITEM-CANNOT-BE-FILED-WHERE-IT-BELONGS'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[health] check_manager_scope vouches for health-review-backlog only, so a manager routing an item to ml/performance/research backlog can never actually file it"}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 14, "clears_when": "EITHER the two on-demand tools write to docs/claude/pending-pings.jsonl like their siblings (and say, as work_phase_ping.py already does, that an uncommitted row is a ping that never happened), OR they detect that runtime_logs/ is gitignored-and-undrained in this environment and REFUSE with a message naming the tool that does work, rather than reporting 'queued'. [!] Reporting 'queued -- the bot drains within ~5s' when no bot exists is the specific behaviour to remove; a tool may not claim a delivery path it cannot see. [!] NOT SUFFICIENT: documenting which tool to use. Two tools that look right and silently do nothing is the defect, and a doc does not stop the next session reaching for the obvious one.", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-21"}, "id": "BL-20260904-BOTH-ON-DEMAND-PING-TOOLS-WRITE-TO-A-GITIGNORED-DIR-SO-A-SANDBOX-PING-IS-LOST", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "review", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json#BL-20260904-BOTH-ON-DEMAND-PING-TOOLS-WRITE-TO-A-GITIGNORED-DIR-SO-A-SANDBOX-PING-IS-LOST", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='BL-20260904-BOTH-ON-DEMAND-PING-TOOLS-WRITE-TO-A-GITIGNORED-DIR-SO-A-SANDBOX-PING-IS-LOST'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[health] send_ping.py and work_digest_now.py queue to a gitignored runtime dir, so a ping queued from a sandbox session never reaches the operator and never satisfies the F6 check"}
