{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 30, "clears_when": "MEASURED 2026-09-07 while building the MI-165 be_floor_r corpus: every `--source yfinance` call from a Claude Code web session fails with `Cookie fetch from fc.yahoo.com failed (SSLError)` then `curl: (35) Recv failure: Connection reset by peer`, and the lane's final line is `ERROR: no candles returned (source attempted: yfinance)`. The venue is NOT down and the symbol is NOT unmapped: Yahoo's own chart endpoint (query1.finance.yahoo.com/v8/finance/chart/<TICKER>) answers HTTP 200 with real bars from the same session using an ordinary User-Agent — verified for GDX (1926 daily bars, 2018-01-02 to 2025-08-29) before it was relied on. It is the yfinance LIBRARY's cookie/crumb handshake that the egress proxy resets, not the data fetch. This is diagnostic-provenance sub-class A (CLAUDE.md): the failure message names a cause — no candles from the venue — that no code path established, and a reader concludes the equity lane is unusable when only the auth dance is. MI-165 worked around it with a direct chart-endpoint reader and said so in its memo rather than silently substituting. RESOLVES WHEN: the yfinance lane either (a) distinguishes the COOKIE stage failure from a real empty-result in its message and exit path — the lane already has a YfFetchFailed/stage vocabulary to hang it on — or (b) falls back to the chart endpoint when the handshake is refused. Either is fine; what must not survive is a stage-1 auth failure rendering as 'the venue returned nothing'.", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-21"}, "id": "BL-20260907-YFINANCE-LANE-BLOCKED-BY-PROXY-COOKIE-HANDSHAKE", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "review", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json#BL-20260907-YFINANCE-LANE-BLOCKED-BY-PROXY-COOKIE-HANDSHAKE", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='BL-20260907-YFINANCE-LANE-BLOCKED-BY-PROXY-COOKIE-HANDSHAKE'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[health] fetch_backtest_candles.py --source yfinance fails at the COOKIE stage behind the session proxy and reports 'no candles returned', a message naming a fetch that never happened"}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 45, "clears_when": "MEASURED 2026-09-07 on branch claude/mi165-be-floor-sweep-20260907. The branch is Tier-1 by the canonical definition (research harnesses + a sweep script + tests + a memo; no src/, no config/, no order path, nothing armed), but three of its paths — scripts/backtest_trend.py, scripts/backtest_pullback.py, scripts/backtest_squeeze.py — are outside TIER1_SURFACE, which covers scripts/ci/**, scripts/ops/**, scripts/research/** and scripts/reports/** but not the backtest harnesses at scripts/ root. R5 therefore refuses `landing: \"self\"`, correctly and by design ('a path outside TIER1_SURFACE is not thereby dangerous; it is one the guard cannot certify'). THE GAP IS IN HOLD_REASONS, WHICH IS A CLOSED VOCABULARY OF FIVE AND NAMES NONE OF THIS CASE: changes_landing_machinery (verified false by R8), depends_on_unmerged_pr (false), awaiting_evidence (false), operator_asked_to_hold (the CLOSEST available and the one MI-165 used, but not what the hold is actually caused by — the commission explicitly PERMITTED landing the Tier-1 half), tier_2_3_needs_approval (this PR is Tier-1 and arms nothing). So the branch must declare a typed reason that does not name its real cause, and a typed verdict whose basis is stated only in free text is the weaker half of the discipline OPEN-PRS.json::operator_decision exists to enforce. MI-165 therefore put the true cause in hold_text and filed this row rather than leaving the mismatch unrecorded. RESOLVES WHEN either (a) HOLD_REASONS gains a term for it — …[truncated]", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-21"}, "id": "BL-20260907-PR-LANDING-HAS-NO-HOLD-REASON-FOR-TIER1-WORK-OUTSIDE-THE-CERTIFIABLE-SURFACE", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "review", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json#BL-20260907-PR-LANDING-HAS-NO-HOLD-REASON-FOR-TIER1-WORK-OUTSIDE-THE-CERTIFIABLE-SURFACE", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='BL-20260907-PR-LANDING-HAS-NO-HOLD-REASON-FOR-TIER1-WORK-OUTSIDE-THE-CERTIFIABLE-SURFACE'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[health] check_pr_landing R5 refuses self-land for scripts/backtest_*.py, but no HOLD_REASON names 'Tier-1 work the guard cannot certify', so the declaration must state a reason that is not quite true"}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 14, "clears_when": "MEASURED 2026-09-07 by MI-168 while re-scoping the IB close-confirmation (PR on branch mi-168-trade-scoped-close-confirmation; reasoning in docs/design/close-confirmation-scope-DESIGN.md section 6). execute.close_open_position receives a PER-TRADE qty and, on the alpaca branch, DISCARDS it: AlpacaClient.close(symbol) accepts no quantity at all (pinned by test_alpaca_close_signature_cannot_express_a_trade_scoped_close) and issues DELETE /v2/positions/{sym}, Alpaca's whole-symbol liquidation; _close_extended_hours likewise reads qty from the LIVE POSITION (pos.get('qty')), not from the caller's trade. So with two open journal rows on one symbol, closing trade A removes trade B's shares too and B's row stays open with no venue position behind it. This is the MIRROR of the IB defect MI-167 measured: IB falsely REFUSES a close that succeeded; Alpaca silently OVER-CLOSES. Pinned as current behaviour by test_alpaca_close_of_one_trade_liquidates_its_sibling_too in tests/test_close_confirm_trade_scope.py. !! DO NOT 'FIX' THIS BY RE-SCOPING THE CONFIRMATION, which is what MI-167's proposed fix asked for at alpaca_client.py:842 and :1049. The confirmation is symbol-scoped and so is the OPERATION, so the two MATCH and Alpaca is NOT exposed to the false refusal (positive control: test_alpaca_close_is_not_exposed_to_the_false_refusal passes both before and after MI-168). Confirming a trade-sized reduction while the venue liquidated everything would journal one trade closed and leave its si …[truncated]", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-21"}, "id": "BL-20260907-ALPACA-CLOSE-OF-ONE-TRADE-LIQUIDATES-ITS-SIBLINGS", "next_action": "ask_operator", "origin": {"kind": "review", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json#BL-20260907-ALPACA-CLOSE-OF-ONE-TRADE-LIQUIDATES-ITS-SIBLINGS", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='BL-20260907-ALPACA-CLOSE-OF-ONE-TRADE-LIQUIDATES-ITS-SIBLINGS'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[health] AlpacaClient.close takes no qty and flattens the WHOLE symbol, so closing one journal trade silently liquidates every sibling trade's shares while their rows stay open with nothing behind them"}
