{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 45, "clears_when": "NOTICED 2026-09-07 by MI-168 while re-scoping the close-confirmation in the same method; FILED, NOT FIXED, deliberately — MI-168's scope was the confirmation, and changing the clamp changes ORDER SIZING, which is a materially riskier Tier-3 edit than changing what counts as confirmation. src/units/accounts/ib_client.py Step 0 reads live_qty = self._live_position_qty(sym) — the SYMBOL total — and sets close_qty = min(requested_qty, live_qty). That is the same scope confusion as the confirmation gate, one step earlier in the same method. !! IT IS MUCH LESS SEVERE THAN THE CONFIRMATION BUG AND THAT SHOULD NOT BE OVERSTATED: it is a SAFETY clamp and it errs toward UNDER-closing (never transmitting more than the venue holds, which on a one-way futures account would open a reverse position), so it fails in the safe direction. The residual concern is a case nobody has observed: if the symbol total is BELOW this trade's qty because a sibling's lots are gone, the clamp closes the symbol's remainder rather than this trade's lots, which can close another trade's position. NO INSTANCE HAS BEEN MEASURED — this is read from the code, not from a journal, and is filed so the reasoning is not lost rather than because it has bitten. RESOLVES WHEN: either the clamp is re-derived from the trade's own lots with the symbol total kept purely as an upper bound (so it can never exceed the venue AND can never silently retarget another trade's position), or it is established that the current behaviour  …[truncated]", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-21"}, "id": "BL-20260907-IB-CLOSE-QTY-CLAMP-IS-SYMBOL-SCOPED-LIKE-THE-CONFIRMATION-WAS", "next_action": "ask_operator", "origin": {"kind": "review", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json#BL-20260907-IB-CLOSE-QTY-CLAMP-IS-SYMBOL-SCOPED-LIKE-THE-CONFIRMATION-WAS", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-