{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 30, "clears_when": "A route-level test exists for EVERY /api/diag/* route whose accessor takes keyword-only parameters, asserting the accessor receives those parameters through a run_account_read stub that accepts POSITIONAL ARGS ONLY -- i.e. a stub that reproduces loop.run_in_executor's contract, so a kwargs regression raises in the test exactly as it does live. Verified by a POSITIVE CONTROL: revert the functools.partial in one such route and observe the test FAIL; a suite that stays green under that revert has not been fixed, it has been re-verified as blind. NOT SUFFICIENT: accessor-level tests (they cannot reach the seam by construction, which is the finding); a green `guards` or `pytest-run` (both were green while the route raised on every request); or fixing this one route (tests/test_diag_closed_pnl_route_wiring.py covers it, and the class remains open for every other route with a keyword-only accessor).", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-21"}, "id": "BL-20260909-A-DIAG-ROUTE-PASSED-KWARGS-THROUGH-A-POSITIONAL-ONLY-EXECUTOR-HOP-AND-ONLY-A-LIVE-REQUEST-COULD-CATCH-IT", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "review", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json#BL-20260909-A-DIAG-ROUTE-PASSED-KWARGS-THROUGH-A-POSITIONAL-ONLY-EXECUTOR-HOP-AND-ONLY-A-LIVE-REQUEST-COULD-CATCH-IT", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='BL-20260909-A-DIAG-ROUTE-PASSED-KWARGS-THROUGH-A-POSITIONAL-ONLY-EXECUTOR-HOP-AND-ONLY-A-LIVE-REQUEST-COULD-CATCH-IT'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[health] An accessor-level test suite cannot reach a diag route's executor-hop wiring, so a route that raises TypeError on EVERY request shipped green"}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 30, "clears_when": "A Bybit position row is interpretable ALONE: it carries a leg-derived protection read beside the position-level fields -- covered qty per side and a state that distinguishes protected_on_position / protected_on_legs / unprotected / could_not_look -- verified by reading a LIVE Partial-mode position off /api/diag and seeing a non-null protection state while stopLoss stays null. [!] A test fixture does NOT clear this; the whole finding is about what the venue returns. [!] AND IF THE DECISION IS TO LEAVE THE ROW AS IS because its one caller always ships the legs, that CLEARS this row only as a RECORDED decision naming the invariant a future second caller would have to preserve -- not by the row going quiet.", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-21"}, "id": "BL-20260909-A-BYBIT-POSITION-ROW-CANNOT-STATE-ITS-OWN-PROTECTION-UNDER-PARTIAL-MODE", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "review", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json#BL-20260909-A-BYBIT-POSITION-ROW-CANNOT-STATE-ITS-OWN-PROTECTION-UNDER-PARTIAL-MODE", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='BL-20260909-A-BYBIT-POSITION-ROW-CANNOT-STATE-ITS-OWN-PROTECTION-UNDER-PARTIAL-MODE'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[health] Under BYBIT_TPSL_MODE=partial a Bybit position row emits stop_loss/take_profit/tpsl_mode ALL null while fully protected, so the row alone cannot distinguish 'unprotected' from 'protected on the legs'"}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 14, "clears_when": "EITHER (a) claude-pr-automerge FAILS LOUDLY (non-zero, named check) rather than exiting 0 when it cannot arm because the PR is a draft -- verified by a run log on a deliberately drafted PR showing the red; OR (b) it un-drafts a PR it ADOPTED (adoption is consent; it already correctly refuses to un-draft one it did not open), verified on a real PR; OR (c) CLAUDE.md's remedy paragraph states that the PR must be opened NON-DRAFT for the arming file to take, AND a session is observed following it. [!] (c) alone is a prose fix for a class this repo has twice paid for as prose -- prefer (a), which is the board-post.yml posture of refusing to report a post it did not make. [!] A GREEN SELF-TEST CLEARS NOTHING: the failure is that a real workflow exits 0 having armed nothing.", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-21"}, "id": "BL-20260909-OPENING-A-PR-AS-A-DRAFT-SILENTLY-DEFEATS-THE-ARMING-FILE-AND-MARKING-IT-READY-AFTERWARDS-DOES-NOT-ARM-IT", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "review", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json#BL-20260909-OPENING-A-PR-AS-A-DRAFT-SILENTLY-DEFEATS-THE-ARMING-FILE-AND-MARKING-IT-READY-AFTERWARDS-DOES-NOT-ARM-IT", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='BL-20260909-OPENING-A-PR-AS-A-DRAFT-SILENTLY-DEFEATS-THE-ARMING-FILE-AND-MARKING-IT-READY-AFTERWARDS-DOES-NOT-ARM-IT'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[health] RECURRENCE of BL-20260904 through CLAUDE.md's OWN newly-documented remedy: open-the-PR-yourself + create-as-draft leaves auto-merge unarmed, and marking it ready afterwards does not retroactively arm it"}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 30, "clears_when": "BOTH, and they are different facts — say which you cleared. (a) The input count is brought to <= 10 (drop one, or fold two into a single CSV input — the file already uses CSV inputs for `roster` and `arms`, so the pattern exists) AND a push to a `claude/**` branch is OBSERVED producing NO ranking-key-ab run, or a run that is not a zero-duration failure. Read the run history, never the workflow file. (b) A `workflow_dispatch` run of ranking-key-ab.yml is OBSERVED reaching a non-startup conclusion — i.e. the experiment can actually be run. ⚠️ (a) WITHOUT (b) IS THE TRAP: silencing the push noise while leaving the A/B undispatchable fixes the symptom nobody was paying for and leaves the capability dead. ⚠️ A GREEN YAML LINT CLEARS NOTHING — the file parses fine as YAML today (verified with yaml.safe_load); the violation is a GitHub-specific schema limit no YAML parser knows about. ⚠️ AND IF THE OWNER DECIDES the experiment is retired, this row does NOT clear — it CHANGES: the workflow should then be DELETED rather than left emitting a startup failure on every push, and that must be RECORDED as a decision rather than left looking like a fix about to happen.", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-21"}, "id": "BL-20260909-RANKING-KEY-AB-HAS-11-DISPATCH-INPUTS-AGAINST-A-LIMIT-OF-10-SO-IT-IS-A-STARTUP-FAILURE-ON-EVERY-PUSH-AND-CANNOT-BE-DISPATCHED-AT-ALL", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "review", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json#BL-20260909-RANKING-KEY-AB-HAS-11-DISPATCH-INPUTS-AGAINST-A-LIMIT-OF-10-SO-IT-IS-A-STARTUP-FAILURE-ON-EVERY-PUSH-AND-CANNOT-BE-DISPATCHED-AT-ALL", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='BL-20260909-RANKING-KEY-AB-HAS-11-DISPATCH-INPUTS-AGAINST-A-LIMIT-OF-10-SO-IT-IS-A-STARTUP-FAILURE-ON-EVERY-PUSH-AND-CANNOT-BE-DISPATCHED-AT-ALL'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[health] ranking-key-ab.yml declares 11 workflow_dispatch inputs against GitHub's hard limit of 10, so it startup-fails on EVERY push to claude/** and its workflow_dispatch is dead — the A/B it exists to run has never been runnable"}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 30, "clears_when": "The manager-cadence Routine's prompt no longer instructs the fire_trigger session_id comparison as the delivery signal, AND states the get_session IDLE->RUNNING check together with its own limit -- that absence of a transition is NOT evidence of non-delivery. Verified by reading the Routine's prompt back through list_triggers or update_trigger and quoting the new text. !! IT DOES NOT CLEAR ON A SINGLE SUCCESSFUL POKE: that is already filed as BL-20260902-AN-IDLE-CLOUD-SUB-SESSION-IS-RESUMABLE-VIA-A-SESSION-BOUND-ROUTINE-NOT-UNREACHABLE and proves the CHANNEL, never the CHECK. !! IT DOES NOT CLEAR BY DELETING THE CHECK with nothing in its place -- a manager with no delivery signal at all is the state that produced the MI-37 double-respawn. !! AND IT DOES NOT CLEAR ON A CI GUARD: none is possible, because the Routine prompt is not a repo file.", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-21"}, "id": "BL-20260910-THE-DOCUMENTED-POKE-VERIFICATION-CHECK-FAILS-EVERY-SUCCESSFUL-POKE", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "review", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json#BL-20260910-THE-DOCUMENTED-POKE-VERIFICATION-CHECK-FAILS-EVERY-SUCCESSFUL-POKE", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='BL-20260910-THE-DOCUMENTED-POKE-VERIFICATION-CHECK-FAILS-EVERY-SUCCESSFUL-POKE'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[health] The manager cadence's own poke-verification check -- compare fire_trigger's returned session_id against persistent_session_id -- returns a MISMATCH on every call including ones that demonstrably delivered, so following it reports 100% of pokes as failed"}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 30, "clears_when": "A manager checking mergeability CANNOT be misled by the armed driver, and it is OBSERVED not being misled. Either (a) the local check itself is made honest — a helper the merge-queue duty actually calls (not prose) that either merges with the drivers DISABLED (`git -c merge.jsonregister.driver=false merge`, or `-c core.attributesFile=/dev/null`) or reports both results side by side and labels which one predicts GitHub; or (b) the duty stops test-merging locally to answer this question at all and reads `mergeable_state` from the API first, with that written into the merge-queue contract rather than left to judgement. VERIFY BY REPRODUCING THE FALSE NEGATIVE AND WATCHING IT NOT HAPPEN: construct two branches that each append a row to the tail of a `merge=jsonregister` register, and confirm the check reports CONFLICT (or 'GitHub will conflict') in an ARMED clone. A test asserting the helper's behaviour on a synthetic repo is NECESSARY BUT NOT SUFFICIENT — the defect only appears when the driver is armed AND .gitattributes maps the path, so the check must exercise that shape. DO NOT 'fix' this by disarming the driver or removing the .gitattributes mapping: that trades this false negative for the eleven hand-resolutions BL-20260906-REGISTER-MERGE-DRIVER-SHIPS-UNARMED-AND-A-SESSION-PAID-ELEVEN-HAND-RESOLUTIONS records, which is a worse trade. The driver is doing its job; what is missing is a way to ask the OTHER question. AND MORE PROSE IN .gitattributes DOES NOT CLEAR THIS — the w …[truncated]", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-21"}, "id": "BL-20260910-AN-ARMED-CLONES-LOCAL-TEST-MERGE-IS-A-FALSE-NEGATIVE-ON-GITHUB-MERGEABILITY", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "review", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json#BL-20260910-AN-ARMED-CLONES-LOCAL-TEST-MERGE-IS-A-FALSE-NEGATIVE-ON-GITHUB-MERGEABILITY", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='BL-20260910-AN-ARMED-CLONES-LOCAL-TEST-MERGE-IS-A-FALSE-NEGATIVE-ON-GITHUB-MERGEABILITY'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[health] With merge.jsonregister armed, a local test-merge of a register-touching PR reports a clean merge for a PR GitHub grades `dirty` — the manager's own pre-merge check silently inverts"}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 14, "clears_when": "1. THE OPERATOR ANSWERS DEC-20260910-EXIT-EVAL-60S-REMEDY on WO-20260910-ROOT-CAUSE-THE-EXIT-EVAL-60S-BREACHES. The cause is established; what is owed is a Tier-2 decision, not more measurement. !! DO NOT RE-MEASURE TO ADVANCE THIS ROW -- the population is a complete census and re-reading the same route adds nothing. 2. IF R2 IS ARMED: a pass is OBSERVED inside a 04:00-05:00Z IBKR reset window whose pass_ms is bounded near one queue timeout (~35s) rather than n x 29s, read off /api/bot/exit-interval/soak. !! A GREEN TEST CLEARS NOTHING -- a harness cannot reach an IBKR reset window, which is the entire condition. !! AND A QUIET WINDOW CLEARS NOTHING EITHER: the denominator is IB packages open during the window, so establish that at least three IB-routed packages were open before reading a clean pass as evidence. 3. IF THE OPERATOR ARMS NOTHING: record that as a DECISION with its consequence stated, rather than leaving the row looking like a fix about to happen.", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-21"}, "id": "BL-20260910-EXIT-EVAL-60S-BREACHES-DID-NOT-CEASE-1216-LIFETIME-MAX-120S-AND-EVERY-PRIOR-READ-WAS-A-TAIL", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "review", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json#BL-20260910-EXIT-EVAL-60S-BREACHES-DID-NOT-CEASE-1216-LIFETIME-MAX-120S-AND-EVERY-PRIOR-READ-WAS-A-TAIL", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='BL-20260910-EXIT-EVAL-60S-BREACHES-DID-NOT-CEASE-1216-LIFETIME-MAX-120S-AND-EVERY-PRIOR-READ-WAS-A-TAIL'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[health] RECURRENCE of BL-20260825: the 60s exit-eval requirement did NOT stop being breached -- 1216 breaches / 67444 intervals lifetime (1.803%), max 120189.1ms = 2.0x the requirement, last breach 2026-09-10T00:30:58Z -- and every figure the repo has quoted came from a 1000-row TAIL that reads ZERO"}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 14, "clears_when": "1. THE OPERATOR ANSWERS DEC-20260910-EXIT-EVAL-60S-REMEDY. The cause is established; what is owed is a Tier-2 decision. !! DO NOT RE-MEASURE -- the 444 boundaries are a COMPLETE census (ungradeable 0, overlapping 0, unattributed_rows 0), not a sample. 2. IF R1 IS ARMED: the boot_reconcile -> DECOUPLED segment is OBSERVED at ~0s in a /api/diag/journalctl restart window, AND the post-arm restart-gap distribution is re-read off summary.restart_gap over a population of at least ~100 boundaries. !! A SMALLER SAMPLE CANNOT SETTLE IT -- that is exactly the error OI-20260910 made at n=11, and R1's predicted effect (~3s median) is smaller than the day-to-day noise at low n. 3. THE TWO OUTLIERS ARE NOT COVERED BY THIS CAUSE and must not be closed on it: 210.4s (2026-08-26) and 112.6s (2026-09-07) exceed what A+B+C+D can produce, and the journal no longer retains back to either date. Either a THIRD large gap is caught while the journal still holds it and decomposed, or it is recorded that the class is unexplained at 2 of 325. 4. IF THE OPERATOR DECLARES THE RESTART BOUNDARY OUT OF SCOPE for the 60s promise, this row does NOT close silently -- it CHANGES: that is a legitimate decision and must be RECORDED as one with its consequence stated (756 s/day in which no open position's exit is evaluated).", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-21"}, "id": "BL-20260910-RESTART-GAP-BREACHES-THE-60S-REQUIREMENT-ON-17-PERCENT-OF-RESTARTS-MAX-210S", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "review", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json#BL-20260910-RESTART-GAP-BREACHES-THE-60S-REQUIREMENT-ON-17-PERCENT-OF-RESTARTS-MAX-210S", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='BL-20260910-RESTART-GAP-BREACHES-THE-60S-REQUIREMENT-ON-17-PERCENT-OF-RESTARTS-MAX-210S'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[health] The RESTART GAP breaches the 60s exit-eval requirement on 77 of 442 restarts (17.4%), max 210.4s = 3.5x -- ~10x the within-process breach rate, and it was invisible until MI-230 built the grader"}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 30, "clears_when": "R14 (and R5, which shares `changed_paths`) DISTINGUISHES an empty changed-set from a surveyed-and-clean one, and is OBSERVED doing so: running `check_pr_landing.py` on a tree with uncommitted changes and an empty committed diff must report something like `no changed paths — nothing to grade (did you forget to commit?)` and must NOT emit the 'every changed path IS inside TIER1_SURFACE … declare landing: self and arm it' remedy. ⚠️ VERIFY BY RUNNING IT ON AN UNCOMMITTED TREE, not by reading the diff — this defect is invisible to a committed-tree run, which is exactly why it survived. ⚠️ A test asserting the new message on a synthetic empty list is NECESSARY BUT NOT SUFFICIENT: the failure only appears when a real working tree has changes the guard cannot see, so the check must exercise that shape. ⚠️ AND DO NOT 'fix' this by making changed_paths fall back to including uncommitted work — that would make the guard grade something CI never grades, which is a different and worse wrongness; the fix is to report the absence, not to invent a denominator.", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-21"}, "id": "BL-20260910-PR-LANDING-R14-FIRES-VACUOUSLY-ON-AN-EMPTY-DIFF-AND-TELLS-YOU-TO-SELF-LAND", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "review", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json#BL-20260910-PR-LANDING-R14-FIRES-VACUOUSLY-ON-AN-EMPTY-DIFF-AND-TELLS-YOU-TO-SELF-LAND", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='BL-20260910-PR-LANDING-R14-FIRES-VACUOUSLY-ON-AN-EMPTY-DIFF-AND-TELLS-YOU-TO-SELF-LAND'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[health] pr-landing-guard R14 reports 'every changed path IS inside TIER1_SURFACE' over ZERO paths on an uncommitted tree, and its remedy text tells you to declare landing: self and arm it"}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 30, "clears_when": "1. DECIDE RENAME-VS-ADD FIRST, and note this is a SCHEMA CHANGE with existing readers -- exit_restart_gap.py (grouping key), exit_interval_soak.py (builder + summary), src/web/api/routers/exit_interval.py, and any session reading the raw JSONL. A blind rename breaks the grouping; the cheaper fix is to keep the existing key as the process IDENTITY it actually is (consider `process_key` / `first_pass_at_utc`) and, if boot time is genuinely wanted, ADD a real one captured at module import or from /proc/self/stat rather than repurposing this. 2. THE DOCSTRING IS PART OF THE FIX, not an afterthought: exit_interval_soak.py's module docstring dates five processes from these values and must be corrected in the same change, or the stale claim outlives the field. 3. !! DO NOT BACK-FILL EXISTING ROWS. Every row already on disk carries the first-pass-completion value; rewriting them to a reconstructed boot time would assert an observation nobody made. Distinguish by row date. 4. !! A GREEN TEST CLEARS NOTHING HERE -- the field is internally consistent and always has been; the defect is that its NAME describes a different quantity. What clears it is the field (or its replacement) read back off /api/bot/exit-interval/soak and shown to differ from the first-pass completion by the startup interval, cross-checked against a systemd 'Started ICT Trader Live' line in /api/diag/journalctl for the SAME restart.", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-21"}, "id": "BL-20260910-PROCESS-STARTED-UTC-IS-THE-FIRST-PASS-COMPLETION-NOT-THE-PROCESS-START", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "review", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json#BL-20260910-PROCESS-STARTED-UTC-IS-THE-FIRST-PASS-COMPLETION-NOT-THE-PROCESS-START", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='BL-20260910-PROCESS-STARTED-UTC-IS-THE-FIRST-PASS-COMPLETION-NOT-THE-PROCESS-START'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[health] exit_loop_health's `process_started_utc` is the FIRST PASS'S COMPLETION time, not when the process started - it is late by exactly the boot-plus-cold-pass interval (23.2s measured), and the soak's own docstring dates processes from it"}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 30, "clears_when": "Either (a) the reachable-set claim is re-derived on demand rather than recorded as a constant -- a script or workflow that runs RiskManager.position_size over the current roster at the current balance and current prices, whose output is the thing sessions read -- or (b) the two prose copies carry an explicit as-of date and a statement that reachability decays with price, so a reader knows to re-measure. !! (b) ALONE IS WEAKER AND MUST BE RECORDED AS THE CHEAPER CHOICE IF TAKEN: a dated claim still goes stale, it merely says so. !! DO NOT close this by editing the GDX line to say 'refused' -- that is the same defect with a different value, and it would be stale again on the next meaningful move in GDX. The claim under repair is that the set is CONSTANT, not the value of any one entry.", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-21"}, "id": "BL-20260910-THE-ALPACA-LIVE-REACHABLE-SYMBOL-SET-IS-RECORDED-AS-A-CONSTANT-AND-GDX-HAS-SINCE-FALLEN-OUT-OF-IT", "next_action": "ask_operator", "origin": {"kind": "review", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json#BL-20260910-THE-ALPACA-LIVE-REACHABLE-SYMBOL-SET-IS-RECORDED-AS-A-CONSTANT-AND-GDX-HAS-SINCE-FALLEN-OUT-OF-IT", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='BL-20260910-THE-ALPACA-LIVE-REACHABLE-SYMBOL-SET-IS-RECORDED-AS-A-CONSTANT-AND-GDX-HAS-SINCE-FALLEN-OUT-OF-IT'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[health] config/accounts.yaml and the 2026-08-25 affordability sweep both record alpaca_live's reachable set as 'TLT IEF SLV USO IAUM GDX' as though it were a constant -- GDX no longer sizes, and the drift is in the permissive direction"}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 30, "clears_when": "EITHER (a) a new hold_reason exists for this condition -- e.g. `self_land_would_churn_a_shared_register` -- so the remedy MI-204 documented can be declared truthfully, OR (b) R13 stops requiring the claim to live in the DIFF, which removes the conflict source and makes the hold unnecessary. !! (b) IS THE STRUCTURAL FIX AND IS NOT OBVIOUSLY RIGHT: check_pr_landing.py's own docstring says R13 records an ATTRIBUTABLE claim and 'does NOT serialize' (a committed claim reaches no other session until merge, BL-20260810-MERGE-SLOT-MIRROR-UNWRITABLE-PRE-MERGE). If it serializes nothing, what the in-diff requirement buys is attribution alone -- and it is worth measuring whether that is worth this much churn before changing it. Decide, do not just widen. !! DO NOT CLOSE THIS BY ADDING A CATCH-ALL `other`: R7's whole point is a closed vocabulary, and an `other` bucket restores the adjective it was written to forbid. !! AND A GREEN GUARD RUN CLEARS NOTHING -- the guard passes today, on a declaration that is either false or self-landing into the loop. The condition is about which honest options exist.", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-21"}, "id": "BL-20260910-PR-LANDING-HOLD-REASONS-HAVE-NO-TERM-FOR-SELF-LANDING-WOULD-CHURN-A-SHARED-REGISTER", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "review", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json#BL-20260910-PR-LANDING-HOLD-REASONS-HAVE-NO-TERM-FOR-SELF-LANDING-WOULD-CHURN-A-SHARED-REGISTER", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='BL-20260910-PR-LANDING-HOLD-REASONS-HAVE-NO-TERM-FOR-SELF-LANDING-WOULD-CHURN-A-SHARED-REGISTER'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[health] check_pr_landing.py's closed hold_reason 