{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 30, "clears_when": "TWO PARTS, and the second is the one that actually protects a session. (1) The measurement is re-run and either confirms or refutes the monotonic fall-off on a later window (a second population, not the same one re-read). (2) THE CONSEQUENCE IS ABSORBED WHERE IT BITES: any clears_when clause or guard that grades a cadence against its DECLARED slot is rewritten to grade against the observed distribution, or to grade only that the cadence DELIVERED at all. ⚠️ DO NOT 'FIX' THIS BY TIGHTENING CRONS — declaring `*/30` to get hourly delivery is cargo-culting against an unverified cause and would make the shortfall worse if throttling is per-repo. ⚠️ AND IF THE OPERATOR DECIDES the sub-6-hourly cadences should move onto the VM's own timer (as the hourly digest already proposes), this does NOT clear — it CHANGES, and must be recorded as that decision with the consequence stated.", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-21"}, "id": "BL-20260911-NOTHING-DECLARED-MORE-OFTEN-THAN-EVERY-SIX-HOURS-DELIVERS-ITS-DECLARED-RATE-AND-EVERY-DAILY-CRON-FIRES-FOUR-TO-FIVE-HOURS-LATE", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "review", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json#BL-20260911-NOTHING-DECLARED-MORE-OFTEN-THAN-EVERY-SIX-HOURS-DELIVERS-ITS-DECLARED-RATE-AND-EVERY-DAILY-CRON-FIRES-FOUR-TO-FIVE-HOURS-LATE", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='BL-20260911-NOTHING-DECLARED-MORE-OFTEN-THAN-EVERY-SIX-HOURS-DELIVERS-ITS-DECLARED-RATE-AND-EVERY-DAILY-CRON-FIRES-FOUR-TO-FIVE-HOURS-LATE'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[health] Measured across all 27 scheduled workflows: delivery falls off monotonically with declared frequency (hourly 25%, 2-hourly 37-48%, 3-hourly 62%, 6-hourly 91-100%, daily 100%), and the median cron fires 4-5 HOURS after its declared slot — so no clears_when clause may read a declared minute as a firing minute"}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 14, "clears_when": "TWO PARTS, different facts — say WHICH was cleared.\n\n(1) THE MEASUREMENT GAP IS CLOSED FIRST, because it is what let this go unremarked: the LIVE surface must not be able to read `within` while the durable record reads `breached`. Either the live grade carries the durable max beside its own, or requirement_state is computed from the soak rather than from per-process memory. ⚠️ This half is CHEAP and is the one that stops recurrence — the per-process reset is not a bug in itself (CLAUDE.md is right that max_interval_ms is legitimately per-process), but publishing it as THE requirement verdict is.\n\n(2) THE BREACH RATE ITSELF comes down, measured over a NEW population of comparable size — not the same rows re-read. Report in-process breach % and restart-gap breach % separately; they have different causes (tail latency vs cold-start) and a fix for one will not touch the other.\n\n⚠️ A QUIET WINDOW CLEARS NOTHING: 1.69% means ~1 breach per 59 intervals, so a short window will show zero by arithmetic. State the interval count. ⚠️ AND A GREEN requirement_state ON A FRESH PROCESS IS THE EXACT READING THAT IS ALREADY WRONG — do not clear on it. ⚠️ IF THE OPERATOR DECIDES 60s is the wrong requirement, this does NOT clear — it CHANGES, and the new bound must be recorded as a decision with the measured distribution beside it.", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-21"}, "id": "BL-20260911-THE-60-SECOND-EXIT-EVALUATION-REQUIREMENT-IS-BREACHED-ON-THE-DURABLE-RECORD-WHILE-THE-PER-PROCESS-INSTRUMENT-READS-WITHIN", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "review", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json#BL-20260911-THE-60-SECOND-EXIT-EVALUATION-REQUIREMENT-IS-BREACHED-ON-THE-DURABLE-RECORD-WHILE-THE-PER-PROCESS-INSTRUMENT-READS-WITHIN", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='BL-20260911-THE-60-SECOND-EXIT-EVALUATION-REQUIREMENT-IS-BREACHED-ON-THE-DURABLE-RECORD-WHILE-THE-PER-PROCESS-INSTRUMENT-READS-WITHIN'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[health] M20's 60s exit-evaluation requirement is BREACHED — 1,219 of 72,148 intervals (1.69%, max 2.00x) and 77 of 462 restart gaps (16.7%, max 3.51x) — while the live per-process grade reads `within` because one 95-minute process did not draw the tail"}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 14, "clears_when": "TWO PARTS. (1) The transient_market_data_unavailable volume is reduced at source OR aggregated — a repeated fetch failure on one (symbol, timeframe) should roll up with a count rather than emitting one row per attempt, the same latching discipline this repo already applies to target_naked and stop_over_cover. ⚠️ DO NOT FIX THIS BY SUPPRESSING THE CLASS: MGC 15m failing 274 times on the frame driving a live 35-lot position is a real signal, and silencing it would be strictly worse than the occlusion. (2) Establish whether the MGC fetch failures have a single cause (an IB pacing/session condition on that contract) and whether exit evaluation was actually degraded for that position — which needs the monitor-blindness record over an UNtruncated window.\n\nCLEARS WHEN: the warn feed's top class is under ~25% of rows AND the reviewable window extends materially past 5.2 days, measured the same way. ⚠️ A QUIET MARKET-DATA WEEK CLEARS NOTHING — establish that fetches were still being attempted before reading a drop in failures as a fix.", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-21"}, "id": "BL-20260911-78-PERCENT-OF-THE-WARN-FEED-IS-ONE-MARKET-DATA-CLASS-AND-IT-TRUNCATES-THE-REVIEWABLE-WINDOW-TO-FIVE-DAYS", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "review", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json#BL-20260911-78-PERCENT-OF-THE-WARN-FEED-IS-ONE-MARKET-DATA-CLASS-AND-IT-TRUNCATES-THE-REVIEWABLE-WINDOW-TO-FIVE-DAYS", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='BL-20260911-78-PERCENT-OF-THE-WARN-FEED-IS-ONE-MARKET-DATA-CLASS-AND-IT-TRUNCATES-THE-REVIEWABLE-WINDOW-TO-FIVE-DAYS'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[health] 781 of 1000 WARN+ rows (78.1%) are transient_market_data_unavailable — MGC alone is 403 (40.3%), on the 15m frame driving an open 35-lot position's exit evaluation — and the volume caps the reviewable warn window at 5.2 days"}
