{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 14, "clears_when": "A consumer of /api/bot/trades/closed or /api/bot/performance that is told an account is `known_divergent` is told IN THE SAME RESPONSE when that verdict was established (`as_of`) and how large the divergence is (`realized_usd`), without a second query — i.e. the two fields `journal_trust_for` ALREADY computes reach the wire instead of being dropped at the extraction site. Mechanically checkable: a live GET for an account with a ledger record returns a non-null date beside the state, verified by calling the route rather than by reading the diff. ⚠️ It is NOT resolved by adding a fourth trust state or a staleness threshold — no threshold has been decided and inventing one would be a fabricated denominator; the ask is to surface the date the producer already has. ⚠️ And it is NOT resolved by refreshing the ledger: a fresh export makes today's reading current and leaves the next reader with the same blind spot.", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-21"}, "id": "BL-20260912-JOURNALTRUST-DROPS-THE-LEDGERS-AS-OF-AND-MAGNITUDE-AT-BOTH-WIRE-SURFACES-SO-A-61-DAY-OLD-RECONCILIATION-READS-AS-A-FRESH-ONE", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "review", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json#BL-20260912-JOURNALTRUST-DROPS-THE-LEDGERS-AS-OF-AND-MAGNITUDE-AT-BOTH-WIRE-SURFACES-SO-A-61-DAY-OLD-RECONCILIATION-READS-AS-A-FRESH-ONE", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='BL-20260912-JOURNALTRUST-DROPS-THE-LEDGERS-AS-OF-AND-MAGNITUDE-AT-BOTH-WIRE-SURFACES-SO-A-61-DAY-OLD-RECONCILIATION-READS-AS-A-FRESH-ONE'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[health] `journal_trust_for` computes `as_of` and `realized_usd` and BOTH read surfaces discard them, so a 61-day-old reconciliation renders byte-identically to one taken today"}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 14, "clears_when": "TWO things, and they are different — say WHICH was done. (1) THE CONTRADICTION IS SETTLED IN THE FILE: `occurrences` and `residual_risk` no longer disagree about whether the escalation trigger has fired, and if the answer is 'it has', the row SAYS SO rather than being quietly reworded to condition on an 11th. Mechanically checkable: the trigger sentence names a number strictly greater than `occurrences`, or names no number and states the condition as met. (2) THE ESCALATION IS EITHER BUILT OR DECLINED ON THE RECORD. Built = a diff-scoped CI guard over analysis scripts in the canonical-db-resolver / diagnostic-provenance family, registered in scripts/ci/run_guards.py, whose findings are CENSUSED first so a residue cannot sit unmeasured (the diagnostic-provenance-guard precedent, where a diff-scoped-only guard let 52 findings sit unchanged for 26 days). Declined = an operator decision recorded as a decision, with the reason, not left looking like work about to start. ⚠️ NOT resolved by a stronger adjective on the ledger row — the row forbids that in its own words. ⚠️ NOT resolved by a green `--self-test`: CI already runs both self-tests and they passed throughout every one of the ten occurrences, because they grade whether the LOOKUPS work, not whether anyone ran them. ⚠️ A count of instances since 2026-08-26 is WELCOME evidence but is not sufficient on its own, and must carry its classifier and a positive control if it is offered.", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-21"}, "id": "BL-20260912-THE-RECURRENCE-LEDGERS-LARGEST-CLASS-STANDS-AT-ITS-OWN-ESCALATION-TRIGGER-AND-THE-ESCALATION-WAS-NEVER-BUILT", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "review", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json#BL-20260912-THE-RECURRENCE-LEDGERS-LARGEST-CLASS-STANDS-AT-ITS-OWN-ESCALATION-TRIGGER-AND-THE-ESCALATION-WAS-NEVER-BUILT", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='BL-20260912-THE-RECURRENCE-LEDGERS-LARGEST-CLASS-STANDS-AT-ITS-OWN-ESCALATION-TRIGGER-AND-THE-ESCALATION-WAS-NEVER-BUILT'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[health] RC-STORED-FIELD-READ-AS-ITS-NAME records `occurrences: 10` while conditioning its escalation on \"if a 10th instance occurs\" — the trigger is met or off by one, the guard it names does not exist, and CI only self-tests the two lookups rather than requiring anyone to run them"}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 14, "clears_when": "A bybit_2 position_read_state_soak row is OBSERVED in which EVERY configured symbol is accounted for — either returned by the settleCoin page with all its books, or cross-checked by a symbol-scoped query — with a state that distinguishes 'queried and empty' from 'never queried'. [!] THE SECOND CLAUSE IS THE WHOLE POINT: a read that simply shows more rows does not clear this unless a reader can tell WHICH symbols were asked about. Today could_not_look_count is 0 on a read that skipped two symbols entirely. [!] A GREEN TEST CLEARS NOTHING: a fixture chooses what the page returns, and the defect is that the venue returns less than it holds. [!] AND IF THE REMEDY IS REJECTED on the per-tick-cost argument (a cross-check for every configured symbol on every read is the June 2026 wedge shape), this row does NOT clear — it CHANGES: the incompleteness is then an ACCEPTED condition on a real-money account and must be RECORDED as that decision, with the consequence stated, rather than left looking like a fix about to land.", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-21"}, "id": "BL-20260912-SETTLECOIN-PAGE-RETURNS-FEWER-POSITIONS-THAN-THE-VENUE-HOLDS-AND-THE-BACKFILL-SKIPS-PARTIALLY-SURFACED-SYMBOLS", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "review", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json#BL-20260912-SETTLECOIN-PAGE-RETURNS-FEWER-POSITIONS-THAN-THE-VENUE-HOLDS-AND-THE-BACKFILL-SKIPS-PARTIALLY-SURFACED-SYMBOLS", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='BL-20260912-SETTLECOIN-PAGE-RETURNS-FEWER-POSITIONS-THAN-THE-VENUE-HOLDS-AND-THE-BACKFILL-SKIPS-PARTIALLY-SURFACED-SYMBOLS'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[health] The Bybit settleCoin position page returns fewer rows than the venue holds, and the per-symbol backfill is gated on `sym in seen` so it SKIPS exactly the symbols that surfaced partially — a second live hedge book is never READ, and no counter says so"}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 14, "clears_when": "The fix for the INSTANCE is shipped separately and is not this row's subject. This row closes when the CLASS cannot recur silently: EITHER (a) a CI check fails a diff-scoped checker that reads `--base` as a content ref without deriving `merge-base(HEAD, base)` — with a planted control in BOTH directions and a stated exemption for three-dot ranges, which are already correct; OR (b) the four candidates above are each hand-read and recorded as defective-and-fixed or correct-with-the-reason, AND the shared helper that derives the comparison point has ONE owner that new guards import rather than re-deriving. ⚠️ Fixing the four by hand WITHOUT (a) or the shared owner closes nothing — that is exactly what happened four times to register-field-loss, on the same day, before it recurred in a fifth file. ⚠️ And do NOT close on the count: '19 of 24' is the flag-bearing population, not the defect population, and the 4 is a candidate set from a weak classifier.", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-21"}, "id": "BL-20260912-THE-TIP-NOT-MERGE-BASE-DEFECT-RECURRED-IN-A-GUARD-SHIPPED-HOURS-AFTER-ITS-FOURTH-FIX-AND-REDDENED-A-PR-FOR-DOING-NOTHING", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "review", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json#BL-20260912-THE-TIP-NOT-MERGE-BASE-DEFECT-RECURRED-IN-A-GUARD-SHIPPED-HOURS-AFTER-ITS-FOURTH-FIX-AND-REDDENED-A-PR-FOR-DOING-NOTHING", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='BL-20260912-THE-TIP-NOT-MERGE-BASE-DEFECT-RECURRED-IN-A-GUARD-SHIPPED-HOURS-AFTER-ITS-FOURTH-FIX-AND-REDDENED-A-PR-FOR-DOING-NOTHING'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[health] backlog-unresolve-guard graded against main's TIP and reported a branch that changed NOTHING as a silent re-opening — the same defect register-field-loss has been fixed for four times, reproduced in a new guard the same day"}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 14, "clears_when": "(1) `/api/bot/logs?limit=1000` with NO level filter returns a window WIDER than one tick — measured by reading the returned `timestamp` span against the live endpoint, not by reading the code. Today that span is 13 ms; any fix must be demonstrated against a real trader, since the defect only appears when one source out-writes the others. (2) EITHER a `level=error` query is shown to scan beyond the newest `max(limit, _LOG_TAIL)` rows per source — demonstrated by planting a known error row older than that boundary and retrieving it — OR the response carries the scanned window and a truncation flag, and a consumer is shown reading it. ⚠️ A GREEN TEST CLEARS NOTHING: a fixture chooses how many rows each source holds, and the defect is a production write-rate imbalance. ⚠️ AND DO NOT CLOSE THIS BY RAISING `_LOG_TAIL`: a bigger window is still a window, and the next busy tick restores the defect.", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-21"}, "id": "BL-20260912-API-BOT-LOGS-TAKES-A-TAIL-PER-SOURCE-AND-FILTERS-AFTERWARDS-SO-THE-UNFILTERED-FEED-IS-THIRTEEN-MILLISECONDS-WIDE-AND-A-QUIET-ERROR-QUERY-IS-A-WINDOW-NOT-A-NEGATIVE", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "review", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json#BL-20260912-API-BOT-LOGS-TAKES-A-TAIL-PER-SOURCE-AND-FILTERS-AFTERWARDS-SO-THE-UNFILTERED-FEED-IS-THIRTEEN-MILLISECONDS-WIDE-AND-A-QUIET-ERROR-QUERY-IS-A-WINDOW-NOT-A-NEGATIVE", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='BL-20260912-API-BOT-LOGS-TAKES-A-TAIL-PER-SOURCE-AND-FILTERS-AFTERWARDS-SO-THE-UNFILTERED-FEED-IS-THIRTEEN-MILLISECONDS-WIDE-AND-A-QUIET-ERROR-QUERY-IS-A-WINDOW-NOT-A-NEGATIVE'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[health] /api/bot/logs takes a TAIL PER SOURCE and applies the level filter afterwards, so the unfiltered feed is 13 MILLISECONDS wide and `level=error` returning [] is a statement about a window, not about the fleet — and the returned count being below the cap does NOT establish non-truncation"}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 14, "clears_when": "(1) The share of IB closes recording `pnl == 0.0` on a non-trivial price move is MEASURED over a stated window holding at least 20 IB closes, split by `exit_reason`. ⚠️ Five closes is not a denominator; do not re-quote this row's 5 as the population. (2) A close that cannot establish its PnL leaves the column NULL — demonstrated by a live row, not by a test, since the condition is a venue read the harness cannot reproduce. (3) The already-written zeros are dispositioned: either corrected with the basis recorded, or explicitly marked as unmeasured. ⚠️ DO NOT close this by back-filling a computed PnL onto the existing rows — the exit prices are `cost_source: estimate` and a derived number stamped over them would make an unmeasured close look measured, which is worse than the zero.", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-21"}, "id": "BL-20260912-EVERY-IB-CLOSE-IN-THE-WINDOW-RECORDS-A-PNL-OF-EXACTLY-ZERO-WHILE-THREE-OF-THEM-MOVED-35-POINTS-ON-DOZENS-OF-LOTS", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "review", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json#BL-20260912-EVERY-IB-CLOSE-IN-THE-WINDOW-RECORDS-A-PNL-OF-EXACTLY-ZERO-WHILE-THREE-OF-THEM-MOVED-35-POINTS-ON-DOZENS-OF-LOTS", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='BL-20260912-EVERY-IB-CLOSE-IN-THE-WINDOW-RECORDS-A-PNL-OF-EXACTLY-ZERO-WHILE-THREE-OF-THEM-MOVED-35-POINTS-ON-DOZENS-OF-LOTS'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[health] Every closed IB trade in a 400-row window records `pnl: 0.0` (or NULL) while three of them moved 35-38 POINTS on 43-83 lots — a written zero, not an absent value, and it aggregates silently into every IB performance figure"}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 30, "clears_when": "(1) A merge that invokes the jsonregister driver leaves ZERO new untracked files in the repository root — verified by running a real conflicting merge and diffing `git status --porcelain` before and after, not by reading the script. (2) The question in the action is ANSWERED on the record: whether the driver or git itself creates `.merge_file_*`. If it is git, say so and close (b) as not-applicable — the ignore rule is then the whole remedy and pretending otherwise leaves a row nobody can finish. ⚠️ DO NOT close this on the `.gitignore` line alone: that hides the files from `git add`, which is the near-miss this row is about, and does not stop them accumulating.", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-21"}, "id": "BL-20260912-THE-JSONREGISTER-MERGE-DRIVER-LEAVES-SCRATCH-FILES-IN-THE-REPO-ROOT-AND-A-ROUTINE-GIT-ADD-A-SWEEPS-THEM-INTO-A-COMMIT", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "review", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json#BL-20260912-THE-JSONREGISTER-MERGE-DRIVER-LEAVES-SCRATCH-FILES-IN-THE-REPO-ROOT-AND-A-ROUTINE-GIT-ADD-A-SWEEPS-THEM-INTO-A-COMMIT", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='BL-20260912-THE-JSONREGISTER-MERGE-DRIVER-LEAVES-SCRATCH-FILES-IN-THE-REPO-ROOT-AND-A-ROUTINE-GIT-ADD-A-SWEEPS-THEM-INTO-A-COMMIT'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[health] The jsonregister merge driver leaves `.merge_file_*` scratch files in the REPO ROOT, untracked and unignored, so a routine `git add -A` commits them — 18 of them reached a pushed branch today and only `pr-landing-guard` R5 stopped them"}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 14, "clears_when": "BOTH, and they are different facts -- say WHICH you cleared. (1) THE NINE BARE ROWS: each of the nine named above either carries a closure record (resolved_at plus one of resolution / resolution_note / a terminal `disposition` update) or is RE-OPENED because nobody can establish it was ever finished. Re-opening is an acceptable outcome and for the two HIGH ones may be the right one -- do not treat back-filling a plausible reason as clearing this, because inventing a closure record is strictly worse than the blank it replaces. (2) THE FLOOR EXISTS: a diff-scoped check refuses a row this change moves INTO a terminal status while carrying no closure record, and it is OBSERVED failing on a planted row and passing on a real one (both controls, not just the negative). [!] DO NOT CLEAR ON A WHOLE-TREE GATE: at 26% attributable it would fail every PR on day one and be switched off, which check_backlog_criteria's own comments already say in terms. [!] DO NOT CLEAR BY BACK-FILLING THE 114: 105 of them carry prose and the reason may genuinely be recoverable by reading; bulk-stamping them with a synthesised resolved_by would destroy the distinction between a documented closure and an undocumented one, which is the only thing this row is about. [!] AND IF THE OPERATOR DECIDES the closure record is not worth enforcing, this row does not clear -- it CHANGES: that is a legitimate decision and must be RECORDED as one rather than left looking like work about to start.", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-21"}, "id": "BL-20260912-NINE-BACKLOG-ROWS-ARE-MARKED-CLOSED-WITH-NO-DATE-NO-AUTHOR-NO-REASON-AND-NO-PROSE-ANYWHERE-IN-THE-ROW-SO-THE-BURN-DOWN-NUMBER-IS-UNAUDITABLE", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "review", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json#BL-20260912-NINE-BACKLOG-ROWS-ARE-MARKED-CLOSED-WITH-NO-DATE-NO-AUTHOR-NO-REASON-AND-NO-PROSE-ANYWHERE-