{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 30, "clears_when": "A session that lands a fix is PROMPTED to re-grade the row it fixed, and the prompt is something the session meets rather than something it must remember to run. The cheapest honest shape is a diff-scoped check: a PR whose diff cites a row id in a commit message, PR body or landing record, where that row is still `open` after the PR lands, is SURFACED -- not failed, and never auto-closed. [!] IT MUST NOT AUTO-CLOSE: most criteria in this register demand an observation on the fleet that a merge does not supply, and two of the five rows measured here are exactly that case. An auto-close would have closed both wrongly, which is strictly worse than leaving three open. [!] A COUNT IS NOT A FIX: re-measuring the open-but-met rate proves the class persists and changes nothing -- this row is not cleared by a bigger census. [!] AND CLOSING THESE THREE CLEARS NOTHING EITHER: that is this change, and it is the instance, not the class. [!] If the operator decides the re-grade prompt is not worth building, that is a legitimate outcome and clears this row -- but it must be RECORDED as a decision, with the consequence stated: rows then accumulate in `open` after their fixes land, and the register's open count stops meaning what a reader takes it to mean.", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-21"}, "id": "BL-20260917-THIS-LANE-FILED-FIVE-ROWS-AND-LEFT-THREE-OPEN-AFTER-THEIR-FIXES-HAD-LANDED-ON-MAIN", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "review", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json#BL-20260917-THIS-LANE-FILED-FIVE-ROWS-AND-LEFT-THREE-OPEN-AFTER-THEIR-FIXES-HAD-LANDED-ON-MAIN", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='BL-20260917-THIS-LANE-FILED-FIVE-ROWS-AND-LEFT-THREE-OPEN-AFTER-THEIR-FIXES-HAD-LANDED-ON-MAIN'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[health] A session that files a row and then ships its fix does not close the row -- measured on this lane's own five rows, three of which sat open with their criteria fully met"}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 30, "clears_when": "EITHER the share of open rows the tool can grade RISES on a stated population and by a stated mechanism, OR it is recorded as a decision that draining is a human re-read rather than a shortlist. [!] DO NOT MEET THIS BY READING BACKTICKED TOKENS AS SYMBOLS -- that is measured above at 6.0% headroom with a false-positive vocabulary dominated by state names, and building it would raise the likely_met count while lowering its truth, which is worse than the current silence. [!] THE UPSTREAM REMEDY IS THE ONE WORTH COSTING: criteria are prose, and a criterion that names a checkable artifact is gradeable by anything while one that names a behaviour is gradeable only by a reader. check_backlog_criteria.py already governs what a criterion must carry, so requiring a NAMED ARTIFACT is expressible there -- but it would bind every NEW row and not the 782 standing ones, and a rule that makes rows harder to file is a real cost against a register the operator has explicitly said should not be capped. That trade is an operator decision and this row does not take it. [!] A RE-RUN OF THE TOOL CLEARS NOTHING: re-measuring 4.2% is not progress, and a likely_met count that grows because more rows happen to name paths is not the mechanism moving.", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-21"}, "id": "BL-20260917-THE-DRAIN-TOOL-CAN-EXPRESS-AN-OPINION-ABOUT-FOUR-PERCENT-OF-THE-BACKLOG-AND-THE-CAUSE-IS-HOW-CRITERIA-ARE-WRITTEN", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "review", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json#BL-20260917-THE-DRAIN-TOOL-CAN-EXPRESS-AN-OPINION-ABOUT-FOUR-PERCENT-OF-THE-BACKLOG-AND-THE-CAUSE-IS-HOW-CRITERIA-ARE-WRITTEN", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='BL-20260917-THE-DRAIN-TOOL-CAN-EXPRESS-AN-OPINION-ABOUT-FOUR-PERCENT-OF-THE-BACKLOG-AND-THE-CAUSE-IS-HOW-CRITERIA-ARE-WRITTEN'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[health] backlog_drain_candidates grades 971 of 1014 open rows not_checkable, and the cause is upstream in how criteria are worded rather than in the tool"}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 30, "clears_when": "SHIPPED as MI-280 U119, and shipping is NOT the criterion. It clears when a due-list run produced BY THE CRON (not by a session typing the command) is OBSERVED on main carrying a sources.red_main_runs.note that states the graded distance — read docs/claude/DUE.json's committed copy and check the commit that wrote it, because a stale artifact would look identical. !! A GREEN TEST SUITE CLEARS NOTHING: 31 tests pass and six planted defects are each caught, and a harness cannot reach the cron. !! THE LIVE RUN DONE WHILE BUILDING CLEARS NOTHING EITHER — that was this session invoking the function by hand with a token, which is precisely the 'a session typed the command' case. !! AND A note THAT READS '0 with distance_unknown' IS NOT THE ONLY PASS: distance_unknown appearing is the honest output when the commits page cannot be read or the graded commit is older than one page, and a run that never emits it may simply never have hit those conditions.", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-21"}, "id": "BL-20260917-RED-MAIN-RUNS-REPORTS-THE-DEFAULT-BRANCHS-HEALTH-FROM-A-COMMIT-THAT-MAY-BE-MANY-COMMITS-BEHIND-THE-TIP-AND-NEVER-SAYS-WHICH", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "review", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json#BL-20260917-RED-MAIN-RUNS-REPORTS-THE-DEFAULT-BRANCHS-HEALTH-FROM-A-COMMIT-THAT-MAY-BE-MANY-COMMITS-BEHIND-THE-TIP-AND-NEVER-SAYS-WHICH", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='BL-20260917-RED-MAIN-RUNS-REPORTS-THE-DEFAULT-BRANCHS-HEALTH-FROM-A-COMMIT-THAT-MAY-BE-MANY-COMMITS-BEHIND-THE-TIP-AND-NEVER-SAYS-WHICH'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[health] src_red_main_runs grades 'the DEFAULT BRANCH' off the latest COMPLET