{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 14, "clears_when": "MECHANICALLY CHECKABLE, on purpose. A test asserts that `update_row(path, rid, fields={'updates': None})` against a row whose `updates` holds >= 1 entry RAISES rather than writing, AND that the file on disk is UNCHANGED afterwards -- the second half matters because a raise after a partial write would still have destroyed the row. PLUS a POSITIVE CONTROL in the same suite: extending `updates` to a LONGER list through the same `fields=` path still succeeds, since forbidding list writes outright would break every disposition this register records and would pass a negative-only suite. PLUS a VACUITY CONTROL showing the assertion can go red -- reconstruct the current permissive behaviour and assert the test fails against it. [!] A GREEN `--check-live` CLEARS NOTHING: the clobbered file round-trips byte-for-byte, which is exactly why this went unnoticed by every existing check. [!] AND DO NOT CLOSE THIS ON THE ONE INSTANCE BEING REPAIRED: the row I clobbered was restored from `main` the same minute; what is owed is the REFUSAL, not the repair.\n\nVERBATIM REPRODUCTION, so a successor re-measures rather than trusting this row (tmpdir, never a live register):\n    from scripts.ops.backlog_append import update_row\n    # row 'BL-1' with updates=[{...},{...}]\n    update_row(p, 'BL-1', fields={'updates': None})\n    # observed 2026-09-17: before 2 -> after None, no exception, no warning\n[!] AND RUN IT AGAINST A COPY: the point of the row is that this call destroys data.", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-21"}, "id": "BL-20260917-UPDATE-ROW-ACCEPTS-A-VALUE-THAT-DESTROYS-A-POPULATED-LIST-FIELD-SO-THE-MANDATED-WRITER-CAN-WIPE-A-ROWS-HISTORY-SILENTLY", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "review", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json#BL-20260917-UPDATE-ROW-ACCEPTS-A-VALUE-THAT-DESTROYS-A-POPULATED-LIST-FIELD-SO-THE-MANDATED-WRITER-CAN-WIPE-A-ROWS-HISTORY-SILENTLY", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='BL-20260917-UPDATE-ROW-ACCEPTS-A-VALUE-THAT-DESTROYS-A-POPULATED-LIST-FIELD-SO-THE-MANDATED-WRITER-CAN-WIPE-A-ROWS-HISTORY-SILENTLY'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[health] update_row accepts a value that DESTROYS a populated list field, so the writer CLAUDE.md mandates can wipe a row's entire history without a word"}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 14, "clears_when": "A session picking up unowned work can tell, FROM THE FIELD IT ROUTES ON, that MI-216's deliverable is already merged -- e.g. its checklist `state` reads something other than `ready` (its object is `waiting` on an operator decision, which is the accurate reading and is already recorded on WO-20260901-PHASE-F), or the row's note leads with the merged PR. !! THE CHECKLIST IS THE MANAGER'S REGISTER AND THIS ROW DOES NOT EDIT IT -- that is why this is filed rather than fixed, and a worker silently restating another item's state is the failure this repo has already paid for on the lease and the session registry. !! FIXING MI-216'S ROW ALONE DOES NOT CLEAR THIS. One corrected row is one row; what this asks is whether the NEXT `ready`/unowned row whose work has landed is distinguishable from one whose work has not. If the answer is 'only by a session reading the code first', say so and record it -- that is a legitimate finding and a legitimate cost, and it should be RECORDED rather than left looking like a convention about to change. !! THE INCIDENCE IS UNDER-MEASURED AND A SWEEP NEEDS THE CLONE DEEPENED FIRST (`git fetch --deepen=2000`): at ~42% recall, an id-prefix probe cannot clear a row, only flag one. Do NOT publish the 11 as a count of stale rows. !! AND A GREEN GUARD CLEARS NOTHING HERE: no guard reads checklist `state` against merged history today, and building one that keys on the id-prefix convention would inherit that 42% recall -- it would grade `could_not_look` for most …[truncated]", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-21"}, "id": "BL-20260918-A-CHECKLIST-ROW-READS-READY-AND-UNOWNED-WHILE-ITS-DELIVERABLE-IS-MERGED-ON-MAIN-SO-IT-ROUTES-THE-NEXT-SESSION-INTO-A-REBUILD", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "review", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json#BL-20260918-A-CHECKLIST-ROW-READS-READY-AND-UNOWNED-WHILE-ITS-DELIVERABLE-IS-MERGED-ON-MAIN-SO-IT-ROUTES-THE-NEXT-SESSION-INTO-A-REBUILD", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='BL-20260918-A-CHECKLIST-ROW-READS-READY-AND-UNOWNED-WHILE-ITS-DELIVERABLE-IS-MERGED-ON-MAIN-SO-IT-ROUTES-THE-NEXT-SESSION-INTO-A-REBUILD'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[health] MI-216 reads `state: ready` / `owner: null` / 'ROUTE IT' while its producer has been merged since 2026-09-10 with real records on disk - I picked it as my next unit and nearly rebuilt it"}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 30, "clears_when": "A manager takes an R13 merge slot for its own branch WITHOUT tripping check_manager_scope, on a PR other than the one that changes this -- either because the guard admits the per-branch path, or because CLAUDE.md's manager guidance names the shared field as the manager route and says why the per-branch one is unavailable. !! A GREEN SELF-TEST CLEARS NOTHING: the guard's harness cannot reproduce a manager commit against a live lease register, which is the condition. !! AND 'the manager stopped using merge slots' DOES NOT CLEAR IT -- R13 requires a claim from any armed self-land, so that outcome means Tier-1 manager work stops self-landing, which is the failure.", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-21"}, "id": "BL-20260919-A-MANAGER-CANNOT-USE-THE-PER-BRANCH-MERGE-SLOT-CLAIM-CLAUDE-MD-TELLS-IT-TO-USE", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "review", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json#BL-20260919-A-MANAGER-CANNOT-USE-THE-PER-BRANCH-MERGE-SLOT-CLAIM-CLAUDE-MD-TELLS-IT-TO-USE", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='BL-20260919-A-MANAGER-CANNOT-USE-THE-PER-BRANCH-MERGE-SLOT-CLAIM-CLAUDE-MD-TELLS-IT-TO-USE'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[health] CLAUDE.md tells a manager to take R13's merge slot via the PER-BRANCH route (.github/merge-slots/<slug>.json, claim_merge_slot.py --branch-claim), on measured conflict grounds: 39 of the last 40 commits touching the shared session-board merge_slot field moved it, so a shared claim is a near-certain conflict that restarts CI, while R13's own docstring says the shared field never serialized anything anyway. BUT '.github/merge-slots/**' IS NOT ON check_manager_scope.py's MANAGER_SURFACE, and 'merge-slots' appears ZERO times in that guard (positive control: '.github/pr-landing/**' and '.github/pr-automerge-requests/**' are both there, adjacent, in the same list). So a manager following CLAUDE.md's recommendation fails R2 as 'doing an item instead of managing'. MEASURED, not inferred: PR #12580 commit a3400032 failed exactly that way."}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 30, "clears_when": "open_pr_record.py reports the two populations SEPARATELY and its completeness verdict is reachable — i.e. a manager that has recorded every human/session-authored open PR can obtain a verdict other than `unrecorded`, while the automation count stays VISIBLE rather than excluded. ⚠️ SUPPRESSING THE AUTOMATED PRs ENTIRELY DOES NOT RESOLVE THIS AND IS THE WRONG FIX: an automated PR that STOPS self-landing is a real finding, and a filter that hides it converts this noise problem into a silence problem. ⚠️ NOR DOES A GREEN SELF-TEST: the tool's harness cannot reach a live open-PR list, which is the whole input in question. The observation wanted is a real reconcile against a real census producing a verdict a reader can act on. ⚠️ AND IF THE OPERATOR DECIDES the automated PRs SHOULD each carry a hand row, this does not clear — it CHANGES: that is a legitimate decision and must be RECORDED as one rather than left looking like a filter about to be written.", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-21"}, "id": "BL-20260919-OPEN-PR-RECORD-COUNTS-68-SELF-LANDING-AUTOMATION-PRS-AGAINST-ITS-COMPLETENESS-AND-NOBODY-CAN-EVER-CLEAR-IT", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "review", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json#BL-20260919-OPEN-PR-RECORD-COUNTS-68-SELF-LANDING-AUTOMATION-PRS-AGAINST-ITS-COMPLETENESS-AND-NOBODY-CAN-EVER-CLEAR-IT", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='BL-20260919-OPEN-PR-RECORD-COUNTS-68-SELF-LANDING-AUTOMATION-PRS-AGAINST-ITS-COMPLETENESS-AND-NOBODY-CAN-EVER-CLEAR-IT'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "queued", "what": "[health] ⚠️ NOT A DUPLICATE AND NOT A RECURRENCE — the filing helper flagged 4 lexical neighbours at overlap 0.69 (BL-20260830-A-TRANSIENT-RED-BASE-PERMANENTLY-STRANDS-AN-AUTOMERGE-BRANCH, BL-20260903-PR-OPENER-DRAFT-FALSE-IS-UNREACHABLE-SO-TIER-1-CAN-NEVER-SELF-LAND-THROUGH-THE-RELAY, BL-20260907-COORDINATION-BOARD-6927-IS-FULL-SO-THE-MANDATORY-START-POST-IS-IMPOSSIBLE-BY-EVERY-PATH, BL-20260911-REPLAY-PREGATE-NIGHTLY-HAS-ZERO-SUCCESSFUL-RUNS-IN-ITS-ENTIRE-LIFE-AND-25-MONTHS-OF-ML-PREGATE-EVIDENCE-HAS-ACCRUED-TO-NOTHING) and all four were READ before re-filing. They share this row's vocabulary (PR, auto-merge, self-land, automation) and none is about open_pr_record.py or about a completeness metric; the overlap is the helper's stated LEXICAL measure doing its job, not a prior filing of this defect. THE DEFECT: scripts/ops/open_pr_record.py grades OPEN-PRS.json's completeness against EVERY open PR and makes no automated/human distinction, so 68 self-landing `chore(...)(auto)` PRs opened by scheduled workflows count against a HAND-MAINTAINED handoff record. MEASURED 2026-09-19 over a full census (all 82 open PRs, the UNION of both pages of list_pull_requests(state=open, perPage=50); page 2 returned 32 < 50, so this is a census and not a page): 82 open, 14 recorded after a reconcile that added all 6 missing human-authored PRs, 68 unrecorded and ALL 68 automated. The verdict therefore reads `completeness=unrecorded` whether the manager reconciled diligently or did nothing at all — the two are indistinguishable from the output, which is the desensitised-alarm P1 this repo names as its own worst failure mode, inside the very tool built to make a handoff honest. ⚠️ THE COST IS NOT COSMETIC: it hid a real row. #12497 is a Tier-2 `landing: hold` PR awaiting ONE operator OK since 2026-09-18 with mergeable_state `clean`; it had NO row in OPEN-PRS.json and was carried ONLY by an hourly check-in routine that had fired 33 times — and a routine cannot supply an operator decision, so every firing was noise about a condition it could never clear. A permanently-red completeness verdict is exactly what makes a genuinely-missing row invisible. ⚠️ THE FIX IS NOT SIMPLY 'EXCLUDE chore('. Matching on a title prefix is a string with no denominator behind it (UNPROVENANCED DIAGNOSTIC OUTPUT sub-class B), and an automation that changed its title would silently leave the population. Partition on something structural — the opening actor, or a self-landing branch namespace such as automation/** — and REPORT both counts separately rather than dropping one: `unrecorded (human)` and `unrecorded (automation)` are different facts, and collapsing them is how this started."}
