{"due_when": {"due_date": "2026-09-21", "kind": "date"}, "id": "BL-20260830-PRIORITY-IS-MOOT-COMMENTS-REASON-PER-ACCOUNT-WHILE-ARBITRATION-IS-PER-SYMBOL", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "review", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json#BL-20260830-PRIORITY-IS-MOOT-COMMENTS-REASON-PER-ACCOUNT-WHILE-ARBITRATION-IS-PER-SYMBOL", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='BL-20260830-PRIORITY-IS-MOOT-COMMENTS-REASON-PER-ACCOUNT-WHILE-ARBITRATION-IS-PER-SYMBOL'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "killed", "terminal_reason": "unworked_since_never", "what": "Most rows in DEFAULT_PRIORITIES justified a value of 0 with 'this leg runs ALONE on its (symbol, account), so priority is moot -- it never arbitrates against another strategy'. aggregate_intents elects ONE winner per SYMBOL, globally, BEFORE the per-account fan-out in multi_account_execute; it never sees an account. The justification described a scope the aggregator does not use, and it is the reasoning that produced BL-20260830-TREND-DONCHIAN-SOL-SIGNALS-144-TIMES-AND-JOURNALS-NOTHING-ON-BYBIT-1. MEASURED 2026-08-30 against the live config: 12 symbols are contested by more than one enabled leg -- BTCUSDT 7, SOLUSDT 6, ETHUSDT 6, XRPUSDT 4, MGC 3, AVAXUSDT 3, and six more at 2. Every contesting leg sits at priority 0, so the winner is decided entirely by the name tiebreak. Comments corrected + a mutation-verified regression test added (tests/test_priority_arbitration_scope.py); NO priority VALUE was changed, so behaviour is byte-identical."}
{"due_when": {"due_date": "2026-09-21", "kind": "date"}, "id": "BL-20260830-PRIORITY-CANNOT-RESOLVE-A-DISJOINT-ACCOUNT-TWIN-PAIR", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "review", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json#BL-20260830-PRIORITY-CANNOT-RESOLVE-A-DISJOINT-ACCOUNT-TWIN-PAIR", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='BL-20260830-PRIORITY-CANNOT-RESOLVE-A-DISJOINT-ACCOUNT-TWIN-PAIR'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "killed", "terminal_reason": "unworked_since_never", "what": "Setting explicit priorities was considered as the remedy for the starved trend_donchian_sol / trend_donchian_eth legs. It cannot work, and shipping it would trade one silent failure for another. VERIFIED 2026-08-30: each base leg and its _prop twin route to DISJOINT account sets (trend_donchian_sol -> {bybit_1}, trend_donchian_sol_prop -> {breakout_1}; same shape for eth), and the twins share entries by design -- the prop variant differs only in exits. So the correct outcome is that BOTH trade. aggregate_intents elects exactly ONE winner per symbol, so any ordering starves one of them; raising the base leg above its twin would starve a Tier-3 operator-approved live prop leg on the account where a breach is TERMINAL. The per-account fan-out is the only remedy that expresses the intended behaviour."}
{"due_when": {"due_date": "2026-09-21", "kind": "date"}, "id": "BL-20260830-SCHEDULED-WORKFLOW-LAG-WENT-FROM-1H-TO-6-12H-ON-2026-08-27", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "review", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json#BL-20260830-SCHEDULED-WORKFLOW-LAG-WENT-FROM-1H-TO-6-12H-ON-2026-08-27", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='BL-20260830-SCHEDULED-WORKFLOW-LAG-WENT-FROM-1H-TO-6-12H-ON-2026-08-27'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "killed", "terminal_reason": "unworked_since_never", "what": "Every scheduled workflow in this repo now fires 6-12 HOURS after its declared cron, against ~1h before 2026-08-27. MEASURED INDEPENDENTLY 2026-08-30, not taken from prose. POPULATION: purge-artifacts.yml, declared `0 3 * * *`, the 30 consecutive scheduled runs from 2026-08-01 to 2026-08-30 (lag = run_started_at minus the most recent 03:00Z). 08-01..08-26: 0.66-3.42h, median ~1.3h, no outliers. 08-27 onward: 10.88, 12.01, 6.80, 5.98h. The break is sharp and dated. A second control (diag-relay-sweep, `0 6 * * *`) shows the same break on the same date per PR #10487's commit message, and research-queue-dispatch tracks both. ⚠️ THE CAUSE IS NOT ESTABLISHED. Best-effort scheduling under load is the leading explanation and is NOT proven; GitHub documents scheduled workflows as best-effort and does not guarantee a start time. This row records the MEASUREMENT and the operational consequence, and deliberately does not assert a cause. ⚠️ NOTHING IS BEING DROPPED as far as measured — the runs arrive, late. Do not read this as missed executions without checking, because 'late' and 'never' are different facts and only the first is supported here. ⚠️ CORRECTED 2026-09-10: the 'NOTHING IS BEING DROPPED' sentence holds only for crons declared WIDER than the lag. An hourly cron measured the same day delivered 27% of its declared slots — below the lag, slots coalesce and simply never run. See the re-measurement in `detail`."}
