{"due_when": {"due_date": "2026-09-21", "kind": "date"}, "id": "BL-20260903-THE-PR-QUEUE-WATCHER-CANNOT-SEE-A-TEN-MINUTE-STALL", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "review", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json#BL-20260903-THE-PR-QUEUE-WATCHER-CANNOT-SEE-A-TEN-MINUTE-STALL", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='BL-20260903-THE-PR-QUEUE-WATCHER-CANNOT-SEE-A-TEN-MINUTE-STALL'), indent=2))\""}, "state": "killed", "terminal_reason": "unworked_since_never", "what": "THE FAILURE: PR #10923 was green and mergeable at 11:01:05Z and merged at 11:11Z. Ten minutes. The OPERATOR noticed before any mechanism did; the manager's own 11:04Z sweep timer trig_016Tn8Kt9sDtQHhxG9NKQabT was `enabled` with `next_run_at: 11:04:00Z` and NEVER FIRED -- no error, no log, no observable state change. WHY THE EXISTING WATCHER CANNOT SEE IT, measured by reading it rather than assumed: `scripts/ops/pr_queue_latency.py` has DEFAULT_THRESHOLD_HOURS = 6.0, and `.github/workflows/pr-queue-watch.yml` runs it on cron `41 */6 * * *`. So the event is below its threshold by a factor of 36 AND below its sampling interval by a factor of 36. Two independent margins, either one sufficient. WORSE, IT MEASURES THE WRONG QUANTITY: `grade_pr` computes `quiet_hours` as hours since the last commit on the head branch, so a PR that JUST went green has quiescence near zero and reads as actively worked -- the exact opposite of 'finished and waiting on the manager'."}
