{"due_when": {"clears_when": "`git status --porcelain` is empty immediately after a local `scripts/ci/run_guards.py` run that did produce pytest-report.xml.", "kind": "observation"}, "id": "PI-20260921-M10", "next_action": "dispatch_lane", "origin": {"kind": "session", "ref": "Manager session 2026-09-21 (session_01XQhayAHWdrSYv8jN54P2HH); the stop-hook reported untracked files and the file was a transient pytest-report.xml from a still-running guards job.", "rerun": "cd <repo> && timeout 400 python3 scripts/ci/run_guards.py >/dev/null 2>&1; git status --porcelain | grep pytest-report.xml"}, "routed_to": "One line in .gitignore. Verify by running the guards and re-running `git status --porcelain` — the negative control is that the file IS produced by that run, so a clean status after the ignore is not a no-op.", "state": "routed", "what": "pytest-report.xml is written into the repo root by the guards/pytest run and is not in .gitignore, so a session that runs the guards locally then finds an untracked file it did not create.", "why_it_matters": "It is a false positive on the exact probe a session uses to decide whether it has unlanded work — this session's stop-hook fired on it. A recurring false positive on a completion check trains the check to be ignored, which is the same failure mode as a wall of undispositioned rows."}
{"due_when": {"clears_when": "`git status --porcelain` is empty immediately after a local `scripts/ci/run_guards.py` run that did produce pytest-report.xml.", "kind": "observation"}, "id": "PI-20260921-M10", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "session", "ref": "Manager session 2026-09-21 (session_01XQhayAHWdrSYv8jN54P2HH); the stop-hook reported untracked files and the file was a transient pytest-report.xml from a still-running guards job.", "rerun": "cd <repo> && timeout 400 python3 scripts/ci/run_guards.py >/dev/null 2>&1; git status --porcelain | grep pytest-report.xml"}, "routed_to": "One line in .gitignore. Verify by running the guards and re-running `git status --porcelain` — the negative control is that the file IS produced by that run, so a clean status after the ignore is not a no-op.", "state": "done", "terminal_reason": "Fixed in the same push that filed it: pytest-report.xml added to .gitignore. Verified with a POSITIVE CONTROL rather than a bare clean status -- the file was created, `git status --porcelain pytest-report.xml` printed nothing, and the file was removed. A clean status without first making the file would not have distinguished 'ignored' from 'absent'.", "what": "pytest-report.xml is written into the repo root by the guards/pytest run and is not in .gitignore, so a session that runs the guards locally then finds an untracked file it did not create.", "why_it_matters": "It is a false positive on the exact probe a session uses to decide whether it has unlanded work — this session's stop-hook fired on it. A recurring false positive on a completion check trains the check to be ignored, which is the same failure mode as a wall of undispositioned rows."}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 30, "clears_when": "scripts/backtest_orb.py imports execution_costs and applies a slippage term (in points, unit-reconciled) in addition to FEE_POINTS_ROUNDTRIP.", "kind": "observation"}, "id": "PI-20260921-E3-ORB-NO-SLIPPAGE", "next_action": "dispatch_lane", "origin": {"kind": "session", "ref": "checklist E3 build lane, session_01HmQgaLfZv9rv7uuWfgA321", "rerun": "grep -n \"FEE_POINTS_ROUNDTRIP\\|slippage\\|execution_costs\" scripts/backtest_orb.py"}, "routed_to": null, "state": "queued", "terminal_reason": null, "what": "checklist E3 (cost model reaching every harness): scripts/backtest_orb.py has NO slippage model and was never wired to execution_costs.py. Its fee is modelled in POINTS round-trip (FEE_POINTS_ROUNDTRIP=0.5), not bps -- a deliberate, venue-correct choice for index futures -- but that means execution_costs.roundtrip_cost_r cannot be reused as-is (it assumes a bps-of-notional fee/slippage term). E3 wired the 6 harnesses that had literally zero cost terms (chop_scalp, funding_carry, fvg_range, pairs, xsec_momentum, vwap) plus confirmed the 6 harnesses already wired to execution_costs (fade, ict_scalp, pullback, squeeze, system, trend) resolve slippage/funding via the venue-aware CLI default already. orb.py was NOT wired: it needs a points-vs-bps unit reconciliation (execution_costs.slippage_bps_roundtrip_for(symbol) converted to points via the traded contract's tick/point value) before a slippage term can be added without fabricating a wrong-unit cost."}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 30, "clears_when": "scripts/backtest_xsec_momentum.py charges a funding cost per rebalance period (or a dated decision doc explicitly excludes it with a stated reason).", "kind": "observation"}, "id": "PI-20260921-E3-XSEC-MOMENTUM-FUNDING-GAP", "next_action": "dispatch_lane", "origin": {"kind": "session", "ref": "checklist E3 build lane, session_01HmQgaLfZv9rv7uuWfgA321", "rerun": "grep -n \"FUNDING_BPS_PER_WINDOW\\|funding_bps_per_window\\|slippage_bps\" scripts/backtest_xsec_momentum.py"}, "routed_to": null, "state": "queued", "terminal_reason": null, "what": "checklist E3: scripts/backtest_xsec_momentum.py was wired to execution_costs for SLIPPAGE (via the same per-rebalance turnover-cost mechanism as its existing fee), but FUNDING was deliberately left unwired. The harness holds WEIGHTS across a multi-symbol universe with rebalance-to-rebalance bookkeeping, not per-position entry/exit Trade objects, so there is no clean per-leg hold-duration to charge execution_costs' funding-window drag against. The book trades a crypto-perp universe (per its own docstring: \"crypto long/short, dollar-neutral\"), so funding IS a real, currently-unpriced cost. Needs either (a) a per-symbol position-duration tracker between rebalances so funding windows crossed can be computed per leg, or (b) an explicit, documented decision that funding is out of scope for this harness with a stated reason beyond \"not yet built\"."}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 30, "clears_when": "src/backtest/backtester.py imports execution_costs and its fee/slippage/funding computation is derived from it (fee_bps_roundtrip / slippage_bps_roundtrip / funding_bps_per_window), not a separate pct-based literal set.", "kind": "observation"}, "id": "PI-20260921-E3-BACKTESTER-DUAL-COST-MODEL", "next_action": "dispatch_lane", "origin": {"kind": "session", "ref": "checklist E3 build lane, session_01HmQgaLfZv9rv7uuWfgA321", "rerun": "grep -n \"taker_fee_pct\\|maker_fee_pct\\|slippage_pct\\|execution_costs\" src/backtest/backtester.py"}, "routed_to": null, "state": "queued", "terminal_reason": null, "what": "checklist E3: src/backtest/backtester.py::ICTBacktester (used by bin/backtest_ict.py, and load_data()/summarize() from it are imported by src/backtest/run_backtest_vwap.py) hardcodes its OWN independent cost model (taker_fee_pct=0.055, maker_fee_pct=0.02, slippage_pct=0.02 at the top of the file) and never imports or consults src/runtime/execution_costs.py -- the exact \"a second cost model is how the two drift\" failure execution_costs.py's own module docstring warns about (P1 section 3.B, the cost-model-single-owner guard family). bin/backtest_ict.py is declared scaffolding-only in its own docstring and has no funding term at all (crypto perp funding never modelled). Not fixed in E3 because changing backtester.py's cost math is a bigger consolidation (rebasing it onto execution_costs.roundtrip_cost_usd) that deserves its own review of whether existing backtester.py consumers depend on the current pct-based numbers."}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 45, "clears_when": "A prop sizing refusal for a stale/absent balance is OBSERVED reaching the operator — a row in outcomes.jsonl / /api/bot/logs?level=error, or a /api/bot/notifications banner, naming the account and carrying prop_balance's own refusal_message text — read off the live surface, not from a test. ⚠️ A GREEN TEST CLEARS NOTHING: the refusal path is already exercised in tests and the gap is which channel the message reaches. ⚠️ AND A JOURNALED order_packages ROW IS NOT SUFFICIENT — that is precisely the current behaviour and the thing being reported as inadequate. ALTERNATIVELY the operator DECIDES that a silently non-trading prop account needs no alert, which clears this row as a RECORDED decision stating the accepted consequence (breakout_1 can stop trading indefinitely with the only evidence being a journalctl line), rather than being left looking like a fix about to happen.", "kind": "observation", "last_checked": "2026-09-21"}, "id": "BL-20260909-PROP-SIZING-REFUSAL-ON-A-STALE-BALANCE-IS-JOURNALED-BUT-NEVER-PINGED", "next_action": "dispatch_lane", "origin": {"kind": "review", "ref": "docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json#BL-20260909-PROP-SIZING-REFUSAL-ON-A-STALE-BALANCE-IS-JOURNALED-BUT-NEVER-PINGED", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/health-review-backlog.json')); items=d['items'] if isinstance(d, dict) else d; print(json.dumps(next(i for i in items if i.get('id')=='BL-20260909-PROP-SIZING-REFUSAL-ON-A-STALE-BALANCE-IS-JOURNALED-BUT-NEVER-PINGED'), indent=2))\""}, "routed_to": "Checklist row E16, lane session_012DyVaL6CpsTxEwHBs9MEcB (opus-5), dispatched 2026-09-21. ⚠️ HALF OF THIS ROW'S clears_when HAS NOW HAPPENED, FROM THE WRONG DIRECTION. It reads: 'breakout_1 can stop trading indefinitely with the only evidence being a journalctl line.' On 2026-09-21 the OPERATOR raised, unprompted, that they had had no prop trade notification in a suspiciously long time. MEASURED the same hour off the live host: newest account_status is 2026-08-30T19:33:29Z (528.4h, `stale` against a 24h gate), newest ticket 2026-09-13 `expired`, no fill since 2026-08-30, distance_to_daily_loss_usd null. So the predicted state is the observed state and the discovery method was a human noticing an ABSENCE -- not the alert this row asks for. [!] THE ROW IS NOT CLEARED BY THAT. Its clears_when demands the refusal message reaching an operator-facing channel, read off a live surface; a human independently noticing the silence is the failure it describes, not the fix. [!] AND THE OPERATOR'S OTHER DECISION THE SAME DAY RAISES THE STAKES: the periodic status ask now fires only on new exposure, so the one thing that WAS noisy in the stalled state is going away. E16 is instructed not to ship that silencing without the refusal signal in the same landing.", "state": "routed", "what": "[health] A prop account blocked from trading by a stale balance stops trading with NO operator-facing signal — the refusal is journaled and logged, and the ping that exists for exactly this is on a different branch"}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 14, "clears_when": "Either the prop branch is reached unconditionally for a declared prop account (and that is shown by a test exercising all three states), or an operator decision records that the two bypass paths are acceptable and says why. ⚠️ A passing test of the ONE reachable state clears nothing -- that state already works.", "kind": "observation"}, "id": "PI-20260921-E16-BALANCE-FETCHER-PROP-BRANCH-RARELY-REACHED", "next_action": "ask_operator", "origin": {"kind": "session", "ref": "E16 build lane session_012DyVaL6CpsTxEwHBs9MEcB, PR #12696 'Filed for follow-ups' item 3, 2026-09-21. Read from the code, not inferred from behaviour.", "rerun": "Read src/core/coordinator.py::_default_balance_fetcher in full and enumerate which branches reach src/prop/prop_balance.py::prop_sizing_balance."}, "state": "queued", "what": "src/core/coordinator.py::_default_balance_fetcher reaches prop_sizing_balance in only one of three states: cached_balance_usd is checked first and returns before the prop branch, and an account absent from live_balances takes branch 3 and never consults prop_balance at all. So the stale-balance sizing gate is weaker than prop_balance's own docstring implies.", "why_it_matters": "The gate is the reason it is considered safe for the prop status ask to go quiet while the book is flat -- the argument is 'sizing refuses on a stale balance, so nothing trades off a number nobody measured.' If the refusal is reachable in only one of three states, that argument is weaker than it reads, on a FUNDED account with an $87.34 cushion to an account-killer floor."}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 30, "clears_when": "Either the prop ticket TTL is set from a stated assumption about operator response time (recorded, with the number), or a decision records that expiry-by-clock is the intended behaviour on this bridge and what the accepted loss rate is. ⚠️ Needs the expired-share re-measured over a full window, not the 8/164 sample above.", "kind": "observation"}, "id": "PI-20260921-E16-PROP-TICKET-TTL-SHORTER-THAN-THE-HUMAN", "next_action": "ask_operator", "origin": {"kind": "session", "ref": "E16 build lane session_012DyVaL6CpsTxEwHBs9MEcB, PR #12696 'Filed for follow-ups' item 5, 2026-09-21.", "rerun": "curl -s 'https://ict-bot.duckdns.org/api/bot/prop/tickets?limit=200' and count status=='expired' against the total."}, "state": "queued", "what": "The 2026-09-13 prop ticket carried a 1h validity and went `expired` unanswered. On a MANUAL bridge the validity window may simply be shorter than the operator's response time. Population: 8 of the 164 tickets in the measured window are `expired`.", "why_it_matters": "It is not the cause of the three-week silence -- that is the arbitration defect on E17 -- but it is a real second-order loss: a ticket the bridge could have filled is discarded by the clock while the operator is asleep or at work. And an expired ticket used to LATCH the status ask (fixed in #12696), so its cost was previously larger than it looked."}
