so this is a re-run plus a schema addition, not new machinery. C4 still needs B5 and is untouched by E3."}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 7, "clears_when": "Either the trainer VM has a serialization mechanism that actually exists, or those comments say plainly that heavy work is unserialized and what the consequence is. Reading the comments is not enough -- check whether two concurrent trainer jobs can in fact overlap today.", "kind": "observation"}, "id": "PI-20260921-0001", "next_action": "dispatch_lane", "origin": {"kind": "session", "ref": "session_011Z18XADmiW1npvEeHm5nCP, found via scope-overlap-audit's self-test failing on PR #12674 for a missing board-pointer.json", "rerun": "grep -rln 'board-pointer\\|board_pointer' --include=*.yml --include=*.py . | grep -v '^./docs/archive'"}, "routed_to": "session_0123X5YvhKUFRoScLj1amz3L (build lane, sonnet-5) -- MEASURED, not just read: 2 workflows make the serialization claim of 23 that touch the trainer VM; docs/claude/board-pointer.json is absent at its old path (archived); .claude/skills/session-coordination/ is archived and absent from the live catalog; docs/claude/coordination-board.md's own MEASURED 2026-08-20 record shows vm_lane_guard.sh is inert on Claude Code on the web, 'where most of these sessions run'; a real, separate, CI-enforced on-VM flock (src/utils/trainer_heavy_lock.py) already exists but only covers python -m ml train/build-dataset + an enumerated research-entrypoint list, not arbitrary heavy commands dispatched through these two relays. BOTH acceptance outcomes delivered, split by evidence: trainer-vm-diag.yml's issues+trainer-vm-heavy-request path now wraps its SSH command in that SAME real flock (verified locally -- single acquire, concurrent collision -> refuse exit 75, crashed holder -> next run still acquires, no deadlock); trainer-diag-relay.yml has no heavy/light signal to wrap automatically, so its comment states plainly it applies no serialization and how a heavy script must lock itself. docs/claude/vm-resource-management.md and vm_lane_guard.sh's deny message corrected to stop pointing at the dead board. PR: https://github.com/benbaichmankass/Metis-Insights/pull/12691 (draft, landing=hold/operator_asked_to_hold per lane instructions -- manager merges). scripts/ci/run_guards.py: 36 pass/0 fail/35 skip (not relevant) on the committed diff. NOT YET MERGED and the new flock-wrap step has not yet been exercised against a live trainer-vm-diag SSH session -- that is the first live proof, owed after merge. Flagged in the PR body (not filed here, out of this lane's scope): ~20+ other non-archived files still live-read board-pointer.json/session-board.json, a materially bigger surface than the two workflows this item named.", "state": "routed", "what": "The trainer VM's cross-session serialization protocol is gone: trainer-diag-relay.yml and trainer-vm-diag.yml both document heavy VM work as serialized by a VM-LANE CLAIM/RELEASE FIFO on the coordination board, which the 2026-09-21 reset archived. Comments only -- the relays still RUN -- but on a 1-OCPU box the serialization they promise no longer exists and nothing says so."}
{"due_when": {"clears_when": "PR #12686 merges to main AND a --check / --self-test run on main's own copy confirms: the PI-20260921-0002 collision is still reported (not silenced) and no NEW collision has been introduced by the merge itself.", "kind": "event"}, "id": "PI-20260921-M04", "loud": true, "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "session", "ref": "session_01XQhayAHWdrSYv8jN54P2HH (manager, 2026-09-21) -- found while resolving the E9 merge; caused by this same session", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import sys;sys.path.insert(0,'scripts/ops');import pipeline;r=pipeline.read_log();print('records',r.records,'items',len(r.items))\"; # FINDING STILL APPLIES while records>items for reasons other than genuine state changes, and while append() accepts an existing id carrying unrelated content without complaint"}, "routed_to": "PR #12686 (branch claude/pipeline-collision-guard-m04, session session_01YUnvQssdGXkpjB2MRkddF3) -- the manager merges per this lane's dispatch instruction ('DO NOT MERGE -- the manager merges'). Mark done only after merge and the clears_when observation above. [!] RECORD REPAIRED AT MERGE by the manager: the lane's update had rewritten `what` and repointed `origin.ref` at its own session and PR, which made a legitimate state change indistinguishable from a displaced finding -- and THIS BRANCH'S OWN NEW CHECK FLAGGED IT, on its own record, which is the best possible evidence that the check works. `origin` is where the finding came from; the lane's PR belongs here in routed_to.", "state": "routed", "what": "pipeline.append() HAS NO ID COLLISION CHECK, SO ONE FINDING CAN SILENTLY DISPLACE ANOTHER AND THE STORE STILL READS `clean`. MEASURED 2026-09-21, and it is not hypothetical -- the manager session did it. The store's design is APPEND-ONLY with LAST-RECORD-WINS, which is correct for a STATE CHANGE to a known item and is what gives the audit trail for free. But nothing distinguishes 'a new state for item X' from 'a DIFFERENT finding that happens to reuse X's id'. Two sessions filing concurrently both mint the next free number from the store they each read at start, and neither can see the other. Observed: main carried PI-20260921-0002 (a board-pointer reader finding, filed by session_011Z18XADmiW1npvEeHm5nCP in #12676); the manager appended a pr-landing-guard finding under the same id; load() folded 8 records to 4 items and the FIRST finding vanished from items, due() and render_section_0 entirely. Concurrently the E9 lane minted its OWN 0002/0003/0004/0005 on its branch for four further different findings, so the same collision was queued up three more times behind a merge. [!] `--check` REPORTED `clean -- 4 item(s) validated over 8 record(s), 0 unreadable` THROUGHOUT. That is the serious half: the store cannot distinguish 'this item is gone' from 'this item was never filed', which is the exact collapsed-state class scripts/ci/check_collapsed_states.py enforces against repo-wide, occurring inside the module built to stop findings being dropped. A displaced row is a DROPPED FINDING that looks like a healthy store. [!] DO NOT fix it by making ids opaque (a uuid) and stopping there -- the readable dated id is worth keeping and the real defect is the missing check. append() should refuse an id already present whose record is not plausibly a state change of it, and the caller should have to say which it means. Allocation needs an answer too: two sessions that cannot see each other will keep picking the same next number."}
