ing scripts/ci/check_roster_promotion_evidence.py --population on f3746ab: 8/12 risk-bearing roster slots clear the four-clause bar; fvg_range_15m is the only one of the four failing on C1+C2+C3+C4 rather than C4 alone. The producer-side gap is named in PI-20260922-R1-REMAINING-CORPUS: regime_debt_matrix.classify() has no branch for the fvg_range family (scripts/backtest_fvg_range.py exists; nothing routes to it). THAT row builds the harness; THIS row is the one that comes back and asks the roster question afterwards, which that row's clears_when does not cover. The leg keeps execution: shadow in config/strategies.yaml (untouched) and keeps trading on bybit_1, the full-roster soak book, so evidence can still accrue against it."}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 14, "clears_when": "EITHER a fresh web session can run `python3 -m pytest tests/ -q` with ZERO collection errors (a SessionStart step, a documented one-line install, or whatever route is chosen), OR a lane establishes that partial local test coverage is intended and writes the honest ceiling somewhere a lane reads BEFORE it validates -- naming which test files a session can and cannot run. Either closes it; asserting 'CI has the deps' does not, because that is already true and is not what this row is about.", "kind": "observation"}, "id": "PI-20260922-R2-A-WEB-SESSION-CANNOT-RUN-THE-TEST-SUITE-AND-GETS-NO-SIGNAL-THAT-IT-COULD-NOT", "next_action": "dispatch_lane", "origin": {"kind": "session", "ref": "session_017aScqdVcFoXxqffMcsf5Ge, checklist row R2, incidental observation while validating a Tier-3 config diff", "rerun": "python3 -m pytest tests/ -q 2>&1 | tail -3   # FINDING STILL APPLIES while this reports collection errors on a fresh Claude Code on the web container; it clears when the count is 0"}, "state": "queued", "what": "MEASURED 2026-09-22 in a Claude Code on the web container (this session): `python3 -m pytest tests/ -q` aborts with '149 errors during collection' -- ModuleNotFoundError on pandas and siblings. pytest itself was ALSO absent until I ran `pip install pytest` by hand. The repo ships requirements.txt / requirements-dev.txt / requirements-test.txt and nothing in the session bootstrap installs any of them; .claude/hooks/ contains only vm_lane_guard.sh. [!] THE DEFECT IS NOT THE MISSING DEPS, IT IS THE ABSENCE OF A SIGNAL. A lane that runs a targeted `pytest tests/test_foo.py` and sees green has no way to learn that ~149 other files never collected -- 'the tests I ran passed' and 'the tests that matter passed' are indistinguishable from inside the container, which is the collapsed-state shape docs/CLAUDE-RULES-CANONICAL.md names. This session hit it while validating a TIER-3 real-money roster diff: tests/test_account_side_filter.py showed 6 failures, and only because I checked out origin/main's config/accounts.yaml and re-ran the file did I establish the same 6 fail WITHOUT my change (missing pandas, pre-existing). A lane that did not think to baseline would have read them as its own regression, or -- the dangerous direction -- a lane whose change DID break something in the 149 uncollected files would have pushed on a green partial run. [!] I DID NOT DIAGNOSE THE CAUSE and am not asserting one: I did not check whether the deps are deliberately omitted (container size, install time) or simply never wired. That determination is the first thing the lane should make, because it decides which of the two clears_when branches applies. [!] SCOPE OF MY OBSERVATION, stated because it bounds the claim: one container, one date, one repo checkout. I did not test a second session."}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 14, "clears_when": "EITHER a fresh web session can run `python3 -m pytest tests/ -q` with ZERO collection errors (a SessionStart step, a documented one-line install, or whatever route is chosen), OR a lane establishes that partial local test coverage is intended and writes the honest ceiling somewhere a lane reads BEFORE it validates -- naming which test files a session can and cannot run. Either closes it; asserting 'CI has the deps' does not, because that is already true and is not what this row is about.", "kind": "observation"}, "id": "PI-20260922-R2-A-WEB-SESSION-CANNOT-RUN-THE-TEST-SUITE-AND-GETS-NO-SIGNAL-THAT-IT-COULD-NOT", "next_action": "dispatch_lane", "origin": {"kind": "session", "ref": "session_017aScqdVcFoXxqffMcsf5Ge, checklist row R2, incidental observation while validating a Tier-3 config diff", "rerun": "python3 -m pytest tests/ -q 2>&1 | tail -3   # FINDING STILL APPLIES while this reports collection errors on a fresh Claude Code on the web container; it clears when the count is 0"}, "state": "queued", "what": "MEASURED 2026-09-22 in a Claude Code on the web container (this session): `python3 -m pytest tests/ -q` aborts with '149 errors during collection' -- ModuleNotFoundError on pandas and siblings. pytest itself was ALSO absent until I ran `pip install pytest` by hand. The repo ships requirements.txt / requirements-dev.txt / requirements-test.txt and nothing in the session bootstrap installs any of them; .claude/hooks/ contains only vm_lane_guard.sh. [!] THE DEFECT IS NOT THE MISSING DEPS, IT IS THE ABSENCE OF A SIGNAL. A lane that runs a targeted `pytest tests/test_foo.py` and sees green has no way to learn that ~149 other files never collected -- 'the tests I ran passed' and 'the tests that matter passed' are indistinguishable from inside the container, which is the collapsed-state shape docs/CLAUDE-RULES-CANONICAL.md names. This session hit it while validating a TIER-3 real-money roster diff: tests/test_account_side_filter.py showed 6 failures, and only because I checked out origin/main's config/accounts.yaml and re-ran the file did I establish the same 6 fail WITHOUT my change (missing pandas, pre-existing). A lane that did not think to baseline would have read them as its own regression, or -- the dangerous direction -- a lane whose change DID break something in the 149 uncollected files would have pushed on a green partial run. [!] I DID NOT DIAGNOSE THE CAUSE and am not asserting one: I did not check whether the deps are deliberately omitted (container size, install time) or simply never wired. That determination is the first thing the lane should make, because it decides which of the two clears_when branches applies. [!] SCOPE OF MY OBSERVATION, stated because it bounds the claim: one container, one date, one repo checkout. I did not test a second session. === UPDATE 2026-09-22, same session, after the gap ACTUALLY BIT THIS LANE. The filing above says 'I DID NOT DIAGNOSE THE CAUSE'. It has now been diagnosed, and the finding is SHARPER than first written. [!] IT IS NOT MERELY 'THE DEPS ARE NOT INSTALLED'. requirements-test.txt exists and documents the one-line fix, but INSTALLING IT IS NOT SUFFICIENT: after `pip install -r requirements-test.txt` the suite STILL reported 44 collection errors. Reaching zero additionally required ccxt, email_validator, fastapi and httpx -- fastapi IS listed in requirements-test.txt yet was absent, because pip hit 'Cannot uninstall PyJWT 2.7.0 / cryptography 41.0.7, RECORD file not found' (both debian-packaged in this image) and THAT ERROR SILENTLY ABORTED THE REST OF THE INSTALL LIST -- the swallow-your-own-failure shape the canonical rules name, occurring inside the very file meant to fix this. Both needed `pip install --ignore-installed`. MEASURED progression: 149 collection errors -> 48 after pandas/numpy -> 44 -> 35 -> 0 after ccxt+email_validator, ending at 17,991 tests collected with zero errors, which matches the CI population. So the suite IS runnable here once the REAL dep set is present. [!] AND IT IS NO LONGER HYPOTHETICAL. PR #12707 (this row's own R2 cut, four legs off real-money/prop rosters) was validated locally, everything runnable was green, it was pushed, and CI came back RED with 6 failures in tests/test_breakout_prop_wiring.py, tests/test_m15_eth_pullback_wiring.py and tests/test_stale_decision_routing.py -- every one a test that PINS THE EXACT ROSTERS THE DIFF CHANGED, and every one inside the uncollectible set. A TIER-3 REAL-MONEY ROSTER DIFF REACHED CI UNVALIDATED AGAINST THE TESTS WRITTEN TO GUARD IT. Here it cost one red cycle; the dangerous version is a lane reading 'green locally' as coverage and a reviewer believing it. [!] TWO FIXES, NOT EQUIVALENT: (a) make requirements-test.txt actually sufficient -- add the missing names and handle the debian-RECORD packages; (b) make a session UNABLE to mistake partial collection for coverage -- a SessionStart step running --collect-only and printing the error count, so the number is in front of a lane BEFORE it validates. (b) is the real fix, (a) is its precondition; (a) alone leaves the silent green intact for the next dep that drifts."}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 30, "clears_when": "EITHER comms/strategy_evidence/fvg_range_15m.json reports coverage_state != 'no_harness' and a Tier-3 decision is RECORDED on whether it returns to bybit_2 (re-adding it and leaving it off are equally valid closes), OR an operator/lane explicitly kills the leg with a stated reason so nobody re-asks.", "kind": "observation"}, "id": "PI-20260922-R2-FVG-RANGE-15M-CUT-FOR-NO-HARNESS-IS-REVERSIBLE-BY-BUILDING-ONE", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "session", "ref": "session_017aScqdVcFoXxqffMcsf5Ge, checklist row R2, branch claude/r2-cut-stage2-fleet", "rerun": "python3 scripts/ci/check_roster_promotion_evidence.py --population   # then: does fvg_range_15m still read coverage_state=no_harness? If a harness now routes to it, its Stage-0 verdict has never been taken and bybit_2 membership is re-decidable."}, "state": "queued", "what": "R2 cut fvg_range_15m from bybit_2 (real money) and from the bybit_portfolio mirror on 2026-09-22 under the operator instruction 'cut the legs that don't clear the bar'. THE REASON IS NOT LIKE THE OTHER THREE CUTS AND THIS ROW EXISTS SO THAT DISTINCTION SURVIVES. trend_donchian, eth_pullback_2h and eth_pullback_prop_2h were cut because RULE-D1-STAGE0-NET-OF-FULL-COST was APPLIED to their evidence and returned verdict 'fail' -- the rule was run and said no. fvg_range_15m failed ALL FOUR clauses because coverage_state=='no_harness': nothing routes it to a harness, so no number was ever produced and no rule was ever applied. Per comms/strategy_evidence/README.md and B1's own note that is A STATEMENT ABOUT US, NOT ABOUT THE STRATEGY. It is a correct cut under the plan ('nothing reaches a book without a committed evidence record'), but it is REVERSIBLE BY BUILDING THE HARNESS RATHER THAN BY THE STRATEGY IMPROVING -- and nothing else will ask that question, because once the leg is off the roster it leaves the --population census the guard prints. MEASURED 2026-09-22 by running scripts/ci/check_roster_promotion_evidence.py --population on f3746ab: 8/12 risk-bearing roster slots clear the four-clause bar; fvg_range_15m is the only one of the four failing on C1+C2+C3+C4 rather than C4 alone. The producer-side gap is named in PI-20260922-R1-REMAINING-CORPUS: regime_debt_matrix.classify() has no branch for the fvg_range family (scripts/backtest_fvg_range.py exists; nothing routes to it). THAT row builds the harness; THIS row is the one that comes back and asks the roster question afterwards, which that row's clears_when does not cover. The leg keeps execution: shadow in config/strategies.yaml (untouched) and keeps trading on bybit_1, the full-roster soak book, so evidence can still accrue against it. === UPDATE 2026-09-22, R2 lane, on commit 9036005d3. THIS ROW IS NEARLY ANSWERED AND IS DELIBERATELY NOT CLOSED YET. E25 (#12714) did exactly what this row asked -- added the regime_debt_matrix.classify() branch for the fvg_range family and RAN the harness -- and the manager, landing R2, wrote a comment into config/accounts.yaml saying the record now reads coverage_state: measured, fidelity: faithful, net_r_oos -10.2594, expectancy_r_oos -0.6035 over n=17, 0 of 4 folds positive, verdict `fail` against RULE-D1-STAGE0-NET-OF-FULL-COST. IF THAT LANDS, this row closes with the leg staying off bybit_2 on the SAME ground as the other three cuts -- the rule applied and said no -- and the 'reversible by building the harness' framing in this row's title and text is SUPERSEDED, not vindicated. [!] BUT IT IS NOT TRUE ON THE COMMITTED TREE YET, and this row must not be closed on a comment. VERIFIED by me on 9036005d3 AND on origin/main: #12714 is merged to NEITHER, and comms/strategy_evidence/fvg_range_15m.json reads coverage_state: no_harness with n_trades_oos: null and net_r_oos: null on BOTH. So the figures above are ANTICIPATED from #12714's branch, not measured on any tree a consumer reads. Closing this row on them would be exactly the 'a claim in a PR body is not a record' failure the plan names. [!] WHAT CLOSES IT, unchanged in substance and now with a named commit to check: re-read comms/strategy_evidence/fvg_range_15m.json ON MAIN once #12714 has merged. If it reads measured with verdict `fail`, close terminal -- the leg stays off and the reason has moved from 'we never looked' to 'we looked and it lost'. If #12714 does NOT land, or lands with a different verdict, this row stands as written and the accounts.yaml comment asserting those figures is itself the finding."}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 7, "clears_when": "No two open checklist rows describe the same work. ⚠️ Check by reading the four named pairs, not by a title grep -- C4/E7 have DIFFERENT titles ('...; replace the smoke fixture' vs not) and a title comparison would miss them, which is how they got here.", "kind": "observation"}, "id": "PI-20260922-CHECKLIST-CARRIES-FOUR-TASKS-TWICE", "next_action": "dispatch_lane", "origin": {"kind": "session", "ref": "Manager session_01XQhayAHWdrSYv8jN54P2HH, 2026-09-22, found while answering 'what is next for each lane' -- reading the queue end to end rather than row by row is what surfaced it.", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('docs/claude/work/MANAGER-CHECKLIST.json')); [print(r['id'], r['title']) for r in d['items'] if r['state'] in ('queued','blocked')]\" and read the notes, not just the titles."}, "routed_to": "Marked on all eight rows in the meantime, so the concrete harm (dispatching both) cannot happen by accident. The resolution is NOT a manager edit: the C/D rows come from docs/plans/OPERATING-PLAN-2026-09-21.md and the E/R rows were added later, so collapsing them decides which scheme is authoritative. Whoever takes one of a pair kills the twin with a stated reason in the same push.", "state": "routed", "what": "The checklist carries the same work under two numbering schemes: D1/R1 (flip the cost defaults and re-run the corpus), C4/E7 (wire the 9 orphan backtest harnesses), C5/R4 (write the first ten pre-registered research questions), C3/E6 (unblock replay-pregate). Eight rows, four pieces of work, and both halves render on the operator's page.", "why_it_matters": "It is not cosmetic. A manager reading the queue can dispatch both halves of a pair -- two lanes on one task, which at today's MEASURED rates is a $60-$435 mistake, and it produces two PRs racing on the same files. It also inflates the queue the operator is asked to read, which is the same failure E14 is about at a different scale."}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 30, "clears_when": "A deliberately-failed diag relay run whose comment names the step that actually failed. ⚠️ Verify with BOTH controls: break the VM leg and break the post-processing leg, and check the comment distinguishes them. A single failure mode proves nothing -- the current message is 'right' for one of the two by accident.", "kind": "observation"}, "id": "PI-20260922-DIAG-RELAY-ERROR-MESSAGE-NAMES-THE-WRONG-CAUSE", "next_action": "dispatch_lane", "origin": {"kind": "session", "ref": "E19 ops lane (session_0178qqSNhbW9vpQbREtc4drS), PR #12702, 2026-09-22. It read the failed job log rather than the workflow source, which is what surfaced this -- the manager's dispatch told it to, after the manager had itself published a wrong hypothesis from a workflow file the day before.", "rerun": "Open the auto-comment on a failed vm-diag-snapshot run and compare its named cause against the job log's actual failing step."}, "routed_to": "Small and bounded: the failure comment should distinguish WHERE the run died rather than assert a cause. The SSH/curl step succeeding is already observable in the job log, so the comment can say 'the VM round-trip completed; the failure is in post-processing' instead of pointing at secrets. ⚠️ Do NOT simply delete the secrets hint -- absent/mismatched secrets IS a real failure mode for this relay; the defect is asserting it without checking which step failed. Three states, not one: VM unreachable / VM reached and post-processing failed / could not determine.", "state": "routed", "what": "vm-diag-snapshot.yml's failure comment confidently names the WRONG cause. On run 35649903349 it said 'The request parsed fine, so this is an infrastructure failure' and suggested VM_SSH_KEY / DIAG_READ_TOKEN unset or mismatched. MEASURED from the job log by the E19 lane: the SSH+curl round-trip to the VM SUCCEEDED (6 lines returned, no curl/ssh error), and the run died one step later in pure local Python -- ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'scripts', because the issues-triggered path never ran actions/checkout. No secret was ever involved.", "why_it_matters": "An error message that names a wrong cause with confidence is worse than a generic one: it sends the reader to the one class of fix a session CANNOT perform (originating a secret value is operator-only), so the honest response to it is to stop and ask a human. It cost a real measurement -- the E16 lane could not read prop_status_request's cadence state and had to INFER which ask-arm fired -- and the batched path had been broken since 2026-09-13 (a45d360 added the scripts.* import with no checkout), nine days, with nobody diagnosing it because the message looked authoritative."}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 7, "clears_when": "`merge-claim-audit` no longer posts a red conclusion on a PR that did not touch it -- because it was retired with a stated reason, or because the `|| exit 1` was replaced by the could_not_check path its own header requires. [!] Re-pointing it at the archived board-pointer.json does NOT clear this: that is a green over a detector auditing a dead protocol.", "kind": "observation"}, "id": "PI-20260922-NW5KGJN8-0002", "next_action": "dispatch_lane", "origin": {"kind": "session", "ref": "Engineering lane session_014LSHstYGrXDTZnNW5kgjn8 (row E21), found while driving PR #12710. Run https://github.com/benbaichmankass/Metis-Insights/actions/runs/35699702783", "rerun": "git cat-file -e origin/main:docs/claude/board-pointer.json   # ABSENT == still broken. Or read any PR's merge-claim-audit check."}, "state": "queued", "what": "`merge-claim-audit` is STRUCTURALLY RED on every PR since the 2026-09-21 operating reset, for a reason no diff can fix. MEASURED 2026-09-22 from run 35699702783 (job 106654535066) on PR #12710: the job is `pull_request_target`, so it checks out MAIN (logged `git checkout -B main refs/remotes/origin/main`, HEAD 9eb13192b) and never touches the PR branch. Its first step is `python3 scripts/ci/board_pointer.py --self-test || exit 1`, which fails with 'the SHIPPING pointer parses (could_not_check -- docs/claude/board-pointer.json does not exist)'. VERIFIED independently: that path is ABSENT from origin/main (b6e87b2bb) and now lives at docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/registers/board-pointer.json -- the reset archived the coordination board and this workflow still reads it. [!] THE SHARP PART: the workflow's own header declares 'THREE STATES, NEVER COLLAPSED: claimed / unclaimed / could_not_check', and the step BELOW the self-test handles rc!=0 correctly, emitting `::warning::merge-claim-audit: could_not_check`. The `|| exit 1` on the self-test line kills the job before that branch is reached, so the three-state handling is UNREACHABLE and 'we could not look' renders as a hard red X. The workflow breaks the rule it documents. [!] NOT ESTABLISHED, left open rather than guessed: main's trigger is `pull_request_target: types: [closed]` only, yet this fired at 07:27:51Z on a ready_for_review on an OPEN PR. Establish the real trigger surface before changing it. [!] DISPOSITION IS PROBABLY 'RETIRE IT': the register it audits is archived and the session-start contract forbids recreating it, so re-pointing it at the archive would keep a detector alive for a protocol that no longer exists. The live replacement is the per-branch merge-slot claim. Killing it with a stated reason is likely right -- but that is a CI-owning lane's call, not E21's, and must not be a silent deletion.", "why_it_matters": "A check red on every PR for a reason no PR can fix teaches every session and the operator to skim past a red X -- the alarm-fatigue P1 CLAUDE.md names in its own right, and worse here because the next genuinely-red check on a live-money PR arrives into a feed where red is already normal. [!] IT ALREADY COST SOMETHING: this lane reported 'no red CI' on PR #12710 twice before finding it."}
