ss' framing in this row's title and text is SUPERSEDED, not vindicated. [!] BUT IT IS NOT TRUE ON THE COMMITTED TREE YET, and this row must not be closed on a comment. VERIFIED by me on 9036005d3 AND on origin/main: #12714 is merged to NEITHER, and comms/strategy_evidence/fvg_range_15m.json reads coverage_state: no_harness with n_trades_oos: null and net_r_oos: null on BOTH. So the figures above are ANTICIPATED from #12714's branch, not measured on any tree a consumer reads. Closing this row on them would be exactly the 'a claim in a PR body is not a record' failure the plan names. [!] WHAT CLOSES IT, unchanged in substance and now with a named commit to check: re-read comms/strategy_evidence/fvg_range_15m.json ON MAIN once #12714 has merged. If it reads measured with verdict `fail`, close terminal -- the leg stays off and the reason has moved from 'we never looked' to 'we looked and it lost'. If #12714 does NOT land, or lands with a different verdict, this row stands as written and the accounts.yaml comment asserting those figures is itself the finding. === CLOSED 2026-09-22, R2 lane, ON THE COMMITTED TREE AND NOT ON A COMMENT. E25 (#12714) LANDED TO MAIN as f783ae1c3 and was merged into this branch, which is the ordering this row's previous update asked for. VERIFIED BY ME by reading comms/strategy_evidence/fvg_range_15m.json on the merged tree -- not from the PR body, not from the manager's comment: coverage_state `measured`, fidelity `faithful`, n_trades_oos 17, net_r_oos -10.2594, expectancy_r_oos -0.6035, decision_rule RULE-D1-STAGE0-NET-OF-FULL-COST (registered 2026-09-22T05:34:08Z, strictly before the run) verdict `fail`. THE QUESTION THIS ROW EXISTED TO ASK IS ANSWERED: the harness was built, it ran, and the leg LOST. The cut stands, and its reason has MOVED -- from 'nothing ever graded it', a statement about us, to 'it was graded and it failed', a statement about the strategy. Same ground as the other three R2 cuts. This row's own title ('REVERSIBLE BY BUILDING ONE') is SUPERSEDED rather than vindicated: the harness now exists, so the cheap reversal path is spent and any re-promotion needs a NEW measurement clearing the four-clause bar, not a new tool. Anyone reading that title later should read this closure first. [!] ONE FIGURE IS STILL UNBACKED and is deliberately not carried as measured: the record has folds_positive 0 with folds_total NULL, so the widely-quoted '0 of 4 folds positive' has no committed denominator. Quote it as '0 of an unstated fold count' until the producer emits a total. Not worth its own row -- 0 positive folds is adverse at any denominator -- but it should not be re-quoted as '0 of 4'. [!] NOT CLOSED BY THIS ROW, still live: PI-20260922-R1-REMAINING-CORPUS carries the remaining ~43 legs of the full live roster, whose evidence records E25 does not touch."}
{"id": "PI-20260922-R1-REMAINING-CORPUS", "state": "routed", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "session", "ref": "session_016FkzGuob8CJMJgqYRddvSV, checklist row R1, PR on branch claude/r1-d1-cost-corpus-rerun", "rerun": "python3 scripts/ops/build_strategy_evidence.py --out comms/strategy_evidence   # every enabled leg not yet covered; cross-check against scripts/ci/check_roster_promotion_evidence.py --population for the risk-bearing subset"}, "due_when": {"kind": "observation", "check_every_days": 7, "clears_when": "Every enabled leg in config/strategies.yaml has a comms/strategy_evidence/<leg>.json with coverage_state==measured (or a named no_harness/harness_failed reason) carrying a cost_stack and decision_rule block, OR is explicitly dropped with a stated reason.  -- CHECKED ON MAIN, never on a branch."}, "what": "R1 (re-run the backtest corpus with cost defaults on) covered only the 12-leg risk-bearing promotion-evidence population (11 of 12 measured; fvg_range_15m still no_harness), not the full ~55-leg live roster R1's original note scoped. MEASURED 2026-09-22 by running scripts/ops/build_strategy_evidence.py --dry-run against config/strategies.yaml: the remaining legs still need a classify()+run pass with the same extended producer (cost_stack + decision_rule fields, this session's scripts/ops/build_strategy_evidence.py diff). fvg_range_15m specifically needs a regime_debt_matrix.classify() branch for the fvg_range family (backtest_fvg_range.py exists; nothing routes to it) before it can be measured at all -- that is a separate, smaller fix from the corpus re-run itself. Also out of scope here: the three harnesses E3 filed as having no bps cost model (backtest_orb.py, src/backtest/backtester.py, xsec_momentum's funding term -- PI-20260921-E3-* rows), which this producer does not reach at all (regime_debt_matrix only classifies trend/squeeze/pullback). === UPDATE 2026-09-22, R5 lane (checklist row R7, session_01W2KHBwLh4ouP1b8De2bmfV), on branch claude/r5-remaining-corpus-cost-stack cut from 0e0a8f3. THE WORK IS DONE AND THIS ROW IS DELIBERATELY NOT CLOSED, for the reason PI-20260922-R2-FVG-RANGE-15M-CUT-FOR-NO-HARNESS-IS-REVERSIBLE-BY-BUILDING-ONE states in terms: a row must not close on an unmerged tree. WHAT RAN: scripts/ops/build_strategy_evidence.py over the 40 legs R1 did not cover, with --workdir comms/strategy_evidence/runs/2026-09-22-r5 so every source_run names a COMMITTED file rather than the dead /tmp path PI-20260922-EVIDENCE-SOURCE-RUN-IS-A-TMP-PATH records. RESULT, read off the producer's own tail: 30 measured / 9 no_harness / 1 harness_failed / 0 not_attempted. Of the 30 measured, 25 pass and 5 fail RULE-D1-STAGE0-NET-OF-FULL-COST (mes_trend_long_1d, spy_pullback_1h, squeeze_breakout_4h, tlt_pullback_1d, tlt_pullback_1h). [!] THIS ROW'S clears_when IS SATISFIED ON THE BRANCH AND I RAN THE PREDICATE RATHER THAN ASSERTING IT: all 52 enabled legs in config/strategies.yaml now have a record that either carries cost_stack + a decision_rule verdict (42 = R1's 12 + this lane's 30) or names a no_harness/harness_failed reason (10); the predicate returns an empty list on this branch. WHAT CLOSES THIS ROW: run the same predicate ON MAIN once this lane's PR has merged. [!] POPULATION CORRECTION: the 40 are 30 measured + 9 no_harness + 1 harness_failed, NOT the 31 + 9 this row and the dispatch both carried. splg_trend_long_1d is harness_failed -- yfinance==1.7.0 returns zero rows for SPLG while the SPY control in the SAME call returned rows, so the venue is up and the gap is SPLG-specific. Filed as PI-20260922-R5-SPLG-FEED-RESOLVES-TO-ZERO-ROWS. [!] AND THIS ROW'S PREMISE IS PARTLY WRONG, which matters more than the counts: the 40 records were NOT pre-cost numbers. MEASURED three ways -- config_fingerprint unchanged on 40/40; three legs (fade_breakout_4h, uso_trend_1h, tlt_pullback_1h) byte-identical old->new on net_r_oos despite a 9-day window shift, which a fee-only->full-cost change makes impossible (their fee-only gaps are +1.47R, +1.65R and +9.35R); and git log --since=2026-09-10 over backtest_trend/pullback/squeeze + regime_debt_matrix returns two commits, neither touching cost resolution. The 2026-09-13/16 numbers were ALREADY net of the full stack; what was missing was the RECORD saying so and any rule being applied to it. Nobody should quote R1's '3 of 11 flipped' as a corpus before/after -- it is a fee-only vs full-cost counterfactual inside one run. Full write-up: docs/research/R5-REMAINING-CORPUS-COST-STACK-2026-09-22.md. [!] STILL OUT OF SCOPE AND UNTOUCHED: the nine no_harness legs (row E28) and the three harnesses with no bps cost model at all (PI-20260921-E3-* rows).", "routed_to": "checklist row R7, branch claude/r5-remaining-corpus-cost-stack (the R5 lane)"}
{"id": "PI-20260922-R5-STAGE0-VERDICT-FLIPS-ON-A-9-DAY-WINDOW-SHIFT", "state": "queued", "next_action": "dispatch_lane", "origin": {"kind": "session", "ref": "session_01W2KHBwLh4ouP1b8De2bmfV, checklist row R7, branch claude/r5-remaining-corpus-cost-stack", "rerun": "python3 scripts/ops/build_strategy_evidence.py --strategy squeeze_breakout_4h --strategy trend_donchian_sol   # then compare net_r_oos and decision_rule.verdict against the committed record and against comms/strategy_evidence/runs/2026-09-22-r5/*__trades.jsonl. A verdict that differs again while config_fingerprint is unchanged means the instability is still present."}, "due_when": {"kind": "observation", "check_every_days": 14, "clears_when": "EITHER build_strategy_evidence.py pins the measurement window so a re-run on a later date is reproducible (a fixed --end, or the window recorded and honoured), OR decision_rule carries a power/precision clause (a minimum n, or an interval that a near-zero net_r_oos fails to clear) so a leg inside the noise band returns a distinct third state instead of a crisp pass/fail, OR D1's owner records a DECIDED that a date-sensitive verdict is acceptable and says why."}, "what": "MEASURED 2026-09-22 by the R5 lane by re-running scripts/ops/build_strategy_evidence.py over the 40 legs R1 had not covered and diffing every record against its committed predecessor. POPULATION: the 30 legs that graded `measured` in both the committed run (2026-09-13/16) and this one (2026-09-22). RESULT: config_fingerprint is IDENTICAL on 40 of 40, so no config moved, and the cost code did not change in the window (git log --since=2026-09-10 over backtest_trend/pullback/squeeze + regime_debt_matrix = two commits, neither touching cost resolution) -- yet 2 of the 30 FLIPPED THE SIGN OF THEIR VERDICT on nothing but a 9-day shift of the rolling 365-day window. squeeze_breakout_4h: +0.5994 `pass` -> -0.4874 `fail` (n 9->10). trend_donchian_sol: -1.0697 `fail` -> +2.8045 `pass` (n 56->57). [!] WHY THIS IS A DEFECT AND NOT MERELY VARIANCE: window_days=365 is measured BACKWARD FROM THE RUN DATE, so the same leg with the same config is a different measurement every day, and nothing in the record says so -- a consumer reading decision_rule.verdict cannot tell a robust pass from one that read fail last week. RULE-D1-STAGE0-NET-OF-FULL-COST is a strict `net_r_oos > 0` test with no minimum-n and no interval, so a leg sitting near zero gets a crisp verdict out of what is, at these n, noise. squeeze_breakout_4h sits at n=10, exactly the producer's own MIN_TRADES_FOR_POOLED floor, and it is `execution: live`. [!] THIS IS NOT A REQUEST TO WIDEN OR LOWER THE BAR and must not be read as one -- the canonical rule against moving a floor to manufacture a verdict stands, and the fix runs the other way: make the instrument REPRODUCIBLE, and make `inside the noise band` a state the record can express, per CLAUDE-RULES-CANONICAL 'Collapsed states'. [!] IT BLOCKS ACTING ON THE TWO OPERATOR QUESTIONS THIS LANE ANSWERED: trend_donchian_sol_4h (+4.1648, n=34, down from +8.9882 nine days earlier) and htf_pullback_trend_2h (+2.7979, n=80) both pass, and neither should be promoted while the verdict that would authorize it moves this much. Write-up: docs/research/R5-REMAINING-CORPUS-COST-STACK-2026-09-22.md FINDING 2 and proposal P1."}
{"id": "PI-20260922-R5-CLAUDE-MD-FEE-ONLY-CORPUS-CLAIM-IS-STALE-FOR-FOUR-HARNESS-FAMILIES", "state": "queued", "next_action": "ask_operator", "origin": {"kind": "session", "ref": "session_01W2KHBwLh4ouP1b8De2bmfV, checklist row R7, branch claude/r5-remaining-corpus-cost-stack", "rerun": "sed -n '72,82p' scripts/backtest_trend.py   # does main() still resolve unset --slippage/--funding to venue-aware defaults?  then:  python3 -c \"import json;d=json.load(open('comms/strategy_evidence/htf_pullback_trend_2h.json'));print(d['cost_stack'], d['net_r_oos'], d['net_r_oos_fee_only'])\"   # a non-null cost_stack with slippage and funding > 0 is the corpus contradicting the CLAUDE.md sentence"}, "due_when": {"kind": "observation", "check_every_days": 7, "clears_when": "EITHER CLAUDE.md's 'The real-money promotion mandate does not arm until D1 lands' paragraph is corrected to scope its fee-only claim to the harness families that actually lack a bps cost model (backtest_orb.py, src/backtest/backtester.py, xsec_momentum's funding term), OR the operator records a DECIDED that the arming block stands for a different stated reason, OR D1 lands and the paragraph is retired with it."}, "what": "FOUND 2026-09-22 by the R5 lane while regrading the 40 uncovered evidence records. CLAUDE.md states, as the condition blocking the real-money promotion mandate: 'the harnesses default slippage and funding to 0.0, so today's corpus is fee-only and optimistic by an unknown amount (+0.57R on the one leg measured)'. MEASURED: that is FALSE for the four families scripts/ops/build_strategy_evidence.py reaches (trend / pullback / squeeze / fvg_range). Their CLI path resolves unset --slippage/--funding to the venue-aware defaults before writing the --json summary (scripts/backtest_trend.py:74-76), so all 42 legs now carrying a cost_stack show fees 7.5 / slippage 5.0 / funding 1.0 bps and a net_r_oos already net of all three -- and net_r_oos_fee_only, now on the record, makes the size of the correction readable PER LEG (it ranges from +0.04R on scha_trend_long_1d to +9.35R on tlt_pullback_1h), so 'optimistic by an unknown amount' is no longer true either. The MODULE GLOBALS do default to 0.0, which is what the sentence is reading, but that path is taken only by a DIRECT run_backtest() caller (the lever unit tests) and by no evidence-producing run -- 'read the field, not the prose about it', one level in. [!] IT IS STILL TRUE for the three harnesses E3 filed as having no bps cost model at all: backtest_orb.py, src/backtest/backtester.py and xsec_momentum's funding term (PI-20260921-E3-* rows). So the paragraph is not wrong so much as UNSCOPED, and the fix is a scope, not a deletion. [!] WHY I FILED IT INSTEAD OF EDITING IT, stated so the next reader does not treat the omission as an oversight: that sentence is the stated ARMING CONDITION for a mandate that routes REAL MONEY. Correcting the evidence is a research lane's job; deciding what arms that mandate is not, and a lane quietly rewriting its own gating condition is the exact shape the tier boundary exists to refuse. OPERATOR/MANAGER DECISION. Write-up: docs/research/R5-REMAINING-CORPUS-COST-STACK-2026-09-22.md FINDING 1."}
{"id": "PI-20260922-R5-SPLG-FEED-RESOLVES-TO-ZERO-ROWS", "state": "queued", "next_action": "dispatch_lane", "origin": {"kind": "session", "ref": "session_01W2KHBwLh4ouP1b8De2bmfV, checklist row R7, branch claude/r5-remaining-corpus-cost-stack", "rerun": "python3 scripts/ops/build_strategy_evidence.py --strategy splg_trend_long_1d   # a coverage_state other than 'harness_failed' means the feed resolves again"}, "due_when": {"kind": "observation", "check_every_days": 14, "clears_when": "EITHER comms/strategy_evidence/splg_trend_long_1d.json reports coverage_state == 'measured' with a cost_stack and a decision_rule verdict, OR the symbol map is corrected / a substitute feed is wired and the record names which, OR the leg is explicitly dropped from config/strategies.yaml with a stated reason so nobody re-asks."}, "what": "MEASURED 2026-09-22 by the R5 lane: splg_trend_long_1d is the ONLY leg of the 40 regraded that came back `harness_failed`, and the failure is a data gap, not a code defect. The record's own error field carries a POSITIVE CONTROL rather than a bare failure: 'yfinance returned no rows for SPLG (yfinance==1.7.0); venue_state=serving -- the SPY control returned 5 rows, so the venue is up and this is specific to SPLG (delisting, a short listing history, or a wrong symbol map). NOT a dependency problem.' So this is NOT the MI-287 yfinance-resolution class that cost 23 of 52 legs in September -- that floor is satisfied at 1.7.0 and the same call served SPY. [!] WHY IT MATTERS MORE THAN ONE ungraded leg: splg_trend_long_1d is one of the TWO declared affordability proxies carved out of the Alpaca mirror equality invariant in CLAUDE.md, so it is a leg the roster reasons about BY NAME, and it now has NO gradeable offline evidence at all -- not a pass, not a fail, and not even a no_harness (a harness exists and routes; it ran and could not get data). Under the promotion ladder that means it can never leave Stage 0, and unlike the E28 family nobody has a row asking why. [!] harness_failed IS NOT `no edge` AND IS NOT `no harness` -- three states, never collapsed. Do not read the null net_r_oos as a zero or as a verdict about the strategy. Write-up: docs/research/R5-REMAINING-CORPUS-COST-STACK-2026-09-22.md, proposal P5."}
{"id": "PI-20260922-R5-PATH-STATS-MISSING-FOR-THE-12-LEGS-WHOSE-SERIES-IS-IN-A-DEAD-TMP-DIR", "state": "queued", "next_action": "ask_operator", "origin": {"kind": "session", "ref": "session_01W2KHBwLh4ouP1b8De2bmfV, checklist row R7, branch claude/r5-remaining-corpus-cost-stack", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json,glob,os; print([json.load(open(p))['strategy'] for p in glob.glob('comms/strategy_evidence/*.json') if json.load(open(p)).get('coverage_state')=='measured' and not json.load(open(p)).get('path_stats')])\"   # every measured leg still carrying no path_stats; empty list == closed"}, "due_when": {"kind": "observation", "check_every_days": 7, "clears_when": "EITHER every `measured` record carries a path_stats block (which requires re-running the 12 legs whose source_run is a dead /tmp path, and R8/B6 accepting that their net_r_oos will move on the window shift), OR R8/B6 records a DECIDED that the prop half is settled without a path statistic for breakout_1's two legs and says on what basis."}, "what": "MEASURED 2026-09-22 by the R5/R7 lane, after B6 asked for the per-trade OOS R series. GOOD NEWS FIRST: the series did not need a re-run -- this lane had already committed the per-trade rows under comms/strategy_evidence/runs/2026-09-22-r5/, so path_stats (max peak-to-trough drawdown in R, worst single exit-day R sum, worst/best trade, final and max equity) was computed from committed data and written into all 30 records this lane produced, cross-checked against each record (final_equity_r == net_r_oos and n == n_trades_oos on 30 of 30). [!] THE GAP: of 52 records, 30 now carry path_stats and 22 do not, and the 22 split into TWO DIFFERENT STATES that must not be collapsed. 10 have NO SERIES AT ALL (9 no_harness + 1 harness_failed) -- nothing was ever emitted. 12 have a series that WAS emitted and is now UNREACHABLE, because source_run points into a /tmp dir that no longer exists (R1's 11 + E25's 1). [!] AND THE 12 ARE EXACTLY THE WRONG 12: every risk-bearing roster leg is among them, INCLUDING trend_donchian_sol_prop and trend_donchian_eth_prop -- the two legs on breakout_1, which is the account B6 exists to decide. So P(target before floor) is not computable for the prop book today, and the reason is a provenance defect rather than anything about the strategies. [!] THIS IS THE SECOND AND LARGER COST OF PI-20260922-EVIDENCE-SOURCE-RUN-IS-A-TMP-PATH, and it was not visible when that row was filed as a provenance defect: a dead locator does not merely stop someone RE-CHECKING a number, it stops any NEW question being asked of the old measurement. Every future statistic over those 12 costs a full re-run. [!] WHY THIS LANE DID NOT JUST RE-RUN THEM, stated so the next owner can overturn it rather than re-derive it: re-running fixes the locators and produces the path stats in one pass, but PI-20260922-R5-STAGE0-VERDICT-FLIPS-ON-A-9-DAY-WINDOW-SHIFT shows the net_r_oos figures WILL move, and those 12 records are the ones R2 already acted on (four roster cuts) and R8 is about to read. Silently replacing evidence mid-decision is worse than the stated gap. OPERATOR / R8 / B6 CALL. Write-up: docs/research/R5-REMAINING-CORPUS-COST-STACK-2026-09-22.md FINDING 4."}
{"id": "PI-20260922-R1-REMAINING-CORPUS", "state": "done", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "session", "ref": "session_016FkzGuob8CJMJgqYRddvSV, checklist row R1, PR on branch claude/r1-d1-cost-corpus-rerun", "rerun": "python3 scripts/ops/build_strategy_evidence.py --out comms/strategy_evidence   # every enabled leg not yet covered; cross-check against scripts/ci/check_roster_promotion_evidence.py --population for the risk-bearing subset"}, "due_when": {"kind": "observation", "check_every_days": 7, "clears_when": "Every enabled leg in config/strategies.yaml has a comms/strategy_evidence/<leg>.json with coverage_state==measured (or a named no_harness/harness_failed reason) carrying a cost_stack and decision_rule block, OR is explicitly dropped with a stated reason.  -- CHECKED ON MAIN, never on a branch."}, "what": "R1 (re-run the backtest corpus with cost defaults on) covered only the 12-leg risk-bearing promotion-evidence population (11 of 12 measured; fvg_range_15m still no_harness), not the full ~55-leg live roster R1's original note scoped. MEASURED 2026-09-22 by running scripts/ops/build_strategy_evidence.py --dry-run against config/strategies.yaml: the remaining legs still need a classify()+run pass with the same extended producer (cost_stack + decision_rule fields, this session's scripts/ops/build_strategy_evidence.py diff). fvg_range_15m specifically needs a regime_debt_matrix.classify() branch for the fvg_range family (backtest_fvg_range.py exists; nothing routes to it) before it can be measured at all -- that is a separate, smaller fix from the corpus re-run itself. Also out of scope here: the three harnesses E3 filed as having no bps cost model (backtest_orb.py, src/backtest/backtester.py, xsec_momentum's funding term -- PI-20260921-E3-* rows), which this producer does not reach at all (regime_debt_matrix only classifies trend/squeeze/pullback). === UPDATE 2026-09-22, R5 lane (checklist row R7, session_01W2KHBwLh4ouP1b8De2bmfV), on branch claude/r5-remaining-corpus-cost-stack cut from 0e0a8f3. THE WORK IS DONE AND THIS ROW IS DELIBERATELY NOT CLOSED, for the reason PI-20260922-R2-FVG-RANGE-15M-CUT-FOR-NO-HARNESS-IS-REVERSIBLE-BY-BUILDING-ONE states in terms: a row must not close on an unmerged tree. WHAT RAN: scripts/ops/build_strategy_evidence.py over the 40 legs R1 did not cover, with --workdir comms/strategy_evidence/runs/2026-09-22-r5 so every source_run names a COMMITTED file rather than the dead /tmp path PI-20260922-EVIDENCE-SOURCE-RUN-IS-A-TMP-PATH records. RESULT, read off the producer's own tail: 30 measured / 9 no_harness / 1 harness_failed / 0 not_attempted. Of the 30 measured, 25 pass and 5 fail RULE-D1-STAGE0-NET-OF-FULL-COST (mes_trend_long_1d, spy_pullback_1h, squeeze_breakout_4h, tlt_pullback_1d, tlt_pullback_1h). [!] THIS ROW'S clears_when IS SATISFIED ON THE BRANCH AND I RAN THE PREDICATE RATHER THAN ASSERTING IT: all 52 enabled legs in config/strategies.yaml now have a record that either carries cost_stack + a decision_rule verdict (42 = R1's 12 + this lane's 30) or names a no_harness/harness_failed reason (10); the predicate returns an empty list on this branch. WHAT CLOSES THIS ROW: run the same predicate ON MAIN once this lane's PR has merged. [!] POPULATION CORRECTION: the 40 are 30 measured + 9 no_harness + 1 harness_failed, NOT the 31 + 9 this row and the dispatch both carried. splg_trend_long_1d is harness_failed -- yfinance==1.7.0 returns zero rows for SPLG while the SPY control in the SAME call returned rows, so the venue is up and the gap is SPLG-specific. Filed as PI-20260922-R5-SPLG-FEED-RESOLVES-TO-ZERO-ROWS. [!] AND THIS ROW'S PREMISE IS PARTLY WRONG, which matters more than the counts: the 40 records were NOT pre-cost numbers. MEASURED three ways -- config_fingerprint unchanged on 40/40; three legs (fade_breakout_4h, uso_trend_1h, tlt_pullback_1h) byte-identical old->new on net_r_oos despite a 9-day window shift, which a fee-only->full-cost change makes impossible (their fee-only gaps are +1.47R, +1.65R and +9.35R); and git log --since=2026-09-10 over backtest_trend/pullback/squeeze + regime_debt_matrix returns two commits, neither touching cost resolution. The 2026-09-13/16 numbers were ALREADY net of the full stack; what was missing was the RECORD saying so and any rule being applied to it. Nobody should quote R1's '3 of 11 flipped' as a corpus before/after -- it is a fee-only vs full-cost counterfactual inside one run. Full write-up: docs/research/R5-REMAINING-CORPUS-COST-STACK-2026-09-22.md. [!] STILL OUT OF SCOPE AND UNTOUCHED: the nine no_harness legs (row E28) and the three harnesses with no bps cost model at all (PI-20260921-E3-* rows).  === CLOSED 2026-09-22, terminal. See terminal_reason: the predicate was RUN against origin/main at f50a25928, not asserted.", "routed_to": "checklist row R7, branch claude/r5-remaining-corpus-cost-stack (the R5 lane)", "terminal_reason": "clears_when SATISFIED and CHECKED ON MAIN at f50a25928 (#12720 merged), not on a branch and not on a PR body. All 52 enabled legs carry a record: 42 measured with a cost_stack and a RULE-D1-STAGE0-NET-OF-FULL-COST verdict, 9 no_harness, 1 harness_failed. The predicate in origin.rerun returns an empty list. What this row does NOT close, stated so the closure is not read as wider than it is: the 9 no_harness legs still have no harness (row E28), splg_trend_long_1d still cannot be graded (PI-20260922-R5-SPLG-FEED-RESOLVES-TO-ZERO-ROWS), the 12 legs R1/E25 wrote still name a dead /tmp source_run so no path statistic can be derived for them (PI-20260922-R5-PATH-STATS-MISSING-FOR-THE-12-LEGS-WHOSE-SERIES-IS-IN-A-DEAD-TMP-DIR), and the Stage-0 verdict remains date-sensitive (PI-20260922-R5-STAGE0-VERDICT-FLIPS-ON-A-9-DAY-WINDOW-SHIFT). This row asked for a complete corpus and the corpus is complete; it never asked for those four."}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 14, "clears_when": "Either the guard exists and its baseline is declared and shrinking, or the operator has decided not to build it and that decision is recorded. ⚠️ The count falling on its own does NOT clear this: the row is about the MISSING DETECTOR, not about the 40 paths. Re-running the probe and finding fewer is evidence lanes are sweeping by hand, which is the cost this exists to remove.", "kind": "observation"}, "id": "PI-20260922-ARCHIVED-REGISTERS-STILL-READ-BY-40-PATHS-ACROSS-LIVE-CI-SOURCES", "next_action": "dispatch_lane", "origin": {"kind": "session", "ref": "E29 build lane (session_01AX7C1vHqCEnh4EyqLnm5p8), PR #12719, 2026-09-22", "rerun": "Re-run the archived-and-still-referenced probe, which is the whole measurement: `git diff --no-renames --diff-filter=D --name-only 3ea2777^1 HEAD` for the archived set (⚠️ --no-renames is load-bearing: the reset RENAMED files into docs/archive/, so plain --diff-filter=D returns ZERO and the probe reports a false all-clear), then grep each surviving path across .github/workflows, scripts/ci, scripts/ops and .claude/skills. Positive control: docs/claude/board-pointer.json must appear in the archived set."}, "state": "queued", "what": "MEASURED 2026-09-22 (E29 lane, PR #12719). 289 paths were archived by the 2026-09-21 operating reset and are absent from the tree; 40 of them are STILL NAMED by live sources under .github/workflows, scripts/ci, scripts/ops and .claude/skills. Heaviest fan-out: health-review-backlog.json (31 namers), OPEN-ITEMS.json (25), ml-review-backlog.json (25), performance-review-backlog.json (23), SESSIONS.json (16), research-review-backlog.json (15), board-pointer.json (12). ⚠️ THIS IS NOT THE 137-PATH NUMBER AND MUST NOT BE CONFLATED WITH IT. A raw 'path literal that does not exist' scan is NOT a usable detector: re-running that shape produced 216 and was dominated by noise -- an extension-ordering bug splitting .jsonl as .json, self-test fixture literals, docstring prose, and files the naming workflow PRODUCES (the econ-calendar and macro backfills). The decidable signal is narrower: a path the reset ARCHIVED that a live source still names. PROPOSED REMEDY: one guard keyed on archived-and-still-referenced, shipped REPORT-ONLY against a declared baseline that shrinks -- never blocking, because 40 paths across ~60 files would go red on every PR at once and a guard nobody can go green against gets waived rather than satisfied. WHAT IT WOULD HAVE CAUGHT on 2026-09-21: E23 (health-review's hard completion gate, plus its three unfixed sibling skills), E27, and all three of E29's workflows -- four instances found independently by four lanes in one day.", "why_it_matters": "The reset archived registers and nothing swept their readers. That single defect has now surfaced FOUR times in one day, each time costing a lane most of a session to rediscover from a red check or an unsatisfiable skill gate -- and each lane fixed only its own instance. Without a detector the remaining 40 keep surfacing one at a time, and the ones behind dormant workflows (the board family) will not surface at all until somebody dispatches them."}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 7, "clears_when": "Zero open PRs with an automation/reconcile-open-prs-* head ref, each closed one carrying a recorded disposition. ⚠️ `killed` is the expected terminal shape here, not `done`.", "kind": "observation"}, "id": "PI-20260922-RECONCILE-OPEN-PRS-14-STRANDED-PRS-NEED-CLOSING-NOT-MERGING", "next_action": "dispatch_lane", "origin": {"kind": "session", "ref": "E29 build lane (session_01AX7C1vHqCEnh4EyqLnm5p8), PR #12719, 2026-09-22", "rerun": "List open PRs and filter head refs matching automation/reconcile-open-prs-*. Re-verify the two claims before acting: `git diff --stat main <branch>` for the revert risk, and diff the branch's docs/claude/work/OPEN-PRS.json against docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/work/OPEN-PRS.json for the payload claim. ⚠️ Do not close on the strength of this text -- the archive comparison is the reason it is safe."}, "state": "queued", "what": "14 open automation/reconcile-open-prs-* PRs (#12451…#12511, opened 2026-09-17T15:30Z … 2026-09-18T04:47Z) are stranded by a workflow E29 retired in PR #12719. THEY MUST BE CLOSED UNMERGED, and it is measured rather than assumed: (1) every branch is cut from a PRE-RESET main -- `git diff --stat main <branch>` gives 1,055 files / 315,876 deletions on the newest (#12511) and 1,190 / 394,616 on the oldest (#12451), so merging ANY ONE reverts the operating reset wholesale; (2) nothing is lost -- their only payload is docs/claude/work/OPEN-PRS.json (the job's `paths:` names that file alone) and it is strictly STALER than the archived copy (branch 9 open / 114 settled vs archive 12 / 118; the one number the branch has that the archive's open_prs lacks, #12400, sits in the archive's settled_prs, i.e. already reconciled); (3) the known drop hazard does NOT apply -- sweep_stale_automation_prs.py warns that closing a WORK-DIGEST PR drops queued pending-pings.jsonl rows, and these carry no ping payload. Each close should record a disposition: open_pr_record.py grades a closed_unmerged row with none as `undispositioned`.", "why_it_matters": "These are not inert. They are open, mergeable-looking PRs whose merge would silently revert the 2026-09-21 operating reset -- the single largest change on main this cycle. Leaving 14 of them in the queue is a live footgun for any session or automation that merges by label, by age or in bulk, and it is also 14 of the 50 open PRs obscuring the real queue."}
{"id": "PI-20260922-E27-MERGED-BUT-NO-ALLOWLISTED-ACTION-CAN-RESTART-THE-WEB-API", "state": "queued", "next_action": "dispatch_lane", "what": "E27 MERGED (#12729, 2026-09-22) AND CANNOT BE DEPLOYED, so the row it closes cannot close. Its change adds `ssh.service` to `_CANONICAL_UNITS` in src/web/api/routers/diag.py — code served by `ict-web-api.service`. MEASURED 2026-09-22 against the allowlist: `restart-bot-service` wraps scripts/ops/restart_bot.sh, whose `UNIT=\"ict-trader-live.service\"` — it restarts THE TRADER, not the web API. `scripts/ops/restart_web_api.sh` EXISTS in the repo and names ict-web-api.service four times, but grep finds it in NEITHER docs/claude/system-actions.md NOR .github/workflows/system-actions.yml, so there is no dispatch path for it. [!] THE SHAPE OF THIS IS THE POINT AND IT GENERALISES BEYOND E27: any merged change to the web API's own code is UNDEPLOYABLE by a session today. `ict-git-sync` pulls main every ~5 min, so the FILE reaches the VM and the RUNNING PROCESS keeps the old code — which is precisely the merged-vs-deployed collapse E31 exists to make visible, arriving here as a concrete stranded change rather than a principle. [!] AND THE WORKAROUNDS ARE WORSE THAN THE GAP, stated so a later session does not reach for one: `reboot-vm` restarts everything including the live trader and needs a sudoers entry that may not exist; `fix-data-dir` and `set-env` restart the web API as a SIDE EFFECT of doing something else, so using either as a restart vector means dispatching a mutation for its side effect — exactly the kind of off-label use the allowlist exists to prevent. Neither should be used for this. [!] WHAT THE FIX IS: add `restart-web-api` to the allowlist the way § 2 requires — the doc, the workflow's `inputs.action.options`, the wrapper mapping in system-actions.yml, and the priority case. Tier 2, single unit, restart-only, and the script it wraps already exists and already reads its pre/post is-active state.", "origin": {"kind": "session", "ref": "manager (session_01XQhayAHWdrSYv8jN54P2HH), 2026-09-22, found while trying to deploy E27 after merging #12729", "rerun": "Two greps and one read, all cheap. (1) `grep -rn 'restart.web.api' docs/claude/system-actions.md .github/workflows/system-actions.yml` — an empty result is the finding. POSITIVE CONTROL, which is what makes the empty result trustworthy: the same grep for 'restart-bot-service' MUST return hits in both files; if it does not, the probe is broken, not the allowlist. (2) `grep -n '^UNIT=' scripts/ops/restart_bot.sh` must still read ict-trader-live.service. (3) confirm scripts/ops/restart_web_api.sh still exists."}, "due_when": {"kind": "observation", "check_every_days": 7, "clears_when": "`restart-web-api` is dispatchable through the system-actions allowlist AND has been run once against ict-web-api.service with its post-state read back. ⚠️ The row it unblocks (E27) needs a SECOND, separate observation to close: the diag probe that returns HTTP 400 `unknown_unit` for journalctl?unit=ssh.service today returning 200 WITH journal lines. Do not read the restart landing as E27 closing — that is the merged/deployed/observed collapse this row is about."}}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 14, "clears_when": "EITHER assess/starved_account_alert can report a fan-out-caused starvation (an account present in rounds_planned and absent from rounds_written) as a distinct, alertable state -- NOT by folding it into starved_count, which is a different population -- OR a lane/operator explicitly kills the row with a stated reason, which ARBITRATION_FANOUT_MODE=annotate (E33 option (c)) would be a valid ground for, since it removes the mechanism that produces the unreportable state.", "kind": "observation"}, "id": "PI-20260922-FANOUT-STARVATION-INVISIBLE-TO-ASSESS", "next_action": "check_observation", "origin": {"kind": "session", "ref": "session_01W2xuf1sayMSq7hP8f1n5Vr, checklist row E33, branch lane/e33-per-account-arbitration-scope", "rerun": "Re-take ONE diag read with issue_write working: log_file?name=arbitration_fanout_soak&lines=200 as the ONLY path in the issue. Count rows where set(rounds_planned[].accounts) strictly contains set(rounds_written[].accounts) while starved_count == 0. Any such row is an instance."}, "state": "queued", "what": "arbitration_fanout.assess grades `starved` against the GLOBAL election and knows nothing of ARBITRATION_FANOUT_ACCOUNTS, so starvation CAUSED BY THE FAN-OUT ITSELF is invisible to starved_accounts, starved_count and therefore to src/runtime/starved_account_alert.py, which reads them. MEASURED on the live soak row 2026-09-21T20:30:08.586710Z ETHUSDT (diag-request issue #12708, run 35695727461, read 2026-09-22T06:38:27Z): per_account.bybit_2.state `routed`, per_account.bybit_portfolio.state `routed`, starved_accounts [], starved_count 0 -- ON THE VERY TICK rounds_planned[0].accounts [bybit_1, bybit_2, bybit_portfolio] was written out as rounds_written[0].accounts [bybit_1] and both accounts were dropped from the dispatch. `routed` is true of the global path and FALSE of the path that actually ran. bybit_2 is real money. [!] The only field pair that makes the condition sayable is rounds_planned vs rounds_written, and NO alert reads that pair. Same defect class as E22 and E31: a reading surface structurally unable to express the state that matters. [!] FOUND BY the E33 lane while measuring the fan-out's scope defect; NOT FIXED THERE, deliberately -- E33's deliverable is the routing proposal and this is a separate observability change. Fixing the routing (E33 (a) or (c)) makes this condition rare but does NOT make it reportable, so this row survives either."}
{"id": "PI-20260922-UTN353OZ-0001", "state": "queued", "next_action": "ask_operator", "origin": {"kind": "session", "ref": "session_01LfjQsydv3W4J6butn353oz, checklist row E28, branch e28-ict-scalp-harness-route", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json,glob; print({json.load(open(p))['strategy']: (json.load(open(p)).get('decision_rule') or {}).get('verdict') for p in sorted(glob.glob('comms/strategy_evidence/ict_scalp_*.json'))})\"   # the eight verdicts as committed; re-run scripts/ops/build_strategy_evidence.py --strategy <leg> to refresh one"}, "due_when": {"kind": "event", "check_every_days": 7, "clears_when": "EITHER the manager/operator records a Tier-3 decision on the four failing 5m legs (demote from bybit_1's roster, keep them running as soak instruments with the fail on record, or re-scope them), OR a later measurement over a different window overturns the verdicts and says so."}, "what": "MEASURED 2026-09-22 by routing the eight ict_scalp_* legs to scripts/backtest_ict_scalp.py for the first time (E28). Against RULE-D1-STAGE0-NET-OF-FULL-COST, registered 2026-09-22T05:34:08Z BEFORE every run, net of fee 7.5 / slippage 5.0 / funding 1.0 bps roundtrip, over 365d of real bars from data.binance.vision (2025-09-22 -> 2026-09-21): FAIL on ict_scalp_5m (net_r -16.8140, n=193, folds+ 1/4), ict_scalp_sol_5m (-14.7099, n=258, 1/4), ict_scalp_avax_5m (-13.5621, n=261, 1/4), ict_scalp_xrp_5m (-8.2061, n=245, 2/4); PASS on ict_scalp_eth_15m (+7.9357, n=120, 2/4), ict_scalp_sol_15m (+4.0692, n=133, 2/4), ict_scalp_xrp_15m (+1.1225, n=130, 2/4); ict_scalp_mgc_15m harness_failed with its reason stated. [!] THE SPLIT IS BY TIMEFRAME AND THE COST STACK IS WHAT CAUSES IT: all seven measured legs are POSITIVE fee-only (+2.37 to +13.76R) and the four 5m legs flip negative once slippage and funding are on. SOL is the controlled comparison -- same symbol, same params: 5m -14.7099 over n=258 vs 15m +4.0692 over n=133. XRP likewise. Cost is charged per trade, so a 5m leg pays roughly twice the stack for the same per-trade edge. This is R1's '3 of 11 flipped' class again, at 4 of 7. [!] URGENCY IS LOW AND THAT IS MEASURED, NOT ASSUMED: all eight sit on bybit_1 (7) and ib_paper (1), both account_class: paper, and check_roster_promotion_evidence.py --population lists 8 risk-bearing slots today (R2 cut four; the 12 in E28's original note is stale) and none of the eight is among them. NO REAL MONEY RIDES ON THIS. [!] DO NOT READ ict_scalp_xrp_5m's FAIL AS A VERDICT ON THE LEG AS IT RUNS -- its record grades `approximate` because off_cells is not_expressible by this harness, so the number is the UNGATED arm, and the live builder's own docstring says ungated fails 2/4 folds while gated passes 4/4. [!] THE DECISION IS TIER-3 AND IS DELIBERATELY NOT TAKEN HERE: E28's diff touches neither config/accounts.yaml nor config/strategies.yaml. Evidence records: comms/strategy_evidence/ict_scalp_*.json; per-trade series: comms/strategy_evidence/runs/2026-09-22-e28/."}
{"id": "PI-20260922-UTN353OZ-0002", "state": "queued", "next_action": "dispatch_lane", "origin": {"kind": "session", "ref": "session_01LfjQsydv3W4J6butn353oz, checklist row E28, branch e28-ict-scalp-harness-route", "rerun": "grep -n 'off_cells' scripts/backtest_ict_scalp.py; python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('comms/strategy_evidence/ict_scalp_xrp_5m.json')); print(d['fidelity'], d['omitted_levers'])\"   # a hit in the harness AND omitted_levers == [] means it is closed"}, "due_when": {"kind": "observation", "check_every_days": 14, "clears_when": "EITHER scripts/backtest_ict_scalp.py applies an off-cell suppression (a real SKIP, not a stamp) and ict_scalp_xrp_5m's record grades `faithful` with omitted_levers == [], OR a DECIDED records that the gated arm will not be measured offline and says what the leg's evidence rests on instead."}, "what": "CARRIED FORWARD, NOT NEW: MI-321 filed it on 2026-09-18 (docs/research/ict-scalp-seam-2026-09-18.md sec 5) as BL-20260918-THE-ICT-SCALP-HARNESS-CANNOT-EXPRESS-THE-OFF-CELL-SUPPRESSION-..., and that id no longer resolves -- the four review backlogs were archived by the 2026-09-21 operating reset, so the finding was tracked by nobody. This row is the carrier. THE FINDING: ict_scalp_xrp_5m declares off_cells [[trending, volatile], [chop, volatile]]; the LIVE builder suppresses an entry on it (src/runtime/strategy_signal_builders.py:797-802, `if off_cells and vol_spec:`), the key occurs ZERO times in scripts/backtest_ict_scalp.py, and no flag exists -- MI-321 graded it `not_expressible`. Its sibling vol_spec grades `asymmetric_gate`: --vol-spec-json exists and reaches no skip (12 occurrences, all inside _stamp_decision_time_regime), so forwarding it would LABEL a run with the spec while it behaves as if unset. [!] E28 FIXED THE OTHER HALF OF MI-321 sec 3 (the hardcoded _load_yaml_params read) and deliberately did NOT fix this half: regime_debt_matrix grades the leg `approximate` and NAMES both keys rather than certifying it. CONSEQUENCE, now committed: comms/strategy_evidence/ict_scalp_xrp_5m.json's net_r_oos -8.2061 (n=245) is the UNGATED arm, and the live builder's own docstring says 'XRP's pass was conditional on this gate (ungated 2/4 folds fails; gated 4/4 passes)'. So this harness can only produce the arm that fails, and that leg's Stage-0 verdict is not a statement about the leg as it actually runs. The fix is to port an off-cell SKIP into the harness -- not to widen the lever sets, which would launder the gap."}
{"id": "PI-20260922-UTN353OZ-0003", "state": "queued", "next_action": "dispatch_lane", "origin": {"kind": "session", "ref": "session_01LfjQsydv3W4J6butn353oz, checklist row E28, branch e28-ict-scalp-harness-route", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('comms/strategy_evidence/ict_scalp_mgc_15m.json')); print(d['coverage_state'], '|', d['error'])\"   # coverage_state == 'measured' means it is closed"}, "due_when": {"kind": "observation", "check_every_days": 14, "clears_when": "EITHER ict_scalp_mgc_15m carries coverage_state 'measured' from a feed that serves 15m gold bars over the record's own window_days, OR a DECIDED records that the leg is graded at a shorter window and the evidence record gains a field saying so, OR the leg is dropped from ib_paper's roster with a reason."}, "what": "MEASURED 2026-09-22 (E28). ict_scalp_mgc_15m is the one non-USDT leg of the eight, so routing the family is the first time anything asked Yahoo for 15m bars over 365 days. It returns nothing and the leg's record is `harness_failed` -- the honest outcome, and the reason is now stated correctly in the record after E28 fixed a misattribution (see below). [!] THE LEG IS NOT UNMEASURABLE, IT IS UNMEASURABLE AT THIS WINDOW, and the distinction is the point: GC=F at 15m returns 4,338 rows over 5 days and 0 over 365, with Yahoo's own error naming a 60-day intraday limit. A ~58-day measurement is available TODAY and this lane deliberately did NOT take it, for a stated reason rather than an omission: it would sit in a corpus whose every other record is 365d, the evidence schema has a window_days field but no consumer that compares across windows, and n over 58 days would be roughly a sixth of its 15m siblings' -- i.e. a number that looks like the others and is not. Either give the record a window that is honestly comparable, or source a feed that serves 15m gold over a year (Yahoo will not; the Binance-vision lane this harness uses for the seven USDT legs has no gold). [!] SECOND-ORDER, ALREADY FIXED IN E28 AND RECORDED HERE SO THE FIX IS NOT SILENTLY UNDONE: _yahoo_empty_reason originally blamed the TICKER ('delisting, a short listing history, or a wrong symbol map'), all three false, because its SPY control probes period=5d/interval=1d and therefore varies along NEITHER axis under test. A fourth state (window_exceeds_intraday_limit) and a same-interval self-probe now separate them, with both branches and the refusing_us precedence covered by tests. A control that cannot vary along the axis under test can only return the answer it already had."}
{"id": "PI-20260922-UTN353OZ-0004", "state": "queued", "next_action": "dispatch_lane", "origin": {"kind": "session", "ref": "session_01LfjQsydv3W4J6butn353oz, checklist row E28, branch e28-ict-scalp-harness-route", "rerun": "pip uninstall -y python-dotenv >/dev/null 2>&1; python3 scripts/research/mi321_ict_scalp_seam.py --selftest; echo exit=$?   # a named could_not_look report and a non-crash exit means it is closed"}, "due_when": {"kind": "observation", "check_every_days": 30, "clears_when": "mi321_ict_scalp_seam.py --selftest reports its own `could_not_look` state as a NAMED skip with a distinct exit code instead of raising KeyError, verified by running it with a missing dependency."}, "what": "OBSERVED 2026-09-22 while re-running the MI-321 selftest that E28's prose edits touch. In a sandbox without `python-dotenv`, leg_config_divergence() correctly returns its declared third state -- {\"state\": \"could_not_look\", \"reason\": \"ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'dotenv'\"} -- and the selftest then indexes div[\"legs\"] unconditionally and dies with KeyError: 'legs'. So the tool models `could_not_look` properly and its own selftest cannot express it: a harness that fires on its own plumbing, which docs/CLAUDE-RULES-CANONICAL.md names as worse for trust than a silent pass ('Red while measuring nothing is the same sin as green while measuring nothing'). PRE-EXISTING, NOT CAUSED BY E28 -- verified by git stash, reproducing the identical KeyError at the same line on unmodified origin/main. With the dependency installed the selftest passes 26/26, including after E28's edits, so nothing is blocked by it. The precedent to copy is scripts/check_workflow_shell.py, which exits 2 for 'nothing was checked' against 1 for real findings. NOT fixed here: E28's diff already reaches four files and this is a different tool's exit contract."}
{"id": "PI-20260922-THE-LANE-WATCHDOG-ROUTINE-FIRES-SESSIONS-WITH-NO-REPO-AND-NO-MCP-SO-IT-IS-DISABLED-AND-THE-INVARIANT-IS-STILL-VOLUNTARY", "state": "queued", "next_action": "ask_operator", "origin": {"kind": "session", "ref": "session_011xpvJ2MQgd4jmKkdfg6gzV, checklist row E20, branch claude/e20-lane-cost-supervision", "rerun": "list_triggers(enabled=true) and look for `lane-watchdog` -- if it is absent or disabled, the invariant is still voluntary and this row is open. Then fire_trigger on it and get_session the fired session: `session_context.sources` non-empty AND the fired session able to call list_sessions == closed. Checking this file or the prompt doc is NOT the rerun; the Routine's own state is."}, "due_when": {"kind": "observation", "check_every_days": 3, "clears_when": "The `lane-watchdog` Routine is ENABLED, its fired sessions carry a checkout of this repo AND the Claude Code Remote connector, and one firing has been OBSERVED to produce either a reconciliation or an explicit could-not-look -- never a silent pass. OR an operator DECIDED that lane supervision stays a manual manager duty, in which case say so and delete the Routine rather than leaving a disabled one implying coverage."}, "what": "MEASURED 2026-09-22T13:13Z by the E20 lane, by CREATING the Routine and FIRING it once rather than assuming it worked. E20 asked for the lane-reconcile invariant enforced 'somewhere involuntary', because a Routine firing into the MANAGER's session is a reminder a manager can skip -- reason (5), the one that killed DUE.md. The design chosen was a Routine firing into a FRESH session (create_new_session_on_fire), so the manager is not in the path. [!] IT DOES NOT WORK, AND THE FIRING IS WHAT ESTABLISHED THAT. `create_trigger` returned a warning in terms: 'this trigger stores no MCP connectors, so the sessions it fires will run without connector (mcp__*) tools' -- so the fired session cannot call list_sessions, which is the watchdog's entire basis. Worse, get_session on the fired session (session_01GyB7Zej9GgVoodnfKz4Re5) shows its `session_context` carries NO `sources` at all, so it has no checkout of this repo and cannot run scripts/ops/lane_reconcile.py either. It was also served claude-sonnet-5 rather than the claude-haiku-4-5 passed at creation. [!] THE ABSENCE IS A REAL READING, NOT A GAP IN THE PROJECTION: get_session on an ordinary lane DOES return sources: [{git_repository: ...}] -- checked against this session's own record in the same minute -- so the field is emitted when there is a source, and two independent signals agree (the trigger's stored session_request.config.sources is also []). [!] THE ROUTINE IS THEREFORE DISABLED (trig_01MFG5cYsTsS1bci279A2TKi, renamed to say why), NOT LEFT RUNNING. A watcher that fires every six hours, cannot reach its subject and reports nothing is exactly the defect class E20 is about -- a check whose subject it cannot see, still counted as coverage -- and it would bill a session per firing to produce it. Leaving it enabled would be worse than not having it, and reporting 'the watchdog is in place' would have been the same false claim one level up. [!] SO THE INVARIANT IS STILL VOLUNTARY. What stands instead: .claude/skills/manager/SKILL.md now requires `python3 scripts/ops/lane_reconcile.py --sessions <dump>` at every manager check-in, which is a discipline and not a mechanism, and is said that way. [!] WHAT ONLY A HUMAN CAN DO, and why: connectors on a Routine created through create_trigger are limited to those the calling session can pass through, and this session had none to pass. So the operator must open the Routines UI on claude.ai, attach the Claude Code Remote connector AND this repository as a source to `lane-watchdog`, re-enable it, and fire it once to confirm the fired session reports a reconciliation or an explicit could-not-look. Owner class: defaulted_to_human only in the sense that no wire exists from a session; the grant itself is genuinely a human action at the account level. Full write-up and the two cheaper designs that were measured and killed first: docs/claude/work/LANE-WATCHDOG-PROMPT.md.", "routed_to": "operator (the connector + source grant); checklist row E20 recorded the build"}
{"id": "PI-20260922-ONE-BLOCKED-LANE-HAS-HELD-1343-USD-FOR-72-HOURS-HAVING-NEVER-COMMITTED-A-LINE-AND-22-FINISHED-LANES-SIT-UNARCHIVED", "state": "queued", "next_action": "ask_operator", "origin": {"kind": "session", "ref": "session_011xpvJ2MQgd4jmKkdfg6gzV, checklist row E20, branch claude/e20-lane-cost-supervision", "rerun": "From a session holding the Claude Code Remote MCP tools: dump list_sessions(mine=true, limit=60) to a file, then `python3 scripts/ops/lane_reconcile.py --sessions <file>`. Exit 0 == closed. The WALLED and FINISHED-BUT-STILL-OPEN sections are the two this row is about; read the figures with the running/final marker the tool prints, never bare."}, "due_when": {"kind": "observation", "check_every_days": 2, "clears_when": "`lane_reconcile.py --sessions <a fresh dump>` reports ZERO walled lanes and ZERO finished-but-unarchived lanes, OR each remaining one carries a stated reason for being left open. A walled lane needs a HUMAN to clear its permission prompt, so this cannot close autonomously -- which is the point of it being a row rather than a note."}, "what": "MEASURED 2026-09-22T13:02Z by the E20 lane. POPULATION: the 60 most recent sessions on this account (list_sessions(mine=true)), cost_usd read from external_metadata.usage.cost_usd, present on 51 of 60 and totalling $5,892.69. The 9 without a usage block are recorded as COULD-NOT-LOOK and excluded from every total rather than counted as $0 -- 6 of those 9 are in status_bucket WORKING, so the commonest reason a cost is unreadable is that the lane is BUSY, and a fabricated zero would put the cheapest-looking figure on the lanes most likely to be expensive. [!] THE HEADLINE: session_01XYu2vvg9Qgxoqf4jJQyd8i ('ENGINEERING LANE MI-305 -- /api/diag/journal ignores limit and offset'), spawned by the PREVIOUS manager (session_01GHpyXT2qssLJsrvXBQRkQF) on 2026-09-18T00:13Z, has sat status_bucket BLOCKED and session_status IDLE for 72.8 hours holding $1,343.06 -- 447M cache-read tokens, 596k output tokens -- and has NEVER COMMITTED A SINGLE LINE to git (verified by scanning every Claude-Session trailer in the whole history for its id: zero commits). That is MORE THAN E20's entire measured 13-lane day ($1,142) in one session that produced nothing, and E20's own cited instance of this cause (B2, $229.13) is a sixth of it. [!] IT IS ONE OF FOUR: 4 BLOCKED lanes hold $1,347.60 between them, and a fifth appeared while this row was being written (session_01SVqDYb7UMYjzNqZrdLdDjt, the E31 lane, BLOCKED at $24.97 with post_turn_summary 'Stopping here.'). [!] AND THE SECOND HALF: 22 sessions whose work is FINISHED (status_bucket COMPLETED or REVIEW_READY) are NOT ARCHIVED and therefore still able to accrue, holding $2,088.73 of RUNNING spend, largest single line $736.15. That is E20's cause (2), and it is the biggest line in the account -- larger than any lane's actual work. [!] WHY THIS IS A ROW AND NOT A FIX I APPLIED: a BLOCKED lane is sitting on a permission prompt only a HUMAN can clear (fire_trigger is refused there, correctly, because firing would answer the prompt on the operator's behalf), and archiving another manager's lanes is the MANAGER's call, not a worker lane's. Surfacing it is the job. `scripts/ops/lane_reconcile.py` now prints all of it every run, and .claude/skills/manager/SKILL.md carries the two controls. [!] NOTE THE ROW E20 GOT WRONG IN THE FAVOURABLE DIRECTION, since it matters for how the next measurement is read: E20 says six lanes exceeded their ceiling; the reconciler measures 14. The difference is the same cause (1) the row is about -- the numbers were read before the lanes were archived, so they were running totals.", "routed_to": "the manager (archive the 22; triage the walled lanes); the operator must clear each permission prompt"}
{"id": "PI-20260922-THE-PUSH-ON-STOP-WIRE-COVERS-2-OF-60-SESSIONS-AND-ZERO-OF-THE-26-THAT-COST-MONEY-BECAUSE-BLOCKED-AND-COMPLETED-ARE-SILENT", "state": "queued", "next_action": "ask_operator", "origin": {"kind": "session", "ref": "session_011xpvJ2MQgd4jmKkdfg6gzV, checklist row E20, branch claude/e20-lane-cost-supervision; raised by the manager as an addendum to the E20 dispatch and answered with a measurement", "rerun": "From a session holding the Claude Code Remote MCP tools: dump list_sessions(mine=true, limit=60), then count by status_bucket. The push wire reaches FAILED only; compare that count against BLOCKED + COMPLETED + REVIEW_READY (the silent set). `python3 -c \"import json,sys,collections; d=json.load(open(sys.argv[1]))['ccr']['data']; print(collections.Counter(x['status_bucket'] for x in d))\" <dump>` -- coverage is FAILED / (FAILED + the silent set)."}, "due_when": {"kind": "observation", "check_every_days": 7, "clears_when": "EITHER a lane reaching BLOCKED, COMPLETED or REVIEW_READY produces a push event the manager receives without pulling (so the coverage fraction above stops being ~3%), OR an operator DECIDED that lane-stop detection stays a PULL and names what makes the pull non-optional -- a mechanism, not a cadence. A 2-hourly Routine into the manager's own session does not clear this: it is a reminder a manager can skip, which is reason (5) from the operating plan."}, "what": "RAISED BY THE MANAGER 2026-09-22T12:50Z as an addendum to the E20 dispatch, in its own words: 'only `failed` can push an event, while `blocked`, `review_ready` and `completed` are SILENT BY DESIGN. That is WHY four lanes sat dead for over an hour today... the manager's only view of lane state is a PULL it must choose to make, so \"the manager did not notice\" was structurally guaranteed, not a lapse of attention.' It asked whether push-on-stop plus a 2-hourly sweep is sufficient. ANSWERED WITH A MEASUREMENT RATHER THAN AN OPINION, and the answer is NO. POPULATION: the 60 most recent sessions on this account, list_sessions(mine=true) read 2026-09-22T13:02Z. BY status_bucket: COMPLETED 43, WORKING 7, REVIEW_READY 4, BLOCKED 4, FAILED 2. [!] THE PUSH WIRE REACHES FAILED ONLY, SO IT COVERS 2 OF 60 SESSIONS. The conditions that actually cost money are BLOCKED (4 sessions, $1,347.60) and finished-but-unarchived (22 sessions, $2,088.73) = 26 conditions, and the wire covers ZERO of them. The $1,343.06 lane that has sat 72.8 hours having never committed a line is BLOCKED -- squarely in the silent set -- which is why it lasted three days rather than an hour. [!] AND THE REPLY WIRE HAS THE SAME HOLE ONE LEVEL DOWN, which is the sharper half: the lane->manager channel is a create_trigger the LANE must choose to fire. A lane that dies mid-turn, exhausts its budget, or hits a permission wall cannot fire it -- so the wire structurally cannot cover the cases it exists for, because A BLOCKED LANE CANNOT REPORT THAT IT IS BLOCKED. It helps for lanes that finish cleanly and not at all for the ones that do not. [!] AND THE 2-HOURLY SWEEP FIRES INTO THE MANAGER'S OWN SESSION, so it is a reminder a manager can skip -- reason (5) from the operating plan, the one that killed DUE.md. The manager said as much itself when dispatching E20. [!] WHAT E20 BUILT AND WHY IT IS NOT THE ANSWER YET: a Routine firing a FRESH session (so the manager is not in the path) was created, fired, and DISABLED, because the fired session has no repo checkout and no MCP connectors -- see PI-20260922-THE-LANE-WATCHDOG-ROUTINE-FIRES-SESSIONS-WITH-NO-REPO-AND-NO-MCP-SO-IT-IS-DISABLED-AND-THE-INVARIANT-IS-STILL-VOLUNTARY. So BOTH candidate mechanisms are currently unavailable and the detection is a pull. [!] THIS IS FILED RATHER THAN ONLY REPORTED BECAUSE A MESSAGE IS NOT A DISPOSITION. The judgement was delivered to the manager as a report-back trigger at 13:28Z; a trigger prompt is a chat message with extra steps, and close-out check 4 is explicit that neither is filing. The decision owed is whether lane-stop detection becomes a push (a platform capability nobody here controls) or stays a pull with a named non-optional mechanism.", "routed_to": "the manager (raised it); the operator owns the decision and the connector grant the fresh-session watchdog needs"}
{"id": "PI-20260922-ROUTINE-FIRED-SESSIONS-GET-NO-REPO-AND-NO-MCP-WHICH-IS-THE-ONE-ROOT-CAUSE-BEHIND-BOTH-DEAD-WATCHDOGS", "state": "queued", "next_action": "ask_operator", "origin": {"kind": "session", "ref": "session_011xpvJ2MQgd4jmKkdfg6gzV, checklist row E20, branch claude/e20-lane-cost-supervision; the queue-watch half was surfaced by the E31 lane via check_manager_queue_watch.py reporting NEVER_RAN_OVERDUE", "rerun": "list_triggers(recurring=true) -> read `mcp_connections` on each recurring Routine (empty == no connector tools for its fired sessions), then get_session on the newest `last_run.session_id` and read `session_context.sources` (absent == no repo checkout). BOTH empty on a Routine whose prompt runs a repo script or an mcp__ tool == this row still open. Do NOT substitute `enabled: true` or `last_run.status: SUCCEEDED` for either read: both are TRUE today on a Routine that has written nothing in 479 firings."}, "due_when": {"kind": "observation", "check_every_days": 3, "clears_when": "Every recurring Routine whose prompt needs the repo or an mcp__ tool has a fired session that demonstrably HAS them — verified by get_session on an actual firing, not by the Routine's config. For the manager queue watch specifically: docs/claude/work/MANAGER-QUEUE-WATCH.json exists on main with a `generated_at` written by a Routine-fired session, so check_manager_queue_watch.py leaves NEVER_RAN_OVERDUE. OR an operator DECIDED to retire the Routines that cannot be wired, with the guards retired alongside them rather than left reporting on nothing."}, "what": "MEASURED 2026-09-22 by the E20 lane, and it is ONE ROOT CAUSE BEHIND TWO SEPARATELY-FILED DEAD WATCHDOGS. [!] THE MECHANISM: a session spawned by a Routine firing gets NEITHER a checkout of this repo NOR any mcp__* connector tools. Established by DIRECT OBSERVATION on two independent Routines, not inferred. (a) trig_01MFG5cYsTsS1bci279A2TKi, the lane-watchdog this lane created: fired once deliberately as a smoke test, and get_session on session_01GyB7Zej9GgVoodnfKz4Re5 shows `session_context` carrying NO `sources` and a tool list holding no mcp__* entry; create_trigger had already warned in terms that the trigger 'stores no MCP connectors'. (b) trig_01TWdAvrwFLe6T9XFoNopTeo, the Manager queue watch, which has existed since 2026-09-02: `enabled: true`, cron `56 * * * *`, `last_fired_at` 2026-09-22T12:56:24Z, `last_run.status` SUCCEEDED — and get_session on that firing (session_018tj1rtL2SPANzNwQLhUCei) shows the SAME two absences, plus `mcp_connections: []` on the trigger itself. [!] THE NEGATIVE CONTROL, because an absence is only evidence if the field is emitted when present: get_session on an ORDINARY lane DOES return `sources: [{git_repository: ...}]`, checked against this session's own record in the same minute. So the absence is a real reading rather than a gap in the projection. [!] WHAT IT COST AND WHY NOBODY SAW IT: that Routine's prompt has TWO steps it cannot perform — step 1 needs list_sessions (an mcp__ tool it does not hold) and step 2 needs `python3 scripts/ops/queue_latency.py --write-receipt` from the repo root (a checkout it does not have). So it has fired ~479 times over 479 hours at roughly $0.19 per firing — on the order of $90 — reporting SUCCEEDED every time and writing nothing. Its own guard, scripts/ci/check_manager_queue_watch.py, has been correctly reporting NEVER_RAN_OVERDUE, a state added 2026-09-12 precisely to distinguish 'has not fired yet' from 'has fired ~240 times and written nothing'. THE GUARD WORKED. Nothing acted on it, because NEVER_RAN_OVERDUE deliberately PASSES (only the Routine can clear it) and lands only as a due-list row. [!] THE DISPOSITION ON THE GUARD, ASKED FOR EXPLICITLY AND ANSWERED: DO NOT RETIRE scripts/ci/check_manager_queue_watch.py. It is the only thing in the repo that noticed, its five-state model is correct, and its docstring already anticipated the mcp__ half ('a Routine-fired session holds no mcp__* tools'). What it did not anticipate is the REPO half, which is why its own remedy — step 2 of the Routine prompt — was unperformable. Retiring the guard would delete the detector and keep the defect. [!] THE DECISION OWED IS ABOUT THE ROUTINES, NOT THE GUARDS, and there are only two honest options: EITHER the operator attaches the Claude Code Remote connector AND this repository as a source to each recurring Routine in the claude.ai Routines UI (a session CANNOT do this: connectors on a Routine created through create_trigger are limited to those the calling session can pass through, and a CCR session has none to pass), OR the Routines that cannot be wired are DELETED and the guards that grade them are retired in the same change — `killed` with a stated reason beats a watchdog that bills $90 to report SUCCEEDED while doing nothing. Leaving a third option open (a disabled or unwired Routine still implying coverage) is the state this row exists to end. [!] ONE OPERATOR ACTION FIXES BOTH THIS AND PI-20260922-THE-LANE-WATCHDOG-ROUTINE-FIRES-SESSIONS-WITH-NO-REPO-AND-NO-MCP-SO-IT-IS-DISABLED-AND-THE-INVARIANT-IS-STILL-VOLUNTARY. [!] AND NOTE WHAT WAS NOT EVIDENCE: `enabled: true`, a `next_run_at` in the future, and `last_run.status: SUCCEEDED` are ALL TRUE of this Routine right now. The guard's own docstring already said 'A ROUTINE BEING enabled: true IS NOT EVIDENCE IT FIRES'; the sharper lesson is that FIRING is not evidence it DID anything, because the run succeeds when the agent correctly reports that it cannot do the work.", "routed_to": "operator (the connector + source grant, or the decision to delete the Routines); checklist row E20 recorded the measurement"}
{"id": "PI-20260922-EIGHT-CI-GUARDS-GRADE-NOTHING-AND-ELEVEN-MORE-ARE-SILENTLY-NARROWED-BECAUSE-THE-RESET-ARCHIVED-THEIR-SUBJECTS", "state": "queued", "next_action": "ask_operator", "origin": {"kind": "session", "ref": "session_011xpvJ2MQgd4jmKkdfg6gzV, checklist row E20, branch claude/e20-lane-cost-supervision", "rerun": "python3 scripts/ci/check_guard_liveness.py   # the four counts print on every run; `dead: 0` and `degraded: 0` == closed. `python3 scripts/ci/check_guard_liveness.py --strict` fails on the baselined debt as well, which is the shape to run when asking whether the debt has actually shrunk."}, "due_when": {"kind": "observation", "check_every_days": 14, "clears_when": "Every guard in BASELINE_2026_09_22 has either been RE-POINTED at a surface that exists on main, or RETIRED with a stated reason and removed from run_guards.py, and its baseline entry deleted (check_guard_liveness FAILS on a baseline entry whose guard recovered or vanished, so the list cannot rot). PARTIAL credit is visible without closing this: the printed `baselined debt: N of 19` count is the progress measure. Also required before closing: docs/CLAUDE-RULES-CANONICAL.md stops naming a dead guard as a live mechanism."}, "what": "MEASURED 2026-09-22 by the E20 lane, after the manager reframed three separate incidents as one MISSING INVARIANT: a check must be able to say when it cannot see its subject, and that must be loud. POPULATION: 95 guard scripts (scripts/ci/check_*.py + scripts/check_*.py), every MODULE-LEVEL repo-path constant tested with `git cat-file -e HEAD:<path>`. RESULT, four states never collapsed: 46 declare no module-level repo path so THE INSTRUMENT CANNOT SPEAK TO THEM (reported as its own count, never folded into `live`); 30 live; 11 DEGRADED (some subjects absent, so they grade a silently narrower set); 8 DEAD (every declared subject absent, so they grade NOTHING and their green means nothing). [!] THE INSTRUMENT WAS VALIDATED BEFORE IT WAS TRUSTED, and the naive version FAILED. Scanning for any path literal anywhere returns 29 of 69 scripts/ci files and is DOMINATED BY SELF-TEST FIXTURES — docs/WORKPLAN-a.md, scripts/ops/bare_tool.py, .github/pr-landing/nested/f.json are planted inside self_test() and a guard is SUPPOSED to plant paths that do not exist. Restricting to ast.Module.body (never a function body) removes that whole class: 19 of 95 with no fixture noise. The self-test carries that as an explicit control (N3), so a future edit that walks into function bodies again fails rather than silently re-inflating. [!] THE 8 DEAD: check_backlog_unresolve, check_claim_basis, check_open_items, check_operator_owed, check_recurrence_ledger, check_register_field_loss, check_soak_registered, check_stated_population. Every one is the 2026-09-21 operating reset's blast radius — it archived the four review backlogs, OPEN-ITEMS.json, the lease, SESSIONS.json, MERGE-QUEUE.json, session-board.json, RECURRENCE-LEDGER.json and operator-owed-register.json, and nothing swept the guards that read them. [!] AND THREE OF THE DEAD ARE NAMED IN docs/CLAUDE-RULES-CANONICAL.md AS LIVE MECHANISMS, which is the part that should decide the priority. § Backlog governance says the `status` enum is 'enforced whole-file over every review backlog by claim-basis-guard' — all four backlogs are archived, so check_claim_basis.py grades nothing. § 'A soak must carry its own alarm' says check_soak_registered.py 'fails when a *_soak.jsonl log exists in src/ with no register alarm' — its register (docs/claude/OPEN-ITEMS.json) is archived. § Session-end reconciliation says operator-owed-guard 'FAILS when an item has been carried across register commits without a state change' — its register is archived. A canonical doc naming a mechanism that cannot run is the folklore failure that document has its own section about, and it is the #1 doc in the instruction hierarchy. [!] THE MECHANISM BUILT FOR IT: scripts/ci/check_guard_liveness.py, wired into run_guards.py as `guard-liveness` with when: None (whole-tree, because a diff-scoped version would pass vacuously on nearly every PR). It is REPORT-FIRST ON PURPOSE and that is not weakness: the 19 already-broken guards are carried in a dated BASELINE that may only SHRINK with the count printed on every run, and only a NEW dead/degraded guard FAILS. Failing all 19 on day one would red-wall the repo and get the guard reverted rather than the debt fixed — the reasoning check_pr_queue_watch.py records, and § 'could not measure is its own outcome' records a guard whose first CI run emitted 117 fake findings and buried the only fact that mattered. A baseline entry whose subject came BACK, or whose guard no longer exists, also fails, so the list cannot rot into a permanent hole. Self-test 14 cases, every state planted with its control. [!] WHAT THIS SWEEP DOES NOT REACH, stated rather than implied: an archived INPUT is not the only way a check dies. The third incident (check_manager_queue_watch, armed 479h and never receipted) has a subject that EXISTS as a path — what died is the Routine that writes it, filed separately as PI-20260922-ROUTINE-FIRED-SESSIONS-GET-NO-REPO-AND-NO-MCP-WHICH-IS-THE-ONE-ROOT-CAUSE-BEHIND-BOTH-DEAD-WATCHDOGS. And `live` here means ONLY that a subject exists, never that the guard works. [!] THE DECISION OWED: for each of the 19, re-point or retire. `killed` with a stated reason is a first-class outcome and is probably right for several — a guard reading a register the operator deliberately archived may simply not be wanted. Do not repair reflexively, and do not promote the baseline to blocking without the operator choosing to.", "routed_to": "operator (re-point vs retire, for each of the 19, and whether any of it becomes blocking); the doc correction follows that decision"}
{"id": "PI-20260922-ONLY-1-OF-38-IN-REPO-CADENCE-DECLARATIONS-CAN-BE-SEEN-TO-HAVE-RUN-AND-A-ROUTINE-CANNOT-BE-DERIVED-AT-ALL", "state": "queued", "next_action": "ask_operator", "origin": {"kind": "session", "ref": "session_011xpvJ2MQgd4jmKkdfg6gzV, checklist row E20, branch claude/e20-lane-cost-supervision; the design constraint (freshness in CI, not on a timer) came from the manager after the operator asked 'what are we doing to fix that?' about three silent-check instances", "rerun": "python3 scripts/ci/check_cadence_liveness.py   # the six state counts print on every run. `no_receipt: 0` and `unregistered: 0` == closed. `--strict` fails on the baselined debt too, which is the shape to run when asking whether the debt actually shrank. Do NOT substitute reading this row: the counts are derived from the tree each run."}, "due_when": {"kind": "observation", "check_every_days": 14, "clears_when": "EITHER every entry in CADENCE_REGISTRY names a receipt path the scheduled thing itself WRITES (so `no_receipt` reaches 0 and freshness is actually graded), OR each remaining `no_receipt` entry carries an operator-accepted reason for being ungradeable from CI and the count has stopped standing in for coverage. PARTIAL progress is visible without closing: the printed `no_receipt: N of 38` is the measure. Separately required and NOT covered by that predicate: the Routine half, which needs a receipt a Routine-fired session can actually write — blocked on PI-20260922-ROUTINE-FIRED-SESSIONS-GET-NO-REPO-AND-NO-MCP-WHICH-IS-THE-ONE-ROOT-CAUSE-BEHIND-BOTH-DEAD-WATCHDOGS."}, "what": "MEASURED 2026-09-22 by the E20 lane, building the mechanism the manager specified after the operator asked what was being done about three silent-check incidents. [!] THE DESIGN CONSTRAINT FIRST, BECAUSE THE OBVIOUS FIX CONTAINS A REGRESS THIS REPO HAS ALREADY PAID FOR: 'make every check emit a receipt and have something grade receipt freshness' IS ALREADY BUILT -- scripts/ci/check_manager_queue_watch.py is exactly that design, and the Routine it grades has fired ~479 times in 479 hours and written ZERO receipts. The receipt-grader was itself the thing that never ran, so adding receipts moves the silence up one level and asks who watches the watcher. THE WAY OUT IS A DIFFERENT SUBSTRATE, not a better timer: a scheduled job can always be the thing that did not fire and its not-firing is invisible by construction, while CI runs on every push, so CI's own liveness is PROVEN BY THE FACT THAT PRs MERGE AT ALL. [!] WHAT WAS BUILT: scripts/ci/check_cadence_liveness.py, WIRED into run_guards.py as `cadence-liveness` (when: None, self-test first). The POPULATION IS DERIVED FROM THE TREE, never hand-maintained -- a check that never registers would be invisible again, same bug new clothes -- from `.github/workflows/*.yml` carrying `on.schedule.cron` and from `deploy/*.timer` carrying `OnCalendar=`. ANYTHING DERIVED AND UNREGISTERED FAILS; that is the omission-detectable property and it is the structural half. Six states never collapsed: fresh / stale / never_ran / schedule_unknown / no_receipt / unregistered. [!] THE MEASUREMENT, and it is the number the operator needs to choose what becomes blocking: POPULATION 38 in-repo cadence declarations -- 18 of 143 workflows carry a `schedule:`, plus 20 deploy/*.timer units (11 with a readable OnCalendar, 9 triggered by another unit and so declaring no calendar of their own, which is schedule_unknown rather than a fault). RESULT: fresh 1, stale 0, never_ran 0, schedule_unknown 0, no_receipt 37, unregistered 0. [!] SO EXACTLY ONE OF THIRTY-EIGHT SCHEDULED THINGS CAN CURRENTLY BE SEEN TO HAVE RUN (trainer-capture-watch.yml, which commits docs/claude/work/TRAINER-CAPTURE-WATCH.json back to main, so its last commit IS the receipt and no new artifact was needed). The other 37 are ungradeable, and that is REPORTED as its own count rather than folded into `fresh` -- folding it would make the module commit the defect it exists to find, and at 37 of 38 it is the largest bucket by far. [!] THE 37 SPLIT INTO TWO TRACTABILITY TIERS, which is what makes this actionable rather than a lament: 8 more scheduled workflows already COMMIT BACK (measured by grepping commit-to-main / git push across the 18), so each is ONE DECLARED OUTPUT PATH away from being gradeable with no cooperation from the workflow at all -- that is the cheap shrink. The remaining 9 workflows leave no in-repo trace (their history is in the Actions API, which this guard deliberately does not call: a guard that needs the network reds on an outage), and all 20 timers run ON THE VM where nothing they do reaches this repo, so the diag relays are their surface rather than CI. [!] THE RESIDUAL, NAMED IN THE GUARD'S STANDING DISCLOSURE AND PRINTED ON EVERY RUN INCLUDING CLEAN ONES: A ROUTINE IS NOT DERIVABLE FROM THE REPOSITORY. Routines live in the Claude Code Remote API, which CI cannot reach, so the omission of a Routine is NOT detected -- and instance #3 of this class WAS a Routine. This guard covers the IN-REPO half of the class and no more, and says so rather than implying completeness. Closing the other half needs a receipt a Routine-fired session can actually write, which today it cannot, because those sessions get neither a repo checkout nor any mcp__ tool. [!] REPORT-FIRST ON PURPOSE, and the trap was stated by the manager before I could walk into it: 38 red entries on day one gets the guard reverted and then there is nothing. The day-one population is carried in a dated BASELINE that may only SHRINK with the counts printed every run; only an UNREGISTERED new cadence, a newly stale graded receipt, or baseline rot (an entry that recovered, or whose source file is gone) fails. `--strict` fails on the debt too. What becomes blocking beyond those is the operator's call, not this module's. Self-test 25 cases including the DERIVATION CONTROL (a workflow with no `schedule:` must not enter the population) and the refusals (an unparseable cron returns None rather than a guessed interval -- a fabricated threshold under a freshness verdict is the same defect as a fabricated $0 under a cost).", "routed_to": "operator (which of the six states becomes blocking, and whether the 9 no-trace workflows should start committing a receipt); the 8 commit-back workflows are a cheap shrink any lane can take"}
{"id": "PI-20260922-MANAGER-SCOPE-R6-R7-R8-HAVE-NO-SUBJECT-AFTER-THE-RESET-SO-THREE-OF-FOUR-RULES-GRADE-NOTHING", "state": "queued", "next_action": "ask_operator", "origin": {"kind": "session", "ref": "session_011xpvJ2MQgd4jmKkdfg6gzV, checklist row E20, branch claude/e20-manager-identity-fail-closed", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import sys; sys.path.insert(0,'scripts/ci'); from pathlib import Path; from check_manager_scope import rule_liveness; print(chr(10).join(rule_liveness(Path('.'))))\"   # every line reading `live` == closed; any DEAD line names the rule and the missing subject"}, "due_when": {"kind": "observation", "check_every_days": 14, "clears_when": "EITHER every rule in check_manager_scope.RULE_SUBJECTS reports `live` (its subject exists on main, whether restored or re-pointed at a post-reset surface), OR an operator DECIDED is recorded saying R6/R7/R8 are retired with the reset and the rules are deleted from the guard rather than left grading nothing. Re-pointing counts; leaving the DEAD lines printing forever does not."}, "what": "MEASURED 2026-09-22 by the E20 lane while fixing R2's identity, and filed rather than walked past. POPULATION: the four rules in scripts/ci/check_manager_scope.py, each checked with `git cat-file -e HEAD:<subject>` against origin/main. RESULT: R2 live. R6 DEAD (needs docs/claude/work/MANAGER-LEASE.json AND docs/claude/work/SESSIONS.json -- both absent). R7 DEAD (needs the lease -- absent). R8 DEAD (needs docs/claude/work/MERGE-QUEUE.json -- absent). All three were archived by the 2026-09-21 operating reset under docs/archive/2026-09-21-operating-reset/. [!] SO THREE OF THE GUARD'S FOUR RULES GRADE NOTHING, and until this session two of them did it SILENTLY: supervision_gap() and heartbeat_cadence_gap() return None when the lease is absent, which is indistinguishable in the output from `graded and fine`. R8 was the honest one -- it already printed its own absence loudly, which is the shape the other two now follow via rule_liveness(). [!] THIS IS THE SAME DEFECT CLASS AS THE ROW THAT FOUND IT, one level down: a check whose subject was removed, still green, still counted as coverage. The R2 half is FIXED in this branch (identity is now a union of derived sources and fails closed at exit 2 when none is live). The R6/R7/R8 half is NOT fixed, deliberately: R6 and R7 measure the LEASE-and-registry operating model that the reset retired on operator direction, so restoring their subjects would be resurrecting an archived mechanism -- explicitly forbidden by CLAUDE.md -- and re-pointing them needs a decision about what the post-reset equivalents of `supervision record` and `check-in cadence` even are. R8's merge queue has a closer successor (the per-branch merge-slot claims under .github/merge-slots/), so its one invariant (at most one entry `rebasing`) may be portable. [!] WHY IT IS REPORTED AND NOT FAILED: failing every contributor's PR over a register the contributor cannot restore is the alarm-on-its-own-plumbing failure docs/CLAUDE-RULES-CANONICAL.md files findings about -- an alarm that fires on its own plumbing teaches every later session to skim past it. The DEAD lines print on every run so the coverage loss is visible without anyone auditing the guard. [!] THE DECISION OWED: retire R6/R7/R8 with a stated reason (the honest default -- `killed` is a first-class outcome and closing a dead rule beats carrying it), or re-point each at a post-reset surface. Do not leave three rules printing DEAD indefinitely; that is the state this row exists to end.", "routed_to": "checklist row E20 (the lane that found it); the decision itself is the operator's"}
{"due_when": {"check_every_days": 30, "clears_when": "EITHER build_strategy_evidence.py / regime_debt_matrix.py forward --tp-cap-pct (0.099, the Bybit clamp) so a trend/pullback record's net_r_oos is measured on a book that CAN take profit, and the corpus is re-measured under a stated A/B against the uncapped arm -- OR an operator decision records that the uncapped arm is the intended basis, in which case `fidelity: faithful` must stop being reachable for any trend/pullback leg, because none of them models the cap that binds live. [!] E46 fixed only the DECLARED-LEVER half (a binding tp_r is now named as omitted); this row is the half it deliberately left, and re-running the producer clears nothing.", "kind": "observation"}, "id": "PI-20260922-PJVBLXLA-0001", "next_action": "ask_operator", "origin": {"kind": "session", "ref": "session_01AtmcbUvFuN6GThPjvBLxLA, checklist row E46, branch claude/e46-evidence-clobber-guard", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import sys,yaml; sys.path.insert(0,'scripts/research'); import regime_debt_matrix as rdm; legs=yaml.safe_load(open('config/strategies.yaml'))['strategies']; rows=[(n,rdm.classify(b),b.get('tp_r')) for n,b in sorted(legs.items()) if isinstance(b,dict) and b.get('enabled',True) and rdm.classify(b) in ('trend','pullback')]; print('trend/pullback legs:', len(rows)); print('sentinel (>=20R):', sum(1 for _,_,t in rows if t is not None and float(t)>=20.0)); print('any run passing --tp-cap-pct:', any('--tp-cap-pct' in rdm.build_harness_cmd(n,legs[n],h,'d','1h','e','j')[0] for n,h,_ in rows))\""}, "state": "queued", "terminal_reason": null, "what": "[health] `fidelity: faithful` on a trend or pullback evidence record still does NOT mean the harness modelled the take-profit the leg actually has live. Live places `tp = min(entry*(1+0.099), entry + tp_r*risk)` -- the ~9.9 percent Bybit TP-distance clamp on the 50R sentinel -- and `regime_debt_matrix.build_harness_cmd` passes no `--tp-cap-pct`, so `tp_cap_pct` defaults to 0.0 and the harness has NO take-profit exit path at all. MEASURED 2026-09-22 by running the rerun command over config/strategies.yaml: 41 enabled legs route to trend or pullback, 28 of them carry `tp_r: 50.0`, every one declares some tp_r, and ZERO runs pass --tp-cap-pct. E46 (PI-20260922-E41-0005) made a BINDING declared tp_r (< 20R) count as an omitted lever, which took `faithful` from 37/52 to 25/52 enabled legs; the 28 sentinel legs were deliberately left `faithful` because a 50R target cannot be reached -- but the 9.9 percent CLAMP can, and at atr_stop_mult 2.5 with 2-3 percent ATR it lands at 1.3-2.0R, an ordinary frequently-touched target (scripts/backtest_trend.py's own comment). So every trail-family number in the corpus is measured on a book that cannot take profit. This is BL-20260810-BACKTEST-DOES-NOT-MODEL-THE-LIVE-CAPPED-TP reaching the evidence records; NOT folded into E46 because turning the cap on silently re-bases the whole fleet's history, which backtest_trend.py's own `--tp-cap-pct` default-off comment refuses in terms."}
{"id": "PI-20260922-ALLOW-DEGRADED-GUARD-IS-UNSATISFIABLE-SINCE-THE-RESET-ARCHIVED-EVERY-BACKLOG-IT-RESOLVES-IDS-AGAINST", "state": "queued", "next_action": "dispatch_lane", "origin": {"kind": "session", "ref": "session_01PDnYMJCPBohXArAKpUceZm, checklist row E42, branch lane/e42-shared-symbol-resolver", "rerun": "python3 -c \"import sys,glob; sys.path.insert(0,'scripts/ops'); from check_backlog_refs import REPO, BACKLOG_GLOB, filed_ids; print('registers:', glob.glob(str(REPO/BACKLOG_GLOB)), 'ids:', len(filed_ids()))\"   # an EMPTY register list means no new BL-/MB-/FU- id can be filed anywhere check_allow_degraded.py would resolve it"}, "due_when": {"kind": "observation", "check_every_days": 14, "clears_when": "EITHER check_allow_degraded.py resolves ids against a register that still exists (most likely docs/claude/work/PIPELINE.jsonl, whose ids are `PI-`-prefixed and which check_backlog_refs.REF does not match), OR a DECIDED records that no new `# allow-degraded:` annotation may be written and the guard's message says that instead of naming a remedy nobody can perform. Re-check by running the rerun command: a non-empty register list, or a PI- id resolving, closes it."}, "what": "MEASURED 2026-09-22 while landing the E42 shared symbol resolver. scripts/ops/check_allow_degraded.py requires every `# allow-degraded:` annotation to carry (a) a backlog id that RESOLVES to a filed row and (b) an unexpired `until:`. Its id universe comes from check_backlog_refs.filed_ids(), which globs `docs/claude/*backlog*.json` -- and the 2026-09-21 operating reset ARCHIVED all four review backlogs, so that glob now returns an EMPTY LIST (verified by running it). [!] THE CONSEQUENCE: no NEW `# allow-degraded:` annotation can be written anywhere in the repo, because there is no register left to file its id in. The live intake is docs/claude/work/PIPELINE.jsonl and its ids are `PI-`-prefixed, which check_backlog_refs.REF (`\\b(?:BL|MB|FU)-\\d{8}-...`) does not match at all. [!] HOW IT PRESENTED, which is the part worth keeping: it does NOT present as a broken guard. It presents as four ordinary findings against your own new code, with a remedy line -- 'file the row' -- that cannot be performed. A session that does not stop to check the register will either invent a plausible-looking BL- id (which then resolves to nothing and is a dangling reference forever) or weaken the guard. Both are worse than the annotation. [!] WHAT THIS LANE DID INSTEAD, so the next session has the precedent: avoided the marker. The four fail-safe fallbacks in src/runtime/exchange_accounts.py and src/units/accounts/clients.py are now commented `# noqa: BLE001  -- FAIL-SAFE: <reason>` with the reasoning in prose. That is honest and passes, but it is a WORKAROUND -- it means genuinely degraded paths stop being enumerable by the guard that exists to enumerate them, which is the silence this guard was built to prevent. So the guard is not merely inconvenient; every session that meets it quietly shrinks its population. [!] NOT FIXED HERE because it is nobody's Tier-1 five-minute change: pointing REF at PIPELINE.jsonl means deciding whether a pipeline row is the right owner for a degradation exception (it has due_when and origin.rerun, which is arguably a BETTER fit than a backlog row ever was), and that is a call about the operating model rather than a regex edit.", "routed_to": "unrouted -- needs a checklist row; same family as PI-20260921-0002 (archived registers still read by live CI paths)"}
